      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>LADIES' LETTEM</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTEM</p>
        <p>Vie. DEAR NELLIE.</p>
        <p>The newest and. most fashionable shape in fur jackets is the Russian blouse, which has returned to favour in a very short time. The basque is longer and fuller, reaching to the knees, and is put on under a handsome clasped waistband. The loose pouched fronts of the bodice part button over slightly to the left side, and are turned back at the top with pointed or fluted revers. These coats are being made up in caracul, sable, sealskin, and chinchilla, and though undoubtedly handsome one trembles at the thought of them on stout figures, for they are also bulky. Some of them have novel sleeves, a moderately loose shape to just below the elbow, and there finished with a deep frill of full gathered fur, over an underfrill of lace. These coats are worn by the ladies who adopt them as house blouses and tea-jackets, in rich materials, clasped round the waist with the well-known belts, bands of gold and silver thread interwoven with black, and clasped all round with bosses and bars of silver, which are greatly valued by those lucky enough to possess them. These same coats are being also made in lace for tea-jackets, and look very well in the soft, clinging fabric.</p>
        <p>Many ladies are ordering complete sets of furs— hat, ruffle or pelerine, and muff to match, each and all bearing corresponding touches of old lace. Such a mode tends to unity in dress, which is essential to a good appearance. Time was when a fur coat, or, indeed, any fur, was regarded as a lifelong and 'inalterable possession ; it was not lightly come by. Fashions change in furs as quickly and as often as in other things, and valuable furs are chopped and changed about in a manner which is really dis- tressing in view of how such a process lessons their value. There is a new departure in muffs—the carriage muff—a quaint idea, the muff being of an enormous size, only fit for carriage wear, fringed with sable tails, which, when resting on the lap, farm quite a protecting rug in itself.</p>
        <p>Long basques and square coat-tails are not always becoming to all figures, but a compromise is sometimes effected by means of small tabs at the back springing from a shaped waistband, which is far from being unbecoming, especially to matronly figures. Some of the newest belts have these tabs attached to them, so that they can be worn inde- pendently of any one particuiar bodice, and con- vert a blouse into a dignified-looking corsage. Narrow tartan bands and atrappings are a feature on cloth and zibeline frocks, and are also seen to advantage on soft, plain-coloured silk blouses. Several of the new evening gowns show great novelty in the arrangement of the skirt, this being formed of a series of poufs up to the waist, each putting divided by a galon or lace insertion, the bottom of the skirt being finished off with a deep frilling of lace or chiffon. The effect is quaint and becoming to slight figures, for the fulness at the waist in front is kept down by a very long pointed stomacher corsage, giving a straight flat front from the figure downwards. Jewelled lace, which is a ranch more refined garniture than sequined lace, is being much used for evening gowns, and long float- ing scarves of chiffon or lace are also well in evi- dence. An evening cloak should be made to fit very loosely, and vary many are trimmed with rows of braid roiled the collar.</p>
        <p>An evening bodice which can be commended is in soft cripe de chine, with bolero, berthe, and sleeves of guipure, a bow and deep collar and atrappings of black panne, each strap tastened with a tiny gold button. The collar is bordered with cream cloth guipure, whilst a deep band of the folded panne, fastened in front with antique buckle, passes round the waist. A charming short bolero, has spade fronts of black ctracul, bordered with a narrow edgily.; of sable tail, to match the collar and revers. The bolero is cut away at the back and sides to show a deep belt of stitched black satin. Another little coat is in black face cloth, with rounded fronts, cut up sharply at the aides to form a vandyke, then rounding off into a basque behind. It is very fashionable to wear a Fur pelerine that is mingled largely with lace, or to sport a little coat with big sleeves of cloth and fur in company. In one case a pale coffee-coloured cloth coat was cut in strips, and mounted upon black caracul, so that the fur ohowed soft and luxurious as a lining between the strips, which were attached to the fur only at the edge of the garment. Fan. tastic notions like this are seen on every side, and give a pleasant amount of license to the woman of ideas, enabling her to utilise scraps of material and lace and to have her furs re-modelled into something distinctive that shall do her ingenuity credit. An entirely new and completely fur cape or mantle is a most costly investment, whereas an old-world tippet liberally bunched with lace in the front and given long ends of the same, need not require a great outlay, and may be the union of two materials already in possession.</p>
        <p>Your loving .Tirortic.</p>
