      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>MOST NUTRITIOUZ.</head>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUZ.</p>
        <p>E P PS'S</p>
        <p>GFIATEFUL-COMFORTING.</p>
        <p>COCOA</p>
        <p>ZREAKFAST-SUPPEFt</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S OHM.</p>
        <p>The new Lady Mayoress—Lady Dimsdale—will have her little daughter as train bearer at the reception at the Mansion House. Lady Dimsdala will wear a gown of grey and white satin, richly veiled in lace, and the eight maids of honour are to appear in dresses of white mousseline. Sir Joseph Dimsdale, the Lord Mayor elect comes from a very old Essex family, and is a member of the well-known banking firm which his father built up- Most of London's Lord Mayors have come from the draper's shops, or have been mercers, grocers, iron- mongers, fishmongers, or other tradesmen. Bankers have been few,but there lhave been:many goldsmiths who, in olden times, often acted as bankers. The wedding of Lady Clementine Hay and Mr Waring will, according to present arrangements, be celebrated in London on Novernber 4th. This winsome little Scotswoman is an immense favourite with her large circle of friends and acqtraintances, and the very hearty good wishes of all will tollow her Intl the new life she ia entering upon so soon. When still quite a tiny child Lady Clementine was most carefully taught to think of others less fortunate and well placed than herself, and many have good cause to be grateful for this early training. Since life has become a daily struggle to dodge the deadly bacilli, which the medical:joutnals tell us exist everywhere, and in everything, it has been truly said that the only safe place is to get into a carbolic acid bath, and there await a happy release from the dangers that surround us. We can believe in the dangers of the trailing skirt, sweeping up the bacilli in the streets, or in the deadly germs of the infected oyster,but when we are told that tea ia simply stewed bacteria or that the waving of a handkerchief may condemn scores to a lingering death by consumption, we begin to give it up and marvel how it is so many of us survive such mani- fold perils. One is reminded of this by the controversy over the healthfulness of Harris tweeds, to which Lord Rosebery so amusingly referred in his speech at the opening ef the Sale of Scottish Home Industries, in Glasgow the other day. These tweeds, or those ot them that come from the islands of Harris in the Hebrides, were denounced as swarming with bacilli, and, as this seemed likely to in- jure an important Highland cottage industry, the accu- sation has been refuted with considerable heat by those who regard it as " another senseless scare." Amongst these, Lord Bosebery must clearly be included, as he announced that be and his family would wear Harris tweeds as long as they could afford them, and the same view must be attributed to the Duchess of Sutherland who has done so much to assist the promotion of cottage industries in Scotland. When it became impossible to reduce the dimensions of our sleeves any turther,the full cuff crept in. Later, this fullness extended to the elbow, now the over-sleeve shows signs of becoming all too generous in its propor- tions for comfort. Possibly by the time summer is with us, the balloon variety will reign again, but even so, it will prove more becoming to the average woman than the skin tight specimen has done. For dinner gowns, sleeves either terminate at the elbow, in cascades of chiffon or lace, or at the wrist, when they are transparent of course. For very dressy occasions, sleeves continue to almost reach vanishing point.. Ivory is in great request among those fottunates who are able to indulge in, what proves to be at this season of the year, very costly wear, namely, white gowns and coats. Tall old combs of ivory have ap- peared upon many a fashionable head of 'late, and beautifully carved fan sticks and umbrella handles are also shown in ivory inlaid and overlaid with silver. The ' craze for white ornaments is also ( xtecding to our rooms,tiny figures in exquisitively carved ivory appear- ing upon brackets, curio, and writing tables. For the latter, ivory seals, paper knives, and desk furnishings I are very chic, and such items are finding much favors I among demise of wedding elite. In headgear there are two styles that never materially change, one is the fur tcque, which we have with us once again, and the otter the black picture hat. Regarding the former, chinchilla is the fur for the brunette, and sable for the blonde. With white coats and skirts ether the for toque, or the black picture hat may be worn with success, but the laiter, of fine critic-- line. lined with wnite, and adorned with long sweeping uncurled plumes, is perhaps the most becoming finish possible to a well-dressed head. Veils, which never appear with the large hat, promise to be less and less worn as time goes on. Our complexions in these athletic days have grown accustomed to any sort of weather, and moreover it appears to have dawned upon us that looking through a maze, and dodging innumer- able chenille spots, is nct the most healthful procedure, to which to subject our eyes. If popular education has not made us all fine lords and ladies, it has spread many false notices, one result beirg undoubtedly to greatly increase the servant diffi- culty. A striking example of this was afforded the other day in a mother's protest against her daughter being taught cooking, at a Lannon Board School. The girl, ebe said, was going to be a tidy, and not a servant and therefore she did not want her taught such undig- nified details as cooking. It is not often that one hears of such magi ificent snobbery as this, but the worst Df it i 3, that it is only symptomatic of a state of things which is fast making housekeeping an almost intoler- able burden. Amongst all classes there is the same difficulty about servants, though cf course this is not so great in those houses where a number is kept, as in those where only single-handed help is possible. It is the "General Ser- vant" that is so difficult to obtain, and those who are engaged in a constant strugzle with this problem, w appreciate the gentle irony in the following advertise- ment : General Servant :—No enquiries, no work, no caps or aprons; every evening out, • isitors permitted, piano, good wages.—Address Sze. This appeared in a West County paper, the advertiser evidently worn out, with ' repeated failures, finally coming to the conclusion that the lady help must be the mistress, while she herself became the servant. Whether a wife is entitled to save anything fee hersef out;of the weekly allowance for house keeping, is a que!- lion which must concern not a few people in this coun- try. A well-known authoress, recently urged the view, that a woman who does her duty in the home keeps herself, and is really entitled to wages. The common remark that the husband keeps her is, she says a fallacy though it is an old belief, and it would appear to have 'he support of the highest Court of Appal in Austria. In a recent case there, it was decided that a wife who had saved £125 out of the house keeping money, was bound to relund it, and it was moreover declared that such a proceeding amounts to theft. It is not very clear how the law would interpret such a matter in this , Country, but one can imagine the astonishment of many a thrifty housewife if told that her saving habits were , little better than thieving. MADGE.</p>
        <p>PROOF AT YOUR DOOR.—ThCrgi conchlSivo evidence in every part of Lancashire that Dr. Williams' pink pills let pale people are recommended by all classes for the way in which they have cured ancemia,'general weak- ness, loss of appetite, palpitation, shortness of breath, early decay, all forms of female weakness, hysteria, paralysis, locomotor ataxy, rheumatism, sciatica, scrofula, rickets, chronic erysipelas, consumption of the towels and lungs. These pills are not a purgative, and contain nothing that can injure the most &amp;Beate. They are genuine only with the full mum', Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people. and are sold by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, E.G.. at 2s 9d a box, or six boxes for 133 9d post free. They invigorate the ystem after °vet-work, worry, and indiscretion of living. In another column of this issue the emphatic statement of a Bury lady is printed at length.</p>
      </div>
