      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>QUEEN'S SOLDIER.</head>
        <p>A QUEEN'S SOLDIER.</p>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>BY WALTER WOOD, Author of "Barrack and Battlefield," "Famous British Warships," "Through Battle to Pro- motion," "A Captain in the Fusiliers," "A Soldier of the Legion," &amp;o.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: Mark Berm, the son of a substantial farmer in Yorkshire volunteers for service 'in the Yeomanry, about to be despatched to South Africa. After the farewells due to his father and his sweetheart, Lizzie Jackson, he duly embarks, the troop also including his friend Brigg. To' his surprise he finds Lizzre Jackson at Capetown as an army nurse, she having left England on a mail steamer, which had passed the slow-going transport. Benn and Brigg are in a skirmish with three Boers. Poor Brigg is shot through the head, but not before he has wounded one of the Boers, and Benn distinguishes himself by disarming and taking the three prisoners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>CHAPTER VII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VII.</p>
        <p>RETRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>For a moment the Yeoman thought that he was once more a prisoner, and that the figures which covered him with rifles were those of enemies. But he looked again, and knew that his own people were near him. The soldier who had given the order to halt was his own Captain, and for the rest they were his comrades in arms. Bonn and Brigg had been missed, and as it was believed that for some special purpose they had gone out upon the veld, part of their company had made their way to the summit of the kopie in the hope of seeing them.</p>
        <p>"It's Yorky," Benn heard a trooper say, and, looking up, he noticed that the speaker was the man who had envied him the meeting with Lizzie on the wharf. "He's bagged a couple of the beggars, too," said the trooper. "By George! All the luck's going everybody's way but mine. I've had no sport yet. Yes, sir." The trooper, to whom the Captain had spoken, began to hurry towards Benn, whom he shortly joined. "What's the trouble, Yorky ?" he asked. "The Captain's sent me down to give you a lift. Is there anything I can do besides keeping an eye on yout catch?"</p>
        <p>"No," said Benn. "That's all—just now." "Where's Brig"?" demanded the trooper, quickly, noticing that Benn's comrade was not with him.</p>
        <p>"Just behind there," answered Bean; with a backward nod of his head. _ _</p>
        <p>"Dead?" asked the trooper, in a lower voice. It was the first time one of the Yeomen had fallen, and he was deeply thrilled. They were not yet familiar with the worst that war could do. There were those amongst the Yeomen who had not yet seen death in any form, even at home. "Yes," said Benn, in answer to the question. "And how did it happen?" asked the trooper, eagerly, as they slowly climbed the kopje, on the brow of which the Captain and his people remained standing. "I'd better tell that to the Captain," answered Benn ; "but it vrasn' t fair playing of the game— not as I understand it., anyway." He said no more, nor did the trooper question. him, until he had made his report to his Captain. There were men who could not help hearing what Benn had to say to his superior officer, and there .were those who edged closer so that they could heat the story. Mark at best was a man of few words; he said little now, but every sentence burnt itself into the listeners' minds.</p>
        <p>When they knew what had really happened, under- stood to the fall the act of the wounded prisoner, there was a low murmur of rage and horror. Instinctively two or three of the men raised their rifles, and made a move towards the prisoners. , "No, no," said the Captain, comprehending the signs. "Get back there. It isn't our way of doing business. Keep an eye on him, Benn and you, Wade; he'll be seen to by the General." Meanwhile a body of infantry, a fully-equipped battalion, was marching over the veld towards the spot where the Imperials were halted. The Captain looked from them to the fallen trooper. and waited. He was new to the war and its ways, and wished for guidance from the officer com- manding the advancing battalion. That body had been long in the field, It was a bearded, ragged, sunburnt band, the remnant of a regiment half of whose numbers had already been killed in action, or had died of wounds or disease. It had had its campaign training, and had settled down into its place as a businesslike unit of a businesslike army. On a tiny scale it was complete in itself, down to and including a waggon-load of blanket i with eyeleted edges for easy fastening. To the officer commanding the Captain presented himself, and explained matters. There was no difficulty whatever in doing what the Captain wanted.</p>
        <p>A small party from the infantry dug a hole near th 3 spot where i3rigg was lying; one of the eyeleted blankets was fetched, and, stitched in it, with his hat as a pillow, just as he had fallen, the trooper was buried.</p>
        <p>As he Wili lowered into his shallow grave, around which the Yeomen, rigid, sad, with hats uplifted, stood, there were a few halting words in deep, broken tones. No one looked, but the Captain's voice was recognised. It was a short and simple prayer—the only form of service that was held. The grave was filled in, stones piled up, and a rude cross—made of two rough, unpainted boards, presented by the battalion—stuck at the head. A soldier, who was something of a carver, cut with a pocket-knife the name and rank and corps of Brigg upon the crosspiece, and it stood there as inscription. The infantry, scarcely troubled by the episode, resumed their march; the Yeomen also, silently, turned away and, with their prisoners. went back to camp. Mark was deeply moved by what had happened; the company, too, was stirred to its depths. What he, who had seen this thing done, vowed openly, they promised to themselves. "It's a bad beginning of the game," said Mark to Wade; "but it's just as well to know how yon've got to play it. If that's the way it's done, we'll see who plays it best. You'll never catch me napping again so long as we're in South Africa. If I see fit to do it, I shall shoot first, and ask questions after." But there was no time for either Mark or his companions to dwell for long on what had happened. The Captain himself sat down at once and wrote a brief, kind letter to the trooper's father, telling him of his death. It is enough," he said, "as showing what he was, to have told you what his last words were to his comrade, Beim. All I ask is, that whichever of us may have to follow, we may do so with as stout a heart. If there can be cause for pride in such a thing, I am proud, indeed, that such: an example- should have been set by one of my own men; and that if the way had to be led at all, it should have been led in such a fashion by a_trooper under my command." The Captain sighed as he sealed the envelope; but by the time the letter was on its journey, the incident of the veld had faded from his recollec- tion. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Lon.' before Brigg, the brewer, had received the letter, he knew the worst. in the war news which jig daily paper gave him, he read, under the leading, 'Fresh Casualty List," with the name of ~11.3 place and the date of the occurrence: "Killed, 'Atli Company, Imperial yeomanry, 7,705 Private !irigg." There were stern, incessant, calls upon: the yeomanry; sharp, hard work throughout the day for man and horse ; with the pinch of hunger and the pain of thirst to btar with. Often there was promise of exciting-duty ;which ended only in weariness and disappointment. Thcr,-_. were such happenings as the sending of a score of the Yeomanry to a point sixty miles away, tuld for them to find, on reaching it, a telegram oi dering their instant return to camp, so that hard riding and suffering had been for nothing. But if such things were harassing, they were also hardening and soldier-making. Every hour of toil and suffering made the "Yeomen more tenaiou* as soldiers, and more enduring as fighters. They were used in every way as if they had been seasoned Regulars. To Mark, stung as he hid been by the conscious and unconscious sneers at "noodles,' there was a deep satisfaction in these calls upon his fortitude, this passing through privations which were just the same as those that all the finest Ilegalars were called upon to bear. This was no make-believe, no sham, no show; no display to win the admiration and applause of onlookers. There were no on- lookers to watch them, no one to single out the few for praise when praise was equally the right of 1;0614.</p>
        <p>It was a rough, hard, risky life, with deatn in hiding everywhere; but it seized upon and fascinated Mark; and though there were times when the peaceful picture of the Hill Top Farm, as he had seen it on that memorable evening when he walked home, scythe on shoulder, from the hay- field, returned to him with melting force, he would not have excharwed it for the best of all that he had so far lived through. • - •</p>
        <p>It was impossible for him, of course, to forget that Lizzie was in the same country, and not to feel pleasure in the knowledge; but he was at times amazed to learn how easily, with so much stirring work to do, he could forget her temporarily. At home she had been scarcely ever absent from his thoughts, but here, sometimes for !ours together, she was not remembered. But ;dem such a lapse Mark dwelt upon his love with all the greater fondness, and there were times when lie would gladly have given up all the excitement which the war afforded for the joy—the very sober joy, in his case—of seeing her again. Ho was an undemonstrative person, but his love was not the less sincere and firm on that account. He had not</p>
        <p>yet heard of or from Lizzie ; but he had no fear that she was not in first-rate health and spirits. As he was constantly on the move, it was more than likely that her letters had miscarried. For his own part, he had both written and gent verbal messages to her last address.</p>
        <p>The camp was threatening monotony, and there seemed but little chance of leaving it to march to the relief of any town, when news was brought that a strong body of the enemy was in the neighbour- hood. Generally it was understood that the work would be sharp and severe, and that the Yeomen would have the chance of proving just how much their value was in action.</p>
        <p>The Major told them so before they started; and, having done this, and made known that he left the rest to them, they walked forward in extended</p>
        <p>order. As they advanced they heard the booming of artillery. Almost instantly, it seemed to Mark, there was a rushing sound in the air, and a shell struck the ground not more than two hundred yards from the Imperials' left flank. There was something like restlessness in the ranks; but the men went on, following their leader, who gripped his reins and kept his saddle well, and felt that his men were as well in hand as his horse. There was another rushing sound, followed by a crash and the scattering of earth and stones and bullets; and then the Major's loud, clear voice snapped "Trot !" The company wheeled half-left to the flank of the enemy, and so to a kopje, where the horses were left, and on which the Yeomen rushed to the top to fire upon the enemy, by this time stationed on a ridge opposite. From the moment that the rifles rang out Mark forgot all else in the excitement of the combat. From time to time at home he had joined in fox- hunting, more than once on Prince, and had been in at the death with the very man who was now at the head of them on the field of battle. When the ridge had been cleared Mark raised a hoarse cheer, and the rest of the company took it up. They sprang to their feet when the order "Mount' was heard, and cheered again. Almost instantly, it seemed to Bean, there came the Orders "Trot" and "Gallop," then "Charge! " By this time it seemed quite to accord with the fitness of things that the Major should be leading them to the assault with hunting cries. "Yoicks "Tally-ho!" "Forrard on!" 'These, the trooper heard amid the sound of bullets and the thud of hoofs. Reckless of danger in every form, forgetful of everything except the enemy in front, and caring for nothing but the chase and destruction of men, Mark abandoned himself to the task before th.m. But he still saw well enough to know that thick as the bullets came the Yeomen were all mounted; no saddles were as yet empty, no horses were down. With a rumble of hoofs, and in a cloud of dust, the company was halted, and men and horses sought shelter in a small stone squire from which they fired briskly upon the enemy, or rather, the spot where he was supposed to be concealed. It was now seen that the bullets came from a house standing in the open ; and towards this spot, after lying down for some minutes, most of them, the Major leading still, advanced on foot. But not for long. There was no hope of living in such a zone of fire, and officers and men fell flat upon. the ground to escape the bullets. As they lay like that, there was heard the thud of a gun, the scream of a projectile overhead, and the crash of an explosion. The shell had come from a British field piece which had been swept up, and the first shot from which had wrecked the house. Half-a-dozen more shells followed, and under cover of the fire the Imperials rose and charged. They rushed thiS 'shattered building, and with a wild cheer were amongst the ruins with the bayonets. But by this time the survivors had fled to another ridge two hundred yards away, and here, from amongst the rocks, they sent a pelt of bullets to the reeking mass. Behind them they had left dead men and ponies and wounded men; while saddles, bridles, rifles and ammunition littered the ground. For the moment, rushing to and fro amongst the wreckage of the dwelling, Mark did not notice that some of the wounded were crawling to one spot, and seeking shelter and protection under a white handkerchief', waving from the muzzle of a rifle. He and Wade, and others of the company, were turning stones and woodwork over with their weapons, and hurrying to a point or corner here and there to see if any active foe remained. Soon the entire company was busily engaged like this, working in little clusters, to make certain before they stormed the ridge that there would not be left behind a force to bring a cross rifle fire to</p>
        <p>bear upon them. In the confusion and excitement the wounded were lost sight of, and no regard was taken of their signal to surrender. But, pausing in his work, Wade, the trooper, turned and saw that one of the enemy had raised himself, and almost from the very shadow of the flag was taking aim with his rifle at Benn, who was within a yard of a wail behind which Wade was probing with his bayonet. In an instant Wade had dashed out, seized Bonn, and without a word had torn him into a _ _ .</p>
        <p>place of safety. , As Benn fell to the ground the bullet, came and, almost grazing the top of the wall, passed through his hat and flattened itself against a piece of neighbouring stonework. "I saw the chap who did it," said Wade. "You can see him if you squint through this crevice. There, that fellow with the bare bead. Look out, he's going, to have another pot at us. Jump aside, I'll chip in first." "No," said Mark, coolly. "This is my shout." He had picked up current slang rapidly, and spoke as quietly as if he had been rabbit-shooting on the farm at home. Ho thrust the muzzle of his rifle through the crevice of which Wade had spoken, and the trooper near him watched with steady eyes, gazing through the crevice to see what the effect of the shot would' be. There was now none of that feeling left which had possessed Mark when be lay on the summit of /the kopje with Brigg, with rifles presented and covering the unconscious horsemen on the veld. He was true to what he had said on returning to camp after the trooper's burial, that he would shoot. first and question afterwards. He fired now, and the waver of flip flag rolled over. Again the signal of surrender was hoisted and fluttered desperately. But for some minutes the Yeomen did not leave their shelter. When at last they did advance, satisfied that there was no further danger, Mark went up to his assailant. Something in his appear- ance struck him forcibly, and he bent down and lifted up the head. One glance at the face explained things. Here was the very man by whose hands Brigg had died. He had been sent back to his own people because of his wounds, instead of being s detained as a prisoner. Ire had taken the field again, on making the recovery which his captors had not expected him to make, and, true to his instincts, had been repeating the exploit of the veld when Berm had seen him and destroyed him. Mark put the head down again as Wade joined him. "See how queerly things come round," he said, simply. "This is the chap that killed Brigg. I feel more satisfied now." "It's better so," said Wade. "Come on, you chaps, or we shall be too late for the rest of the business." Without another thought for the group of wounded the Yeomen rushed across the open space and joined in the assault on the kopje.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>CHAPTER VIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VIII.</p>
        <p>A PRISONER OF WAR.</p>
        <p>in the ordinary course of things few of the assailants could have hoped to reach the summit. The survivors of the enemy, small though their number was, could have destroyed the Yeomen, as they dashed across the open ground and up the hillside ; while such was their own shelter that Gni were out of hartu'a way from the front.</p>
        <p>But it had happened that while the Yeomen were busy prodding about the burning building a couple of field guns and a Maxim had swept round to the enemy's rear, and the Boers, finding their retreat to all appearances eut off at the moment the storming party came into view, abandoned the fight and sought their own safety. The kopje abounded in rocks and crevices, behind and amongst which the enemy gained almost perfect shelter from the fire which was opened on them from the rear, and which was stopped as soon as it was known that the Yeomen were advancing up the face of the hill, and would themselves within a very few minutes be in danger from it, Where the Boers fled to and how they vanished was not clear either to those behind or those in front of the lull. All that was certain wee that they had gone, for when the Yeomen reached the ifint and saw a body of infantry advancing from ale direction of the field guns and the Maxim there was no sign of any living defender. It could only be assumed that the Boers had hurried down some gullies which ran on the left and right of the position, and in that way had gained the veld and made good their escape. In all their move- ments since the fight began they had been both smart and crafty, but in no case had they shewn more mobility and celerity than when it seemed as if they were inevitably trapped between the two sections of the British force. This suspicion was confirmed when, on climbing to the very brow of the hill and looking down upon the level, a party of horsemen were seen galloping away from the kopje. To all appearances their ponies had been waiting them, and the riders had sprung into the saddles and escaped. To the Major, who had reached the top with the first of his men, the disappointment of seeing the flying enemy was very bitter. For a few seconds he gazed almost desparingly at the hurrying riders, then ordered those who were nearest to him to see what they could do with the rifle. They made an effort, but the distance was deceptive, and the few bullets that were fired whizzed harmlessly ahead of the fugitives. Besides, it was discovered that few of the Yeomen had any ammunition left. From first to last the fighting had lasted six hours, and in that time most of the men had fired very cartridge. Undaunted, the Major tried his Jlick with the gunners. His signals for one to be "orought up, if possible, were understood, and a 15-pounder was hauled up the sloping sides of the kopje and trained on the-Doers. Here again the shots were useless; the worst mischief done was the scattering at the heels of the ponies of a cloud of earth and stones. But no animal was down, and no min had left his saddle. "They'll get away as clean as a whistle," grumbled the Major, bitterly. "If we were only on the level with 'em, mounted! Their cattle are dead beat—l can see that from here. I'm positive, too, they can't have much ammunition left, after the way.they've been squandering it." "Suppose, sin I took a few men with me and tried to cut 'em off from there," suggested a tall subaltern at the senior's elbow. The junior made a comprehensive sweep with his right arm. The Major by no means understood the details, but generally he felt that the idea was right and good. He therefore said: "Do—and go." The subaltern jerked his hand by way of salute, and crying: "Come on, men," leaped down the kopje side and ran over the ground to the spot where the horses were held. Among those who followed were Bean and Wade, to whose company the subaltern belonged. The young man vaulted into his saddle. His lips and mouth were dry and cracked with thirst, and his face was dark with dust, but these were details that be did , not think of; and his men followed his example. They swept round the foot of the kopje and into the open, just in time to catch sight of the retreating Boers. Not till this moment did Bonn notice that he was riding a strange horse, and that Prince had been left 1 behind. In the excitement, he had sprung into the first convenient vacant saddle. With one shout of encouragement, the subaltern got to the front, closed his parched mouth, and led the way. He glanced—merely turning his head to do so— at the brow of the hill, where the Major and the rest of his own people stood • and noticed that his commanding officer was making frantic signs. He also heard the Major's voice, but it was impos- sible to understand his words; and there was no time to stop to make inquiries. It was so palpable to the subaltern that what the Major meant by act and speech was that the pursuers should rush ahead and run the fugitives to earth, that he did not give the matter a second thought. As for the rest of the Yeomen, they neither saw nor heard the commanding officer. Their interest was centred solely in the band of men and ponies on the veld. The Maxim rattled for a few seconds, and the field gun rapped out savagely. The subaltern again marvelled, somewhat feebly, believing, as he did, that such an act was sheer and wilful waste of ammunition. But still, the matter was none of his business, and he tore blindly on. That they were gaining on the enemy was clear. From time to time he saw this with his bloodshot, 1 dust-smartened eyes, and opened his cracked lips 14iro encourage his 'men by telling them that it was</p>
        <p>so. Soon they were out of effective rifle shot; before long they were almost past the reach of the field guns, so swiftly did they course along the weld; and during all the trying, race the forms of the potties and the men upon them grew larger against the sky-line. In point of overhauling it was as if a' destroyer was hurrying in the way of an ocean tramp: To the subaltern and his men it seemed as if the Boers were stationary, so slow their locomotion WAS. If there had been any thought of further firing from the kopje now, the Major must have been compelled to put it from him, for there was just as niuch likelihood of destroying the pursuers as damaging the pursued. This much seemed clear to the subaltern, and he was puzzled slightly con- cerning the ease of his capture when unexpectedly the Boers halted, and deliberately turned about to</p>
        <p>meet him. Instinctively the subaltern reigned in; instinc- tively, too, he was about to give the order to fire a volley before charging and completing his task. Then' something which was not fear almost stopped his heart-beats, and something that was •,ot thirst parched his throat so that he Could epeak no word and make no sound. He understood now why the Major signalled frantically with his arms and shouted, and why the Maxim and the field gun had, as it seemed, sense- lessly wasted ammunition. All these things were signals of recall, for in the excitement of the chase the subaltern and the troopers had ridden off without so much as a rifle or a piece of steel amongst them. Revolvers—very few of them—there were hut before they could have been made use of every man and horse could have been shot. .</p>
        <p>At last the subaltern spoke. "My God!" he exclaimed. "Men—l can't help it; we've got to turn tail, and fly back. Go on; I'll keep to the rear. Gallop! March!" There was no help for it. The horses swept round, heads to the distant kopje, and the spurs were duo into the weary flanks. Instan'tly,the Mausers crackled in•the rear, and the thin, venomous bullets showered about the troopers. The subaltern and his charger went down together; Wade rolled to the ground, and his terrified horse sprang forward furiously. Benn felt a strange sting in his right thigh and his left arm, and comprehended that he was hit in two places. his mount sprang into the air, and made as if he would follow the horse which had decamped, and which *as now tearing riderless ahead of all the company; but Bonn's strong hand and deep voice restrained him, and trembling, but obedient, the mount submitted. Another volley came; more horses stumbled, more men. went down. Even to the most courageous of them it was so palpable that to try and get away was but to court annihilation that the sergeant, who was unwounded, whipped a white handkerchief from his sleeve, stood up in his stirrups, and, as he waved his token of surrender, cried upon thug troopers to halt, Those who could control their horses stopped; thosep who could not, continued blindly and made the best of flight. In this way half a doien regained the foot of the kopje. "We can't help it, lads ; it's the luck of war," shouted the sergeant, as he held the handkerchief up. "Let 'ern come near enough—an' pistol 'em," growled Mark, to whom the very thought of surrender was deplorable. "That would be as low down a trick as theirs," replied the sergeant. "It's too dirty to be played. No, this is the only way." "All right," said Mark, resignedly. "But it's awful to be bagged like this—at the very beginning, LOD."</p>
        <p>"It's better than that, isn't it ?" said the. sergeant, indicating the fallen. "Here they come —we can't help it, I tell you, and I've seen more - of this sort of thing than you have." He still held up the handkerchief, and did not lower it until he had formally surrendered himself and his companions to the enemy. Instantly they were ordered to march, and so, captors and captives, they continued the journey across the veld, the major and the rest still watching. "You want to got to Pretoria, don't you ? asked the Boer leader, genially. " Well, this is the right way to do it. The distance is pretty long, but you'll soon cover it." In this manner Trooper Beim became a prisoner ,of war.</p>
        <p>Almost at the same moment that he was carried off, a wounded captive, Lizzie, now a nursing sister, was arriving at the Yeomanry base hospital, the staff of which she had joined for reasons which will be explained. Less than a dozen miles sevarateil the love 7.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>CRIPPLED BY A SHOCK.</head>
        <p>CRIPPLED BY A SHOCK.</p>
        <p>Bury Woman's Peculiar Mishap</p>
        <p>• Cases of shock, resulting from distressing sights and bad news, are not uncommon, but they rarely have such prolonged and severe after effects as in the case of a woman mow living at Bury, who has been a bedfast helpless cripple in consequence of shock,for eight years.. Many doctors have been interested and puzzled over this particular woman, and as more talk has been created by furtber developments in the case, a reporter of the " Bury Guardian " investigated it last week.. The womar in question is Mrs Sarah Ann Isherwood. wife of Mr Edward Isherwood, engineer and firebeater, and the story which she told was of a most interesting .and astonishing character. When the reporter called on her at 27, Ash-street, he found Mrs Isherwood attend- ing to her household duties in a manner which made it difficult to believe that she had undergone so many years of suffering as the story which she subsequently told diaclosed. "About thirteen years ago," said Mrs. Isherwood, "I was living at Milnrow, near Rochdale, and my hus- band was working at Newhey. I bad had three attacks of rheumatic fever—two Wore and once after I was ntarried. Whilst living at Milnrow, my mother and one of my sisters died rather suddenly within a short time of each other, and the shock caused by their deaths acting upon an already weakened system, made me quite ill. My heart became affected, and I was par- tially paralysed down my left side. So bad was I that I had to take to my bed, and I was practically bedfast for ever eight years. Of course, I had to leave the bed once a day to have it shaken up, but I had to be lifted on and off, and so weak and thin did I get that neigh- bours who vbited me used to speak of it. After I had been in bed at Milnrow for nine months.my husband de- cided that it would be better if he lived nearer his work at Newhey, and there I was conveyed in a cab, being carried in and out of the house like a child. Several doctors were called in to see me, and although I took considerable medicine and tried various remedies, none of them seemed to do me any good. At length I deter- mined to take no more physic,but to spend what money I could afford on nourishments only." "To what, then, do you attribute your present apparently [rood health ?" asked the reperter. "To Dr Williams' pink pills," was the ready reply, "and I shall advise everybody who suffered:as I did to give them a trial."</p>
        <p>is Why did you decide to try them ?" was the next question. " Well, it came about in this way," replied Mrs Isherwood. " After we had been living at Newhey rather over seven years,my husband got work at Bam- ford, near Heywood, and then we removed ; I of course being taken in a cab. At Bamford, I only had one next door neighbour, and she was so struck and upset at my weakness, that she practically took charge ot m 0 coming in and out of the house to attend to me at all hours of the day. Soon after I came to Bamford, the neighbour told me that her danghter had been cured of an illness like mine by Dr Williams' pink pills and advised me to try some. At first I refused, but she wou'd hear of no refusal, and went to Heywood and bought a box ; I took the whole of the contents of the box according to the directions." - .</p>
        <p>s' What effect had the pills on you," asked the reporter.</p>
        <p>Well, the first box apparently did not agree with me. They gave me pains in my bead, and I was for giving up taking them, but neither my husband nor the neighbour would bear of ma doing so. They told me that they must be making some change in me, and at last I consented to go on taking them. A second box was obtained, and after taking about half the contents, I seemed to be getting better, and when I bad taken three boxes, I began to have hopes of getting shout again." •• Row many boxes did you take altogaher ?" waS the next question, "Seven," was the reply," and then I was able to get out of bed. One bar lasted me about a week, se that from commencing to take the pil s until I got out of bed about sevAn weeks had passed. Of course, I was stil: weak, and had to learn to walk again almost like s child ; but feeling had returned to my legs, and that was the main thing. I felt I was cured, as the pills, were adding to my strength daily, and the nourishment Itook seemed to do me good. In time I was strong enough to visit Newbey, and everybody there expressed the greatAst surprise at seeing me able to get about again. We came to Bury some twelve or eighteen months ago. lam now able to attend to my housc- hold duties, and am as well RA ever I was in my life. I cannot speak too highly of Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people, and as for my husband, he practically swears by them, and is newt tired of recommending them to other people. I always keep the pills by me. now, and whenever I feel a bit out of sorts, I take one or two, and theytput me right again. I am thankful, inder d that I took the advice of my Bamford neighbour ti tty Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people." Later on, the reporter saw Mr labs r wood, and be en- dorsed every word his wife hs.d said, and was loud, indeed, in his praise of the good which, so far as hie wife and he were sconcerned, had came from Dr Williams' pink pills,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>_ Professor Waddy Moss war announced to preach two sermons at this church on bunday. At the last moment, however, the rev gentleman was seized with illness, anti was unable Co frome to Warrington, a deputy being pro- vided from Diesbury College. Great disappointment was expressed at the nor -appearaLce of the Professor. who created a favourable impression on a former visit to Warrington. At the evening service the choir rendered the anthem " 0 Lord, my soul thirst,th.' the solo parts being rencered effectively by Miss Cain and Mr Warburton. Mr F Hickman, presided at the organ.</p>
        <p>Holy Trinity Churchl The annual sermons in Rid of the Additional Curate&amp; Society were preached at Holy Trinity Church on Sunday. The pulpit was occupied in the morning by the Vicar, Rev E C E Carleton, and in the evening by the Rev 11 E H Probyn, chaplain to The Bishop. There were good congregations.</p>
        <p>a</p>
      </div>
