      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS:</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS:</p>
        <p>"I seetex many a sleepless night over that new 'book et mine." "It didn't affect me that way." SHE : "This paper tells of a man who stepped -off a cliff and dropped 3001 t." He: "That fellow -must be a regular human centipede." HE (very much gone) : "There's nothing half so -.sweet in life as love's young dream." She: "I don't know. Mixed chocolates are pretty good." Mn. RICHFELLO : "Miss De Slimm is evidently woman of many fine points." Rival Belle: "I ,should flay so. Did you see her elbows ? " " ROBBIE " asked the visitor, "have you any brothers and sisters ? " "No," replied Robbie, I'm all the children we've got." MASTER: "What is an opossum?" Scholar: ." A small animal with a pouch." Master: "What ,does he do with it?" Scholar: "When he is thuntrecl he gets inside." MRS. GADABOUT: "Our new servant is a ewel. `She cooks beautifully, is very economical, never pOP9 out and never talks back." Her husband: "I wish I had met her before I was married." i'disxness : "You have no young man, I hope?" Maid: "On, no, ma'am; he's fifty if he's a day." A LEARNED physician says: "You should always breathe through the nose when asleep." So if you should chance to awaken and find your mouth open, get up and shut it. (to gentleman) : "Do you happen, sir, to have lost, your purse?" Gent (feeling in his pockets): "No 1" Beggar: "Then you can let ane have a little assistance." RUSSIAN soldiers are supplied with handker- chibts at the expense of the Government. This is, no doubt, a necessary expenditure in a country where every name is either a cough or a sneeze. "THEY say she's worth half a million." " Well, she wants it in liar business." " What's that?" 4' Looking for a husband." "Do you know, darling, that I think it makes me a better man eaery time I kiss you ? " "Well, at the rate you are going now, there will soon be no mere room for improvement." MOLLY: 'What did he mean by telling me I was as bight as the morning ? " Dolly: "I sup- Toe, dear, he meant you were as plain as day- light," SPOP.TS:MAN (to Snobson' who hasn't shot a single li rd a l day): "Do you know Lord Peckham?" Soobeon : "Oa, dear, yes; I've often shot at his houaa" Spn.tsman : " Ever hit it ? " "Tins is the parlour, eh?" tentatively re- tracked the estate agent, who was looking over .he house. "Yes," replied old man Kidder, but J usually call it a courtroom—l've got seven daughters, you know." Till; HOUSEWIFE: "Bridget, I smell gas leaking. Do you think it comes from the hall burner" Br;dget (after a sniff): " Lakin', is it? Bad luck to it ! It'll be all over the new carpet." Hastens for the mop. WlLLlS:"Parker's salary was doubled a short time ago, so I hear." Wallace : "Yes, it was; at it got him in lots of trouble." Willis: "How's that?" Wallace : "His wife found it out." Emmy : "I suppose your wife received you with open arms last night, even if you were rather late?" Datk "Well, not exactly; but she had her hands open—l remember that distinctly—with the fingers spread apart and bent." HIS FIGURE WERE WRONG.—Bilkins "It's a mistake Lbout people being made one when they are married. My wife and I were made ten. : "Made ten 1" Bilkins : " Yes; she's the cue and I'm the cipher." Mn. TALKATIVE: "The doctors say nervous people should never drink coffee." Mr. Thought- ful: "They should also abstain from living where they will have to wait an hour and twenty minutes if they miss a train." LAURA: "While Jack was calling the other evening he made the statement that he would kiss too or die in the attempt." Belle " !es? (After a pause.) Well, did he kiss you?" Laura: "You haven't read any account of Jack's death in the .papers, have you?" Bruns: "Teem goes a man who is approached with fear and trembling by all who have dealings with him." Boggs: "Ah I a criminal judge or something of that bort, I presume." Biggs: "No, a dentist." "THERE," said the landlady, as she handed the lodger a rather slender piece of real porterhouse, "you can't say anything about that steak being fie "to sole shoes with." "No," said the lodger, wrapping the meat round his fork, "1 ehould guess it was more fit for bootlaces." A CHRONIC INVALID.—Mrs. Nabor ; "And so the doctor ordered you to give your husband whisky for his rheumatism. Does it seem to do biin any good?" Mrs. Nexdoor "John says it .does him lots of good, but I notice the pains come upon him more frequently than ever." "How did Brown-Smith make all his money?" 4' He invented a new method of advertising a patent medicine." "And than sold the idea to a patent medicine firm?" "Not much! That's where he thawed his cleverness. He hired a men to invett a patent medicine for him, and used the idea him- -self." " You haven't any smokeless tobacco, have you?" .eisked the smart young man. "Lots of it," said the matter-of.fact person behind the counter, pro- ducing a box of snuff. How much do you want ? " Honstt OWNER: ." Suppose the machine of your motor-car goes wrong when you are half way be- tween two towns. What then ?" Motor Owner: 4' Wall, 1 wouldn't b 3 any worse off than if I were half way between two towns with a horse and trap and the horse should die." .MRS. JOHNSON: "Has Mr. Johnson come home for dinner yet?' Jane " No, mum." "I thought liteard him downstairs." "That was Leo growling . over a hone, mum." ANGRY FATHER (stick in hand, to little Willy): ." Woy are your clothes soiled so frightfully?" Little Willy (whimpering) : " I fell in the gutter." 4` And with your new knickers on?" "Yes, pa, I didn't have time to come home and change 'ein." "You young scoundrel !" said the father, seizing disebedient son by the hair ; "ra show yod Itow to treat your mother." And he at once pro- coeded to show Young Hopeful the way by bang- ing him across the ears two or three times, atd then shaking him until his hair began to fall out. "IF I should tell all I know," said the detective, with a solemn shake of the head, "it would create a sensation, and don't you forget it." "If you would refrain from telling what you don't know," oreturned the sarcastic man, "it would create even a greater sensation." His GOOD COSDUCT.—The Manager "How came, you to leave your last place?" Applicant : I was discharged for good behaviour, sir." anager : " D echarged for good behaviour? That's unusual, ien't it?" Applicant : " Well you see, good conduct took nine months off my eentence." GILBACII : "I am surprised, Colonel, at your time oh life, that you should have any trouble in managing your wife. All you have to do is to let her thtnk she is having her own way." Colonel aQ sailer "Yes, but the trouble is that everybody toe thinks so, tee." Tim typiue begged some workmen who were pu:ting up a new telephone not to place it so high sin the wall as they were doing. "You see," she said, "I 'naive to use it as much as anyone, and I am so short I can hardly reach it." "Oh, well, miss," said the humourist in charge of the work, 'you can raise your voice, can't you?" *SHE: "Why should they say stoleu kisses are the sweetest ?" : "I think it is due largely to the natural perversity of human nature. It is anot so much due to the fact of any sweetness in the mere performance of osculation as to the inherent desire for that which is supposed to be unattainable. Now, for instance, I read an article by an eminent sociologist on the--" She: •'j fs getting really chilly out here in the perch. I hefi better QC, 41.ta the honaaa)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of Hoe Warrington Town Council was held in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening. The Mal or (Alderman Roberts) presided and there were also present Aldermen J Fairclough (deputy Mayor), Heaton, Sutton, Shaw Green, Monks, Hutchin- son, Bolton, Councillors William Pierpoint, Baird, Mal- kin, Wilkinson, Davies, Peacock, Shilicock, 'A J Jolley, Smethurst, Bennett, L Greening, James Evans, John Evans, William Roberts,F R Roberts, John Roberts, W J Forshaw, Tinnion, Hulse, A Jolley, Sampson, Rogers, with Messrs J Lyon Whittle (town el-irk), S Wilson (deputy town clerk), J Fairhurst (borough treaserer),T Longelin (borough surveyor), Haddock (gas engineer), J Deis (aster engineer), C Madeley (librarian). MAYOR'S COMMUNICATIONS. THE THREATENED SCHOOL BOARD. The Mayon referring to the fo'l ming passed at a meeting of the ratepayers held at the Town Hall on the 23rd inst :—" Tnat in the opinion of this meeting the Corporation should make application to Parliament in the ensuing session for the powers of a School Board on the lines proposed in the Government Bill of the last session for the purposes of elementary education." said he had only to say that a special meeting of the Council would be called on the 9th N.evern per, to take the matter into consideration. There was no need for any discussion upon the matter that nigh.. WATCH COMMI ['TEE. THE NEW POLICE PREMISES. , At a meeting of the Watch Committee, held the other I day, it was resolved that the old Police Court be closed ion the 11th October, and that petty sessional business be transacted at the new police premises as and from that date.—At a meeting on the 221 d October, the fol- lowing certificate of the efficiency of the police was ordered to be entered on the minutes : " I hereby cer- tify that the police of the borough of Warrington has been maintained in a state of efficien:y in point of num- bers and discipline for the year ending the 29:h ot pterober, 1901. Home Office, Whitehall, 11th October, 1901. CHARLES T. RITCHIE, one of his Majesty's Prin- cipal Secretaries of State." Mr Alderman Heaton, t representative of the Corporation on the Lancashire Inebriates Acts Board, rspeited that the Board bad accepted a tender of £73,418, for the erection of the female division of the Inebriates Home at Laugh°. The minutes were approved on the motion of the MAYOR, seconded by Alderill•H SUTTON. FINANCE. At a meeting of the Finance and Estate Committee a letter was read from the Lecal Government B )ard as foTows :—I am directed by the Local Government Board to state for the information of the Town Council of Warrington that the Board have commenced the dis- tribution of the amounts payable to the Councils of counties and county boroughs from the local taxation account in respect of the current financial Year. In the absence of an adjustment of financial relations between the Councils of the County Borough of Warringtoo and the Connty of Lancaster the Board have obtained from the Commissioners of Inland Revenue a statement of the amount of It cal taxation licence duties collected in the borough during the financial year ended the 31st March, 1901, and they propose to apportion :•he amount of the instalment payable to the Councils from the local taxation licences estate duty grants on the basis of the figures thus fur- nished by the Commissioners. A first instalment, which will shortly be paid to the Councils, will be calculated accordingly. As regards the amounts pay- able from the local taxation (customs and excise duties the shares of the Councils in the several instalments will, pending the adjustment of financial relations, be calculated on the basis of the discontinued grants cer- tified by the Board under Section 22 of the Local Government Act 1888. Any un- derpayment or overpayment involved by the method of apportionment indicated above will be set right after the completion of the pending adjustment. The last letter was from the Assistant Secretary to the Local Government Board. (Mr Noel Kershaw) and read as f ollows :—"Sir. I am directed by the Local Government Board to state that they have had under consideration the report made by their Inspector, Mr North, after the inquiry held by him with reference to the application of the Town Council of Warrington for sanction to the borrowing of the sum of £15,000 for purposes of electric lighting. The Board have, decided to comply with the application, and their formal sanc- tion is forwarded to you herewith, lam to add that no payment must be made out of the loan, to any salaried officer of the Town Council, except in the case cf payments (if any) due to yourself for legal work not within your ordinary duties under the terms of your appointment as Town Clerk. In such a case the bills, as taxed, mar properly be paid out of loan moneys." ALDERMAN SHAW GREEN REPLIES TO CRITICISM. _</p>
        <p>THE " CONSOLIDATION " SCHENIE• Alderman Sidaw GREEN in moving the adoption of the minutes referred to the two orders of the Local Govern- ment Board. They were the consent of the Board to their borrowing in one case of £3,067, and in the other £15,000. The Board had given them a varying period for the repayment of each of the loans ; in one case 25 years, and in the other 27 years. Those varying periods were, under certain well-understcoi regulations of the Board, in accordance with the wear and tear of the buildings or other premises the subject of the loan. It had been suggested to him that be ought to may a word or two with reference to some very remarkable com- ments made at a meeting the other night. He asked whether he would he in order in doing so ? The Mayon : Yes, if it is pertinent to the subject under discussion. Aide man Saw GREEN sail : In one of our public balls on one of the evenings of 1 .et week, a certain! speaker referred to the indebtedness of the boroueb,and be said if a scheme which he propounded was only carried out, a saving equal to 101 in the pound per annum in the rates might take place. Well, if such a thing was possible, I suggest to you that the Finance Committee would have ben the first to make a move in that direction. (Bear, hear.) The suggestion he offered was ' this, that if the whole of our loans could be amal- gamated, or as he terms it, consolidated, and we could obtain a sufficiently long period for the redemption of them that the saving in Um difference between the amnunt we are now paying for the redemption and the amount that we then should be paying for the redemp- tion would be equal to ICcl in the pound per annum. A statement more calculated t damage the interests of the town could hardly be made, and it could only be made with the absence of information which might easily have been obtained if the gentleman who mads the statement had taken the trouble to get it. (11 ear, hear.) We have a considerable sum that we owe to our various bond-holders. That sum is in accordance with sundry Acts of Parliament, and various Orders of the Local Government Board, redeemable within varying periods. The period which this gentleman named was scmething like this. Such a town has had 70 years al- lowed for the redemption of its loan, and if that town had it, why could not Warridgton have it ? That is a very proper qaestim if it were possible. But it is not possible, and we as h have the management of these things know it is not possible, In order, therefore,that the Council may fully understand me when I say it is I not possible I purpose quoting from the Standing Orders of the House of Commons, which Standing Orders are binding upon every Committee of the House. (The Alderman quoted a passage to the effect that the period of redemption should not exceed 60 years.) Parliament has ordered these committee i not to grant more than 60 yeare for the redemption of any loan. That was passed in 1882 Prior to that time longer periods were granted and as far as we have been able to ascertain, the towns tins gentleman referred to were all granted their redemp- tive periods at a period ptior to 1882 We went to Parliament in 1877,and we got then on the concolidation of our loans periods representing 82, 75, and 78 years. It is utterly impossible to get these now. The men who had charge of the town in that day did their utmost and they did it very well. After 1882 we went to Parliament ourselves and Parliament granted us 60 years for the redemption of our waterworks indebtedness. We have since, no longer since than 1899, gone to Parli.ment, and were given for over £200,000 a period of 50 years and we have had many Orders from the Local Government Board since giving us 50 year,s so that were we to consolidate now we should have a very considerable period for the redemp- tion of our indebtedness tc-day. We did equate under the Act Gf 1899 and we obtained very considerable benefits by doing so. As an illustration, our repay- ment last year was a little over £ll,OOO. This gentle- man, referred to .£16,000 as the sum ot our annual redemption. Where he gets his figures from passes my</p>
        <p>:4° -</p>
