        <head>rilE EXAMINER, FRP ) A "V, NOVEMBER 1 1901.</head>
        <p>rilE EXAMINER, FRP ) A "V, NOVEMBER 1 1901.</p>
        <p>comprehension entirely. I ventured to tell him some little time ago that if he would come here and obtain the information we have his views woull be entirely altered, and I venture to say tn the responsible position I stand at this moment that we have dime exceedingly well in the whole of our applications to Parliament and to the Local Government Beard, and that it would be well-aigh impossible to do any better because we have told the Local Government Board the circual- stances of the town and they have given us their sympathy and their help in all our applications. It would be practically impossible also for another expert to take the course suggested by this gentleman, because it could only be done, as suggested by him, upon the Corporation issuing stock instead of borrowing as we now do upon bond. We have given daya of censidfra- tion to this, more days than this gentler:lo in had given minutes (hsar, hear,) and we come to the conclusion that our better course is to pursue the one we have been pursuing since the b orough was incorporated. Even Liverpool, which has adopted both schemes, namely the scheme of issuing stock and the scheme of borrowing upon loan, has f imed occasion to speak very highly of the method of borrow- ing upon bond. A few days ago the Finance Committee, which is a very large committee there,was exposing its thanks to the chairman and vice-chairmen for their services, and the chairmen, who is no mean authority—l refer to Mr Harwood Banner—(applause) —a name well-known in accountant circles thrciughout the kingdom—said he was very pleased indeed at the help the co.nmitte bad given them in borrowing upon bond, and he said he hoped they would pursue that policy, for it was greatly advantageous not only to the Liverpool Corporation but &amp;so to Liverpool people them- selves. We have found it in the same way advantage- ous not only to ourselves but to the people surrounding this town. There was a period not long ego when the whole of our debt was held by people in and surround- ing Warrington. The Mersey Docks and Harbour Beard, according to a report in a Liverpool paper a few days ago, is paying a little over £3 9s per cent, upon its in- d rbtedness. The Liverpool Corporation is paying a little over £3 49 per cent, according to the same authority. Warrington is not paying anything like that. (Applause). This ie a fact that cannot b mistaken, and which speaks in the highest possible terms for the way ia which the finances of the borough have been and are being conducted. There is just smother thing I would refer to: Be (the gentlemen at the meeting) took credit for the enormous increase in the assessable value of Warringenn. He said that when he began the agitation it wae £150,000, and just recently it amounted to £238,000, and I presume ha was himself taking credit tor thie very large increase. Well, now, by one of those curious circumstances he seems entirely to have missed the figures. there are two valuetioas. One is the improvement rate assessable value and the other is the Poor Rate. The difference between the two is exceeding great, I should think nearly £40,000. If he desired to be very accurate he would have taken the same basis, both at the beginning and at the end of the two peeiods he referred to. Tne difference is over £30,000 by adjusting these to their proper lines. In addition we have been to Parliament and have had a considerable increase of the Borough during the period, and these increases of COUfS3 make another very large difference in the extra assessment to which he referred. I don' L know that I need say more. I feel very full of the subject. If I had my own way I should like to sit down and be cross-examined upon it, but I have said enough to satisfy you, and I think the ratepayers of the borough, chat the Finance Committee has acted upon sound lines and are following a policy initiated by our predecessors and continued to the great advantage and benefit of the borough. (Applause). Toe DEPUTY-MAYOR seconded, and the minutes were passed.</p>
        <p>main be laid through Messrs. Pochin's works, to supply Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and S ms, Ltd., with Appleton water,and that the existing 3-inch main be usei for the supply of Winwick water, and that a supply of water be laid on to the lnompson memorial fountain at Win- wick at an annual eh arge of £l. At a further meeting of the Committee it was decided that the tender of Me,sra Bennie and Thompson to execute and complete new Sloers, service reservoir and other works at Apple- ton, according to specification, at £8,867 81 100 be acc pted, Tao Eagineer having reported that he had received and examined the valuations of the water maks in the townships of Cu/cheth and Kenyon, amounting to £842 12 ,and recommcnded that the same be accepted. it was resolvei that the valuations be accepted, and the amount thereof paid to the Leigh</p>
        <p>Rural Dietrict Council, Councillor EvANs moved the adoption of the minutes and explained to tale Council the nature ot the exten- sions which it is proposed to undertake at Appleton. The new service reservoir, he paid, would be a circular tank capable of containiag 700,000 gal.ens. Referring to tee extensions at Wiawick, he said the work there was highly satisfactory. Oa Saturday evening, in con- seq tence of necessary alterations ea the mains in con- nection with the extensions, the supply was shut off, Mr Dlag cal 3ulated that if the supply were shut off at six o'clock on Saturday evening, the necessary altera- tions would be completed by two o'clock on Sunday afternoon. In order to allow for contingencies he fixed the time for turning on the supply again at six o'clock. As a matter of fact the</p>
        <p>supply. was turned on again at ten o'clock on Sunday m3rolog. (El'ar, hear). Everything went off as merry a; a marriage bet,. Mr Dais informed him that that was the last time he would require to turn off the water, in connection with the mcensions. (Applause). Mr Baum seconded and tha minutes were passed. ELECTRIC LIGE! AND t'ONER. THE VALUE OF MOIORS. The Electric Lighting Committee recommended that the scheme and conditions as to the hire of eloctric</p>
        <p>motors submitted by the engineer be approved, and . .</p>
        <p>that application be made the Local Government Board for their sanction to the borrowing of the sum of £l,OOO fir the purchase of motors. Alderman MONKS in moving the adoption of the minutes, said the committee was endeavouring to on- e 'wage the use of electricity for power purposes. The d )mand for power had not been q lite so large as they anticipated ; in. fact it had been practically nil.. It . .</p>
        <p>WA9 perhaps owing t) the fact that those who .required</p>
        <p>power hadplant already in operation and they hesitated</p>
        <p>to threw out that plant. far ticrap and incur further ex-</p>
        <p>penditure for motors of which they had bad no experi- ence.</p>
        <p>Bv_the propoeed arrangeme-at such people w*ould</p>
        <p>he enabled 0 Malr'e the experimmt without running a financial risk which could in any way be considered unreasonable. Another reason why the committee was</p>
        <p>anxious to enc mrage the use of electricity for power purposes was that when the tramways commenced run- ning they would be face to face with the diffi :elty. At present the station was tannin.' with one s:4ff of men, working about seven or eight bours a day. When t tramways commenced it would mean that two other staffs of men wouldlbe regaired because the cars would not get Into the station until 11-30 p.m, and would re- quire to turn out about 5 or 5.30 so as to be able to meet toe requirements of the Board of Trade with reference so workmen's cars. I: would therefore be seen that</p>
        <p>power would be req tired for tramways for twenty out of the 24 hours and ten hours per day was considered too long for men to be °couple&amp; They had some doubt that when the tramways started wits a small s vvice the revenue would be sufficient to cover the cost of running for this extra period. If, the committee could obtain customers who would take power during the day they would help very considerably to pay expenses. That was the ehject the committee had in view in bringing forward that proposal. He was informed that this system had been adopted in other towns and had given general satisfaction. The charges for motors were reasonable and were such that even the smallest traders should b 3 able to bear them. He was sure that some traders would trio them if they knew how easily they coull be adopted for all kinds of work and in all situations. They were not bound to have the motor on the floor. It could be attached to the wall,it could be put on the roof or in the cellar. He had seen a motor working in a cellar. That showed how easily adsptible it was. He would strongly urge anyone requiring motive powerto setiously consider the terms on which the Corporation could supply electric power under the prop)sel arrangement with reterence to motors.</p>
        <p>Councillor BENNETT seconded the adoption of the minutes and stated that one great advantage which had not been referred to by Alderman Monks was that the use of electricity for power purposes would help to solve tne smoke nuisance. (Hear, bear, and Laughter.) The minutes were passed. THE TRAMWAYS SCHEME.</p>
        <p>A plan of the pronoseJ new entrance to the car he at the corner_ of Mersey-street .and Lower Bank-street</p>
        <p>was approved at a meeting of the Tramways Committee and it was vsolved that the details as to ventilation and ft Dish of the cars be lett in the hands of Maj r Cardew. The MAYOR moved and Alderman MONKS seconded the adoption of these minutes, which were approved. THE PROPOSED WIDENING OF WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Iraise any objection to this additional open space. He hoped the proposal which w as the last the Mayor would make durieg his term of office would be carried unani- mously, and be was sure that of all the good things the Mayor had done, none would be more appreciated than this. (Hear, hear). Alderman SUTTON said it had occurred to him that it might be desirable to defer the passing of such a reaolu- Lion until the new police premises had been compl-steri, lie quite appreciated all that the Mayor had said with reference to open spaces,and he thought they were very desirable indeed, but be thought the site occupied by the old buildings was perhaps too small to make much use of.—(" No.")—and the space would rather be a danger to the neighbourhood than anything else. The old buildings might perh spa be made useful for some time, as the residence of one of the policemen and as a tempo- rary lock-up. He therefore thought it would be desirable to defer the matter. Alderman BOLTON considered the old Police Court was no credit to Warrington. It was badly constructed and could not be profitably used for any other purpose. When he was a member of the Watch Committee it took something like £l5O or £2OO a year to keep the premises in a state of repair. The land could not be sold to bring in rates, and it would be of no benefit to the ratepayers whatever, except as a white elephant. (Laughter.) He thought it would be a suitable spot for an underground urinal. (Laughter.) An open space in that neighbourhood was much needed. Alderman IiELTON said he was sorry he could not vote for the resolution. The Corporation had had open spaces for years and they had not spent a penny upon them except to pay the rent. (Laughter.) If they pulled thee) buildings down it would look worse with the brick-iuts lying about than it did at the present moment. (Laughter.) Until the Corporation made better nee at the existing open spaces he was not in favour of adding to them. Councillor A. J. JOLLEy : Supposing this resolution is carried, will it prevent the Corporation from adding to the wilth of the street, The Mavis . No. Tho resolution was then carried.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. TEA MEETING.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. A. Naylor an Exile.</p>
        <p>A very successful tea meeting in connection with the above mentioned society was held in Wycliffe Hall, on Monday evening. About 250 persons sat down to tea. At the subsequent proceedings a capital programme was submitted. The Rev John Yonge (president of the society) occupied the chair in the absence of Mr R k. Naylor. In the course of a few introductory remarks the chairman stated that Mr R A Naylor had written to him from Pwllheli stating that he was an involuntary exile, driven away from his home in cmsequence of the insanitary condition of the neighbourhood of Grappenhall, Mr Naylor stated that he should not return borne until the nuisance was removed and his house was dangerous to live in. Hel (the chair- man) was sure they all sympathised with Mr Naylor in the position he found himself and also sympathis- ed with tee people who lived out there aud who could not get away from their homes. It was to be hoped that the local authority would do its duty quickly and make the district sanitary not only for. Mr Naylor, hut for those who had to live there whether they liked it or not. (Hear, hear.) The Chairman proceeded to thank the members of the brotherhood for the hearty vote of congratulation which was passed in his absence at a meeting on the previous day, on completing his 25th year of service as minister of Wycliffe Church. Twenty- lave years was a fairly-long time fr one man to be in one pulpit, but having obtained the help of God he had continued until this day. He did not feel quite as young as he did 25 years ago—(laughter)—but still he might be pardoned if be used the phrase that there was "life in the old dog yet," (Laughter). So long as he had the sympathy of his people at Wycliffe and the co-operation of the members of the P.S.A., he hoped to go on a short time longer. He hoped they would do their best to make the r.s.A.. a granter success and in this connection be appealed to the ladies to use their influence with the men. During the evening, several choruses were very finely sung by the Warrington Male Voice Choir un- der the conductorship of Mr S. Hassell. The choir has been recently formed but already it gives promise of a very successful career. Several indi- vidual items were also contributed by members of the choir. Solos were sung by Mr S Hassall, Mr Warburton,and Mr George Gough; a duet by Messrs Greenough and Gough; and the amusing quartette "Simple Simon" by four members of the choir. Miss Alice Bridge delighted the audience by her efficient rendering of" The Swallows" and " Love the Pedlar." Recitations were also contributed by Miss Alice Beswiek and Mr Starkey, and Miss Tred- well performed a mandoline solo. Mr. J. E. Balmer, of Manchester, delivered a very interesting and pointed address, in which he gave some excellent advice for the attainment of happi- ness in home life. The proceedings terminated with an acknowledgment of the services of all who had assisted, on the proposition of Mr. Bindloss.</p>
        <p>" Li ght and its Actions." Profevor Stirling continued his course of lectures in the Co-operative Hall, on Tuesday on " Toe senses: light, hearing and touch." The subjsct for the evening was "Light and its actions." The lecturer explained that light, physiologically, was a sensation. In an interesting way he demonstrated that light Was produced by the vibration of waves of ether corning from the sun. In speaking of the action of light, the lecturer showed the value of sunshine in destroying bacteria which was harm- ful to the individual and advised his hearers to gtt as much sunshine into their homes as possible. The Recent Burglaries at Warrington. At the Liverpool Quarter Sessions on Tuesday, before Sir W Forwoou (presiding) and other magis- trates, Michael or John Logan, 40, labourer, pleaded guilty to breaking and entering the dwelling house of Thomas Partridge, Imperial Hotel, War- rington, on September 2nd, and stealing 103 and a bottle of brandy ; also to breaking and entering the dwelling house of James Byrne and stealing a pair of trousers; and to stealing an overcoat, a dog licence, three keys, and a report sheet, the property of William Smith, at Warrington, on March 20th. Mr Four' prosecuted. Prisoner had only one pre- vious conviction against,him and that was six years ago. lie was sent to prison for four months with hard labour. The Late iMr. Beamont.—Tablet at Orford</p>
        <p>School. A tablet has been placed in Orford school to the memory of the late Mr William Beamont (the first Makyor of Warrington), bearing the f3llowing in- scription :—" In loving and reverent memory of William Beamont, of Ortml Hall, who built this school and endowed it for church services for the benefit of the Orf ard people and their children. This tablet is placed here by the widow, LB " The tablet, which has been supplied by Mr Aukland, sen, monumental mason and sculptor, Legh-street, Warrington, is o[ white marble with a border of black boua-cord granite.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>SANITARY WORKS COMMITTEE. THE WAGES OF THE MEN AT LONGFORD DEPOT.</head>
        <p>SANITARY WORKS COMMITTEE. THE WAGES OF THE MEN AT LONGFORD DEPOT.</p>
        <p>At a meeting held on October 2nd it was resolved that information bs obtained from other towns as to the hours of labour and wages paid for similar employment to that of the men at the Longiard Depot; and that the price of manure he reduced to the sum of £5 per ton, with tie usual discounts. Alderman MONKS moved the confirmation of the minutes of the committee and said that with regard to the resolution dealing with tae wages paid to the men at Longford, that it was not advisable to say much. He would like to inform the Council, however, that so far as the inquiries had gone they showed that the wages which tile committee were paying were above the average for the same labour performed by similar men in other towns. (Hear, hear). He thought that that in itself was a sufficient answer to some of the statements made at the meeting held a few weeks ago in regard to this particular point. (Hear, bear). The Alderman then referred to the resolution with refer- ence to the manure, and explained that it had been decided to reduce the price to meet competition and clear off the stock in hand. Councillor Jous EVANS seconded, and the motion was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>PROPERTY OWNERS AND THE COST OF STREET</head>
        <p>PROPERTY OWNERS AND THE COST OF STREET</p>
        <p>MAKING.</p>
        <p>Ald..lMatl IREATON moved the confirmation of the minutes of the Paving and Sewerage Committee, and Councillor TINNION seconded. Councillor JAMES EVAN., drew the attention of the Chairman and the general Council to the cost of certain back passages which had been made. S man of the pro. perty owners of Whitecross Ward did not quite under- stand the difference in cost in the construction of various back passages as it was estimated in the agenda. For instance the cost of paving the passage behind Cartwright-street worked out at 81 101 per lineal foot, and behind Bostock-street, along the same piece of land, the cost was 43 10 per foot. The owners thought that there was either some mistake, or some explanation should be given. Such considerations as this made a considerable difference in the price of a house. Those who bad to pay the piper should know exactly how much the work cost and how the money was spent. Councillor EVANS then went on to ask why a contract for wood setts had teen divided between two firms, when there was a difference of 2, 61 per 1,000 in their respective prices. Alluding to the question of municipal trading, he said he did no: know wilere to get a Council from if trading through limited liability companies was ! denied the members. Alderman Idosxs protested against what he con- sidered the wasting of the time of ,t he Council, Coun- cillor James Evans' question should have been brought up in Committee. The MAYOR: i cannot rule Mr Evans not of order. Councillor Evans : Some of the people have sought information privately and are not satisfied wits what information was given them. Alderman SUTTON said be agreed with Alderman Monks. These questions were only asked that the rate- payers nf the Ward might see their representative was doing something'. Councillor Ey:ass : I object to any such insinuations, Mderman SITTON : Then I withdraw them. Councillor PIERPOINT said Councillor James Evans was quite within his rights, and it wss not quite wise on the part of any Alderman to try to stifle discussion, (Hear, hear). These were matters of public interest, and it was the oral 7 way the public had of acquiring know- ledge ontside the committees. It wool lbe a lamentable thing if discussion was stifl &gt;cl in the Council. Councillor TINNION alluding to the tenders for wood setts, said Councillors Evans had asked the criestion how it was one firm was getting 23 61 per 1,000 more than another. It was nut his fault. If he had had his way there would have been no dividing of the contract and he protested against this dividing of contracts in favour of lomat firms, unless everything was cq sal. In this Case 23 6d per 1,030 could easily be accounted for in the quality of the setts, but he thought the dividing of the contract was a mistake. Alderman HEATON said the spttf, were wanted urgently He could not 10.8 Councillor Evans the explanation he required off-hand, but if he would make application to i the Committee, or if any of the nooole who had corn- ' plainPd wonld do so, they would be able to explain. MARKETS COMMITTEE—THE ASSISTAN INSPECTORSHIP.</p>
        <p>This committee met on the 14‘h October, when it was reported that the total imome from all sources for the past four weeks was £43/1 15 51 ; nerresoonding neriod last year, £4ll 19331 ; increase, £22 16; 21,- 11 was resolved that an advortisoment be inserted in the local papers inviting appltcationa for the position of assistant inspector of markets and inspector of weights and measures at the salary of 35; a week, with uniform, age of the candidates not to exceed 30 years, At another meeting it was resolved that Mr C Place be appointed an Inspector of Weights and Measures, and Aasiatant Inzpector of the Markets, according to terms of the advertisement at a salary of ,75; per week. Alderman Burcrussoli moved the confirmation of the mil rates. Councillor Roopot.s-seconded. —Carried. GAS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>ALTERATIONS AND ADDITIONS TO THE OFFICES. At a meeting of the Gas Cornmitl-ee, on the 11th October, it was resolved that Mr Percy Si[cock be.in- structd to prepare plans and estimate of the cost of alterations and additions to existing offices necessitated by the rogoirements of the Tramways Committee, and that the Tramways Committee btr requested to furnish this committee with a plan showing the portion of the Mersey-street works required for tramway purposes. At another meeting it was resolved that the tender of Mr James Wood for the printing and stationery re- quired by this department for Vara next twelve months, P clording to specification, at £B4 184, be accepted. The Tramways Committee haying submitted a plan show- ing the portion of the M7vey-street works required by them for tramway purposes ; it was resolved, that Mr Percy Si!cock be instructed to value the land so required; that the cost of fixing !autos on the instructions of the ! Street Improvement and Lighting Committee be charged to that committee,and that the price of coke be reduced to Ss 4d per ton. The trinUtP9 were psssed, on the motion of the DEPUTT-MAYOR, seconded by Alderman HEATON. The minutes of several other committees, which con- tained nothing of public interest were passed formally. THE WATER WORKS EXITNSIONS. - -</p>
        <p>The Water Committee recommended that an 8-inch</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee it was resolved that the following resolution of the Tram- way. Committee of the 21st August last be resc.nded :—" That an application be made to IPar- liacnent in the ensuing session for powers to construct a tramway from Stafford-road to the Mulberry Tree Inn Stockton Heath, and for a tramway along Grappenhall. road and Ackers-lane to join with tramway Na. 11 in Knutsford-road. Mr J J Webster having submitted detailed plans and specifications for the proposed widen- ing of Warrington Bride, the General Purposes Com- mittee have resolved that the Town Clerk and 13 srough Surveyor report thereon to the next mesting of this Committee.</p>
        <p>Tne filaYon moved the adoption.of the minutes. and</p>
        <p>in referring to the resolution of the Tramways Com- mittees respecting an application to Parliament, said the Corporation did not intend going to Parliament for powers this session, except possibly in connection wich their application for School 13,ard powers to deal with educational matters. jr was therefore desirable to reecind the resolation of the Tramways Committee and the Council would have an early opportunity of recon- sidering the matter. The Parliamentary agents of the Corporation advised them to apply for a Light RailwaV Order instead of a tramways order for the projeated line.</p>
        <p>Alderman FAIRCLOUGH seconded the minutes and they were approved. AN 0 ?EN SPACE FOR IRLAM-3TREET. THE MAYOR'S PROPOSAL --• _</p>
        <p>The MAYOR subnaitted the following motion :—" That on the completion and occupation of the new police premises the old police buildings in Erlam-street he taken down, and that the site be laid out as an open space. His object in bringing forward that resolution. he said, was to get the Council committed to what hg thoueht was a very necessary and desirable improvement. Oil a previous occasion be ro- ferred to the desirability of considering the necessity of taking down these buildings and making an open space which would be a desideratum to this populous neigh- bourhood, and he thought by the opinions given utterance to,the Council practically unanimously agreed to the suggestion. (Gear, hear.) he thought the effect of open spaces, especially if they were treated as they might easily be treated in Warrington, and not left simply as open spaces without the slightest attempt t 3 make them presentable, comfortable or pleasing in many ways, were for the Public good. They had a great deal to learn in this respect. He had the pleasure and, privilege a few weeks ago of visiting a considerable portion of Sauth Germany and his eyes were opened to e a great deal that was q lite possibl with them at War- rington and which it was very desirable should be attempted. He admitted that the climate paved the way for conditions on the Continent that could not exist horse, but still netwithstanding all th 3 difficulties we had to centend a with, we might do a great deal more for our not too pretty towns. Any plant, shrub, or tree that could be grown was an addition to the pleasuresthe comfort and the health of people in the town. He could not conceive what posaible purpose these old buildines could be used for. Certainly they were not fit to be converted into municipal buildings or offices for!they were obsolete. They might be used as an old warehouse but the exoense of maintaining them would be considerable. Oa the other hand if the buildings were taken down and sotnething done to make the open space worth lookng upon he felt sure they would confer a boon on the people in that put of the town which would be appreciated for many years. It was not fair that the improvements should b 3 conferred ex- clusively on the better class portions of the population. The Queen's gardees in Bold-street were to his mind a standing obj ,ct lesson of doing the kind of thing he was now suggesting in other parts of the town. (hear, hoar.) Councillor BENNETT said he would like to second the resolution because he was usually considered to be opposed to the demolition of ancient property. (Laugh- ter.) [is was oaly in favour of the preservation of ancient buildings when they were picturesqle or had interesting associations. The old police buildings were certainly the rev.rse of picturesque., they were not by any means ancient, and their associations were, he sup- Posed, of a painful character. However much he was in favour of the preservation of buildings, it did not aep"y in this particular eise. (Laughter). He thought they would be doing a wise and reasonable thing in de- molishing the old Police Court. He was in favour of the largest number of open spaces possible, in all part of the town, but he thought that an open space wag imperative in a district like this, where the population was so crowded. It would be a step towards making the surroundings of the people more cheerful, pleasan and heat ay. He did not think the buildings could be put to any real use, and he was satisfied that not only dwellers in float neighbourhood, but people throughout the whole town were begin ning to realise that it was necessary to have attractive spots in various parts of the town. He did not thinkd the man who ju igei everyth leg by the rates caul</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>BRONCHITIS and and ASTHMA</head>
