      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>NOTABLE LANCASHIRE CASE.</head>
        <p>A NOTABLE LANCASHIRE CASE.</p>
        <p>A Complete Cure.</p>
        <p>Certain phases of recurrent fever still haffl the medical profession, despite the conscientious effort of scientists whose self-sacrifice in isolating them- selves in pestilential districts entitles them to the highest regard. At Liverpool a man, who knows yellow fever from painful experience, is able to give much valuable information on the matter. It was in Rio de Janeiro that an engineer named William Brook, living at Fletcher-grove Edge-lane, Liverpool, twice tell a victim to " yellow jack" but he grt over both attacks. This was some rears ago, but in Ausust of 1899 he was afraid there was a recur- rence of the trouble. The mischief began by a "heavy feeling," as he described it to a newspaper roan who is inte- resting himself in these investi- gations.and a pain in the right lung. " I wheezed Ike an old pair of bellows at every breath and gradually getting weaker, found that' coule keen Practic- ally nothing on my Etomaoh,— he said to the reporter. "A. terrible cough also developed, which frequently made me fall off a chair or sofa, and all my friends thought that I was not long for this world.l was com- pelled to throw up my work long before the stage was reached, as I was practicslly helpless. The doctor's opinion, in his own words. was this am very sorry to tell you, poor man, but it is Consumption of the right lung', and he addei that if I had tcy arrangements to make I had bet- ter make them. By this time I had waet3d away</p>
        <p>MR. WIL: (f,val apliala.)</p>
        <p>Consumption and Lung Trouble followed Fever.</p>
        <p>terrlbly, and it appeared that I would never b 3 my— soil again. I had no strength, and felt unfit for we slightest exertion. This was the position of sffirs (scores of people in the Edge-lane district of Liver— . pool know of my case) when I saw the account in a Liverpool paper of a ;.".' markable cure of a somewhat . similar case by Dr. Will'ams' Pink Pills for Pale People." "I thought they could do no harm," he told the writer, " and the doctors arpeared to be doing MP very little good, FO I tried a box of Dr Williams' Pink Pills. "On the third day after starting them I felt an im- provement. I kept on taking the pills and gradually the vomiting ceased, and the dreadful couch got better. L astonished my wife one day by asking for a chop. From that time on I began to eat better and improve in strength. The wasting away stopped, and I began to put on flesh. After being away from work for eighteen months, I am hack again cow, feeling' h?.tter and stronger than I have done for the past four yearr.'' " Have yo,i an objection to your experience being published ? " the writer inquired. "N, I am not against it being published,becauso I believe, as sure as that cup and saucer is there (tapping the articles with his forefinger), that if it were not for Dr Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People I should not he here now."</p>
