        <p>make new blood and impart strength to the nervous system. There is ample evidence that they have effected cures of the following disorders:, Rheumatism An2emia Consumption Sciatica Biliousness Heart Troubles Nervous Breakdown Neuralgia St. Vitus' Dance Liver Complaint Paralysis Eczema Influenza's After-Effects Locomotor Ataxy Ladies' Ailments Sold only in packages bearing full title, Dr.Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People (seven words), by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, Holborn Viaduct, London. Price 2s. 9d. a box, six boxes 135.9 d. Sold also by Chemists. Get the genuine—substitutes never cured anyone and never will, so mind you ask for Dr. Williams'.</p>
        <p>in wince a Christian nation might rightly enter upon war. But such times were few,lar fewer than soldiers and politicians and lovers of military glory, would hay them believe. They also needed to be clear and un avoidable. They were so few that a Christian nation was justified in the longest hesitancy, in the most strenuous righteous effort to avoid in every policy of concession and conciliation that the greatest magnani- mity could vouchsafe. And then, if al.er all, a Chris- tian nation became unhappily involve, however justly as it might appear, the highest justice required that strife should cease on the earliest instant that rightecuS terms were possible. It was not for Christian men to talk of "fighting on to the bitter end! " There ought to be a willingness for peace the moment it was attainable. He ventured to ask their thought to the question whether that moment in our own terrine conflict was not close at band, if it be not already come?' Reference was made by the preacher to Alfred's quali- ties as a conspicpus la*giver and a brilliant and devoted teacher. He was i THE EDUCATOR OF HIS FEOPLII. His expressed desire was that "all the youth of England of free men be iet: to learn until they are eine to read English writing." It was marvellous how truly modern a spirit this monarch possessed. And also how evange- lical a spirit, tor, said he " Far desire is that all the free born youths of my people may persevere in learning until they can perfectly read the English scriptures." That wish, indeed, he was not able to carry out. It was most remarkable that this enthusiasm for the knowledge of the scriptures should have been so sup- reme in the soul of this early English king. Evidently he favoured the evangelical policy of the open Bible and the Bible acie- sible to everybody. Ile had found the book to be lamp and light to his own path and ho wanted all beside to come to that happy experience. This was the true policy of every enlightened lover of his country. Let the word of God have " free course "and it would be " glorified' in the spreading enlightment of the people. Let education be religious in the truest sense.. Let the children be made in every p,ssible way familiar with Bible history, Bible principle, Bible teaching as to . G3d and duty. Sin and goodmss, the meaning of this life and the reality of that to come. But the Bible was not a OY Ctarian book and should never be made so. There was no sect and no church whatever that could claim exclusive understanding of tie Book et God. It was not seemly, it was not right, it woe riot true, for a reprEssntatiue of any one church to tell members of other churches that he and his church. only "</p>
        <p>" UNDERSTAND THE BIBLE</p>
        <p>_ Nor ought any one sec'.; or ichurch Ito claim special and ex:iluive power or privilege in national education upon any such ground. Tee tims would come when at that would come to a desired end. One of the most startling revelations awaiting some good men when they got across the River would be the presence in "the City of the Great King" of multitudes, who, disowning their particular church or creed. Clary had deemed ex- cluded from the true Church or God. It was a fair inference from all we knew of illfred to believe that if he bad lived tc-day he would have been in the very forefront of the broadest and widest and ompletear. 10heme of education.</p>
      </div>
