      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>KING ALFRED.</head>
        <p>KING ALFRED.</p>
        <p>Reference by the Rev. John Yonge.</p>
        <p>" King Alfred—great in ideal, faith, character, and service—a teacher and exemplar for the Eoglaod of to-dav " was the subject of a discourse preached in Wycliffe Congregational Church,Warrington. on Sunday evening. There was a large congregation. The preacher based his remarks on the text " The righteous shell be in everlasting remembrance," Psalm exit., part of 6.11 verse). The preacher observed that on the 28:h October, 901,—a thousand years ago—in the city of Winchester, did that illustrious English prince, whose memory had been cherished by the people of England a " Alfred the great.le was simply historical y true to say that to the comparativele brief life of Attred,Eegland owed more Chant° any other of her kings,and more even than to any other other eons. And when it was remembered that the greatest of all Altred's characteristics, that which lay at the basis of his, whole personality, which built 'up and coloured and controlled his entire life, was his religious faith, there could be no need to ap3:a;ise for directing attention to his memory in such a place as that, la referti3g to TILE DIGII IDEALS DE ALFRED</p>
