      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>ROVEMBER IsT 1901.</head>
        <p>ROVEMBER IsT 1901.</p>
        <p>One result of the South Another War Liability. African war may be to prove the soundness of at least one point in the theory of M Btocu, namely, that war will ultimately become imposs- ible because of the expense. It is, perhaps, well, that the richest State on earth should have :beers the subject of this experiment, because what is sufficient to convince British Statesmen, with all 1 their practically unlimited resources fron3 the wealth and credit of the Empire, should be Ovid- eiace enough for any Government that maY he inclined to weigh the argument by the test of ireason and experience. That there is rapid pro- gress being made to a decision that we cannot any longer afford war is becoming every day more evident, and will no longer be disputed when the CHANCELLOR of the EXCHEQUER next comes to ley his budget before the House of Commons. We have already spent something like £200,000p00 in South Africa, and the game is still going merrily on, at an expenditure computed at £1,250,000 par week. Lord ROBERTS has had his I£loo,ooo with his earldom; and although poor IButLEn. has been disposed of in a singularly differ I ent manner, there will not be lacking other claimants among our generals to large donations Ifrom the public purse. Now we are ant ering 00 another field of liability, the extent of which Ce° only be faintly appreciated as yet. The Commis- siosi which was appointed to investigate the claims of foreigners who were deported from the Transvaal under military necessities has arrived st a stage in its proceedings when it has been able to report some of the results arrived at. The report, so far as it goes, is satisfactory, in respect that sli the Powers whose subjects have claims against us. with the exception of France and the Nether lands, have agreed to accept a lump sum in name of all compensation to claimants of their respectiv° nationalities, and take the responsibility of the distribution of the money. It seems that under International law a militant State is eatitled te deport subjects of foreign States who may 19e proved to be hostile or dangerous to militia operations. But the hasty and wholesale clear an :es from the Transvaal rendered necessary bi military exigencies gave little opportunitY for discrimination, with the result the° many innocent persons were hustled ont of the country at considerable loss as well 0 personal inconvenience. Our Government de- clares such people should be liberally compensated; i 1 but it is well that we have got Germany, Rusgifi and other Continental Governments to undertalt° the appraisement.of their claims. It will save from everlasting litigation with private claimant , and much international friction which wont nave resulted in consequence. It is all nice sn' i generous and easy and comfortable as it has bee; ' arranged; but it is to cost us nearly £70,000, 80 that is not the half of the case. The total valne of the claims with whic'a the Commission hale had to deal was £1,277,000, of which £727,o°Gt were put forward by France and the Netberland4 - alone, and at present remain out of the settle ment. The amounts claimed by 'other natienal6i: ties which are to be settled under the arrer'gte ment mentioned total £550,000 ; and they ere, be settled for a sum of L 69,550, someth'e; like 121 per cent, of the claim. That 5ee'131.1 a fair bargain, and is said to rest a careful examination of 1,252 claims have been investigated by the Comrniesi°a !' BD e The question remains. Will France s , Holland be able to sink their antipathetic feehng and accept a settlement on a similar basis ? TinY, : certainly will not take less, and therefore auotear; . 100,000 will be incurred before the clans the - finally settled. But this is only a drop bucketliThe claims of loyalists: and neutraeetier'. , ' , Cape Colony, Natal and the newly-acquired . llow tory have still to be considered, and we ca ~ li</p>
        <p>hi. ki )d.</p>
        <p>the o lay We ),000 ;oing I at d his Poor ifter- Aber Acne ; on</p>
        <p>caU ornie- the the 311 80 de to Dort, 1111 linst ,ber" Lana° ctive</p>
        <p>de- Lted; take</p>
        <p>'l,, I</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>k</p>
        <p>very well be less generous to our own people than to those of other nations. And then there is the teplenishing of the country wnich has been devas- tated—the re-housing of the 100,000 Doer Prisoners who are still on our hands. and cannot be dumped down in a wilderness empty-handed, With their women and children from the concentra- tion camps. It becomes very apparent that if there had been a Government in power which had the moral courage to face an honest budget the War would have been ended, by hook or by crook) before now. Still financial necessity is a force that Must tell its tale before long. Then the country Will learn its lesson, and return to paths of sanity. All the world also, though it may sneer, will profit by our experience, and war will become more a budget affair than ever it has been.</p>
        <p>An article, or rather The Militia Ballot and mpulsory Service. two, in the November Co number of the Nine- ?-eenth Century deals with the probable adop- tion of some measure of compulsory military ser- vice such as is provided by the Militia ballot. The luestion will certainly confront the people of this country very shortly. It seems to be taken for granted that the Government will have to admit that the new scheme of Army Reform is still a scheme on paper, and therefore a failure so far as anything in the sense of reform is to be under- ttood. The voluntary system has not had a fair trial, either on the lines of the GJvernment policy Which refused to put any kind of emphasis on the Pay and comfort of the troops,or its action which in a moment of panic offered five shillings a day to the waifs of the streets to go and parade themselves as Yeomanry, without either the Physique or training in riding and shooting Which would have entitled them to the money. tut having made their trial and failed, there is every likelihood that the Government will find circumstances driving them into a position where they must declare for compulsory service of some sort. The question, therefore, which the country Will have to face is whether the powers are to be revived which exist under the Militia Acts enabling county authorities to ballot for the number of men required to make up the deficiency in the local quota contributed to the Militia. tither this must be done, or some other method adopted to make good the enormous lapse in the strength of our military forces which will occur at the close of the South African War. The longer the end is in coming, the greater will be the effect in leaving our huge army so depleted as to be non-existent for all practical purposes. The end, When it does come, will find many ix en of the regular force with their time of service expired, and many more entitled to retire to the ranks of the Reserves. What have we got to put in their places? Even the supply of raw, immature 3'cuths which have lately been drained into the array is giving out, and apparently we shall be left without an army so far as foreign service is concerned. Compulsion in any form is obnoxious to the British spirit, and it is open to conjecture that the application of the ballot is only an aggra- vation of the evil. But the attempt to incorpor- ate the Volunteers as a part of our regular system undergoing periodical training has broken down. It may be that the matter has not been gone about properly. It is difficult to see why a volunteer army cannot be got out of the 230,000 Volunteers in the country, prepared for a Period to do garrison duty and stand for nations defence, as they are in duty bound to do. The 'Militia system is not the same as the Continental zstem, and it may not be so good. A month's GclidaY for military training is all very fine, if the „ verranent would compel employers to keep open `lle situations which Volunteers will have to leave in order to engage in the training. But can they 080? And, in any case, is it possible to prevent tither way the utmost confusion in industrial con- cerng. which will only be felt the harder by the Sectton of the community upon whom it falls because it is their luck? The Government car-</p>
        <p>tainly cannot claim to have risen to the spirit cf the age in their military proposals : and it is to li)e feared that if the Militia ballot is to be their next resort they will 3nly find themselves going from bad to worse.</p>
        <p>It has remained for a uniitary correspondent of the most Conservative of journals to suggest a P.esv proposal for ending the war. The writer 'implores the Government to order the guns home, and then, perhaps, there may be a chance, he thinks, of getting at the Boers. He regards our heavy metal as a drag on the columns, compelling them to keep to the roads, and causing their pace to be slow and easy to calculate. Why, in effect, he asks, should we handicap ourselves to this extent against our more fortunate opponents, who, comparatively speaking,:have few guns? At first sight this might seem to be a paradoxical suggestion; but a well-balanced mind recognises that rules of warfare constantly need to be modified in order to meet change of circum— stances. There is little fear that our troops will again encounter large masses of the enemy for a set battle. The muster of burghers during the last few months has in no case gone beyond a thousand or two. It may be fairly argued that if we had no guns to impede a retreat our men could avoid any large force of Beers that could be got together, and go on with the policy of harry- ing the small commandos It might not be heroic but it would be business. Our guns like the swords of hastily trained young military gentlemen whom We see on the stage, are more noticeable for -obstruction than defence. The Government was Wrong in preferring unmounted men for the veldt but there is something to be said for the employ- ment of soldiers without guns. We do not assert that the case is conclusive, but at least it may form fresh material for comment by the war experts who sit in offices, and have been rather reticent of late.</p>
        <p>Whatever may be thought of the method of nxecution at Auburn prison, no sentimental pity . eed be wasted over the criminal himself. An Inquiry into the motives which prompted Czolgosz his too successful attack on President McKin- ley may be useful as a means of determining , preventive measures against the commission of n.linilar acts ; but whether the anarchist was Influenced by personal spite or by a pernicious creed makes no difforence to the horror oe the crime. The act committed at Buffalo on August dth shocked the world, perhaps, more than any Anarchist outrage in the records of modern his- tory ; for the victim was no despot, backed up by • Military power against the choice of a nation, but the freely-elected head of a free people. In the case of such a criminal qualms as to the method of despatch are not likely to be very active; yet there is room for doubt as to whether electrocu- tion produces really as sudden and as painless a death as has been represented, while it is still Illore questionable whether its employment is `Politic. The idea of torturing, condemned persons PI nature yields to the horrible agonies of hours has rightly been abandoned by civilization; but is there much advance in humanity if advantage is taken of the crime to experiment on the effects of Vuwerful currents of electricity on the system ? .;" there in this method of inflicting death the tLgunrainy which is the main deterrent factor in 4;,iat 13ital punishment with such a class of men as from, which Anarchists are drawn ? These ,ar questions which even the justice of the death .tmtence in itself cannot altogether exclude.</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>j</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Latest inquiries among wholesale and retail Tobacco Traders show there had been an antici- pation that the Imperial Company will give • all that the Tobacconists' Alliance have asked in the way of net profit, and it is generally understood Ghat the minimum profit desired is 20 per cent Commenting on the circular referred to below, the " Daily Mail" sass the American trust have "re- duced the prices of most of their imported cigar- ettes to an extert which permits the retailing of them to the public at greatly lower figures, while the British amalgamation has done nothing in this direction as yet.' Still the belief is entertained that the trade throughout the provinces at least will rally to the support of the Imperial Company.</p>
        <p>It will interest smokers to know that the British I Tobacco Combine have just announced to their customers that the organising committee of the Imperial Tobacco Company (of Great Britain and Ireland), Limited, after giving full consideration to the position of the said customers, feet that the amalgamation of interest hitherto in competition will result in a saving of expenses, and will enable the said Company to confer increased advantages on their clientele. All this one can very well un- derstand,but where the smoking customer comes in for assured benefit it has not been so easy to dis- cover. A scheme is in preparation for giving the Company's trading customers a bonus proportion° I to the amount of the:r purchases, full particulars of which are to be forwarded, but whether there will be anything in the shape of a bonus, or a sim- ple saving on his purchases for the smoker, has not tfanspired. The Imperial Tobacco Company take over the respective business as from to-day, 2nd November, provision having been made for its cus- tomers to receive the benefit of the new scheme at once. The respective manufactories are closed to- day for stock-taking purposes.</p>
        <p>At the last moment, the Associated Can!owners of Seuth Wales gave notice, this week, that they would regard the proposed stoppage of work on Thurslay,on the part of the colliers,as illegal. Mon- mouthshire collieries were included in the notice. But the belief was that notwithstanding the notices exhibited at the pitheads of all the associ- ated collieries, there would be a general cessation of work on the last day of the month, and arrange- ments were made for the holding of a number of mass meetings of the men. A Merthyr telegram set forth that on all hands the South Wales colliers were fully determined to observe Thursday as a further holidav,with a view to restricting the out- put of coal. Outsiders in various parts of the dis- trict, and beyond, look on with much uneasiness.</p>
        <p>Without going out of one's way to dwell upon the "tyranny of Trades Unions. it may fairly be suggested that the leaders of the South Wales miners have something to answer for to the public in advising the men to keep up their wages by keep- ing down the supply of coal, and thus raising the price of this indispensable commodity in every household. When the reader opened his paper on Monday morning, he was told that" with a view to keeping up prices, the whole of the collieries in Rhondda Valley were idle" that day and on Satur- day. There are not many working men in other parts of the country who can afford to leave off ' work a couple of days a week whenever it pleases them, and the Welsh miners must have little to complain of with regard to their pay if they can indulge in these holiday freaks without feeling any financial inconvenience, and certainly they weuld not be so ready to play if they needed money. The cause of the stoppage. however, is the peculiar idea of the men and their leaders as to the operation of economic laws, their theory being that the price of fuel is not determined by supply and demand, but by supply alone. If prices fall they argue that the best way to adjust the balance is to stop the output; and they are very persistent in this species of folly When the recent coal boom was at its height and they could have made a good deal of money, the miners kept the supply down, lest by raising large quantities prices would not still further advance, and so orders had to be extended over a longer period. Thus the output of South Wales alone, of all districts of the Kingdom, last year showed a de- cline instead of an increase, and, notwithstanding this limitation prices have shrunk, and still the colliers fancy they have solved the question of supply and demand by the simple and convenient process of playing "one-third of the usual work- ing time ! It never occurs to them, of course, that the shrinkage in rates is due to a diminution of the demand for Welsh coal.</p>
        <p>An announcement is made which should have an especial interest for young people and others who are accustomed to receiving" tips" at Christmas, and to having them in new silver. They will have to content themselves this year with " Christmas boxes" in the old coinage. For a Bank of England official has notified that the existing supply of silver is sufficient and that there will be no Christmas issue of coins bearing the late Queen's effigy. In a tone of lofty rebuke, at which the public are possibly expected to quail, it is adde I that the real re uirements of the circulation will govern future issues," and not any desire on'the part of the public to possess no silver coins other than new at Christmas time." Perhaps some of the public will feel just a little disposed to suggest that the de- mands of bills and taxes at Christmas time are such that any coins will be acceptable, old or new, when the festive but expensive season comes. But, as if to make some amends, another announcement anpears, to the effect that there will be a Nev 7 Year's issue of coinage bearing the King's effigy. Wise children with generous relatives will certainly take old coins at Christmas, and perhaps think it the plain duly.7of their elders to add thereto ex- amples of the new coinage of the New Year; but wise relatives will nol, feel it incumbent on them- selves to read this aloud to their young "hopefuls."</p>
        <p>Realers in provincial districts which are over- , crowded will do well to make mental note of the fact that one of the resolutions adopted by the Association of Municipal Corporations the other day, affirmed the need of extending the term of local loans if the Housing of the Working Classes Act is not to become a dead letter. It would be easy to mention several towns, small as well as large, the authorities of which are hesitating to move in the matter, not because the housing of the poorer classes is not safficiettly pressing, but on account of the period allowed for the rspayment of loans under the Act being considered too limited to justify them in taking steps to borrow the money required.</p>
      </div>
