      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>BRUNSWICK MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLASS. In connection with the above swiety it paper was delivered on Wednesday night by Mr Frank Littler on "My Id eel Sunday School. Messrs. Moore, Douglas Saunders and Hayes took part in the discussion. EUNCORN BOARD SCHOOL ELECTION. A meeting in favour of toe Progressive candi- dates will be held on Friday week the Bth Novem- ber. The principal speaker will be Ellis Griffiths Esq., M.P., and speActies will also be given by the candidates. The place of meeting is Brunswick School. PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER THE CLOCKroa B BOOTS IN LA.NCA SHIEE.—Advt ELLESMERE-STREET IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY. At the above So:A.:sty on Friday night a paper was given by Mr Thomas Saunders on Write Merchant of Venice." There was a very good discussion and a vote of thanks was passed to the essayist. BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER Trot CLOCK.—Ad ASK for CUENINUHABes HIGH-CLASS mihricuat, , arone.—Acivt ST. LUKE'S CONGREGATIONAL MISSION. On Sunday evening roe cook sermons were preached by Mr W Hickman, Warrington. There were good services the Mission Hall being well packed. The choir under the conductorship of Mr IA E Penny rendered several special anthems. Mr T Tickle presided at the organ and Mr T Lacy ac- companied the singing with his flute. The mission lhas been recently renovated and the services wars financial and otherwise very successful. RUNCORN HARRIERS. This club had an open date on Saturday in order that the members might attend the cycle parade. There was rather a poor muster, but those who did turn out made themselves useful as collectors, and J Povey was successful in winning the prize offered by the Parade Committee for the largest collection with pole, The club's fixture for Saturday next is Winnington, at Winnington. SERIOUS FIRE NEAR RUNCORN. As large stack containing the produce of a 12 acre field belonging to Mr John Ackerley, Astmore Farm, was discovered to be on fire on Tuesday morning about five o'clock by a man named Samuel Withington. The Runcora fire brigade was quickly summoned under deputy Superintendent Cousins. There was a good supply of water, the Bridge- water Canal being close at hand. By ten o'clock , the fire was extinguished, but not until damage was done to the extent of £l6O. The damage is covered by insurance. entariGws A 1 CRYSTAL OIL. the best &amp; safest lamp oil. S GARLAND, 51 to 55 BrldgeSt,—Advt. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR GOOD BOOTS.—Adv ST. PAUL'S WESLEY GUILD. The first meeting of the newly formed Reading Circle in connection with the above was held in the Guild Parlour, Brunswick School, last Thursday week. Thera was a good attendance, Mr Thomas Saunders was in the chair. A paper was read by Mr George Hayes, the subject being " Life of Charles Kingsley." The essayist gave a descriptive account of Kingsley's life and character, making reference to the author's descent from the King- sley's near Frodsham ; also his visit to Chester. A discussion ensued, the following members taking part : Mrs Simkin, Miss WeUs, Messrs J Douglas. W A Lightfoot and H Dodd. Two songs entitisd "Call the cattle home," and "The three Fishers," both written by Charles Kingsley, were rendered by Miss Simian who was:alc )rded a good reception. The success of this gathering augurs well for the future meetings of the Reading Circle which are to be held monthly.</p>
        <p>WESTON WESLEYAN SCHOOL. On Atonclav evening a lecture was delivered by the Rsv A Wells on " Dr William' Tindal, dlartyr ' Mr S Dodd presi ling, The lecturer referred to the growing apathy amongst young people of the pre- sent age with respect to historical events of the past. Many are not acquainted with the circum- stances associated with the history of the freedom and conscience of the word of God in this country. A great writer said that teading the life of great men was profit Ibis company and must by doing so inevitably reap something from them. Tindal was one of the grandest and greatest men that ever trod. He the lecturer would yield t no one the admiration he felt for Tindal. To him we owe a debt of gratitude which time itself will never be able to efface notwithstanding the fact that we have in the annals of our country such great heroes as Nelson, Wellington and Wilberforce, heroes which were ever against everything which embraced wrong and slavery, and who snapped the fetters of bondsmen by making thousands of nut fellow creatures free, Yet he yielded not the praise from the man who broke the power of the Pope of Rome—the min who gave to the ploughboys in this country the Book in which are the wonderful words of God.</p>
        <p>! THES-IrIYE3 !—lforr—often have you said I, this to yourself Scores of tim.s. Well, don't despair, write to MELLIi 283, Oxtors.atreet. Hanohester. Eye Snecialist. 27,000 cases aural. Fall particulars Free. (10) —9.dvt Singular County Court Case. At the Warringtoo County Court on Monday. be- fore his Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, K.C., Messrs Watson and Sons. Limited, soap manufac- turers, Leeds sued Elizabeth West, grocer, Stockton Heath, for £,. 7.; 4i, for three boxes of soap.—‘lr Crompton (Messrs Browne and Crompton) who ap- peared for the plaintiffs, called Robert Booth, a carter, in the employ of Messrs Isherwood and Com- pany, who said he delivered three boxes of sou) to the defendant on behalf of the London and North- Western Railway Company.—Defendant said she had only received two boxes. She denied receiving any soap in May.—The witness Booth said the soap was delivered in May and the date was on the rail- way form defendant's daughter signed.—liis Honour said the original consignment would have to be proved and Mr Crompton must bring wit- nesses from Leeds. If he found that alter all de- fendant was wrong she would have to pay all costs. and if what she said was proved to bs untrue he would consider whether he would not prosecute her for perjury.—Defendant: I have told the truth and I cannot do more. A VIOLENT POACHER. At the Runcorn Police Court on Toursday. before Mr E Imison (in the chair; and Mr R H Posnett, Charles Stubbs, Recksavage, was charged under warrant with having trespassed in search of game at Sutton on the 17th inst, and also with having assaulted Frederick Barnes, assistant gamekeeper, The evidence showed that prisoner and a man named Parkinson who was dealt with at the Petty Sessions on lvionday were trespassing on land be- longing to Mr Talbot, Barnes chased them, catch- ing Stubbs, who struck him in the face. He let go but followed when Stubbs produced a revolver and threatened to blow his brains out. Prisoner denied having a revolver in his possession and was fined 40; and 17s 61 costs for the first offence and was sent to goal for 14 days on tbe second charge.</p>
