      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <p>RUNCORN CYCLE PARADE.</p>
        <p>On Siturday afternoon the cycle parade, which has now become one of the recognised institutions of the town, took place amid almost ideal cenditions. Every- one is aware of the noble aim which animates the wheelers, and there are few indeed in Runcorn who did not wish them " God speed " when they started their tour of the streets on Saturday. Owing to their inde- fatigable efforts and the generosity of the townsfolk the ' scheme now nears slowly but sorely its consum- mation. Previous to the parade the sum for the Cottage Hospital in the hands of the treasurer was £1.209 15s 91. This amount consisted of the following sums i—Anaual Cycles Parades. £414 163 91 ; Sir J V Brunner. Bart, M.P., £200; S Taytor, , Esq., LP., £lOO ; H Cougreve,Eaq.,C.E., £lOO ; High- field Tannery Ce., £lOO ; Runcorn Cc-operative Society Ltd., £lOO '• F Norman,E q., J. P., £5O ;R It Posnett, Esq., J. P., £5O; Rev Canon AM M.A., £5O; AR Norman Esq , .1.1'.. £2O; F A Lake, Esq.. £10; ' the Late Dr Steele, £lO ; Miss H C Bickhetn, £5. 0 Saturday there was about £5O collected which with £5O given by the Runcorn Gas Company makes a strand total towards laying the foundation stone of £l3OO in round figures. This year the committee in its wisdom decided to hold the carnival on Saturday which procedure had its advantages and disadvant- age. There was, of course, considerably more loose cash in the hands of the workingmen but the dsy prevented many tradesmen from lending the helping heed which under more favourable circumstances they would have done. Fortunately, however, the fickle weather was in one of its happiset moods. Though somewhat chilly the tem- perature was by no means unpleasant and at the time appointed for starting the thoroughfares were crowded with dense masses of people. Prompt to time the par ade started hem Wilson's Hotel throwing in its front cloud of skirmishers armed with moaey-30X09 to captor if possible the nimele " browns." rusts were ne less than six section. under the resp.ctive oemmends of Messrs J Mort, J F Edwards, S D Matthews, D Edwards, J J Grittens,and J Rushten,Mr. Booth being chief mershal and timist. Previous to toe parade,however,the cyclists and ether competitors met in Wilson's Yard, where their merits and demerits were adju limited up on by Messrs C. Hughes, and A. E. Wright, Liverpool, cycle judges, and Messrs J. W. Bate, M.R.C.V.S., G. B. Yarwood and U. Wade, Sutton Weaver, horse judges tie winners being announced at a later portion of the proceedings. Toe route fatten was as follows s—Bridee-street. Church-street, L rrd- street, Ashridgeeureet, Eeertort-etreet, Waterloo-soa 1, Station-road, Greenway-road, Victoria-toed, Mill Brow, Eielton-road, Dolph Bridge and Bridge-street. It is questionable whether the turn out equalled pre- vious years but comparatively few cyclists Lurning our. Thera was, however, quality if uot quantity and the majority of the costumes were decidedly picturesque. Perhaps the two best groups were the contingents from Crewe—the Morris dancers and the Aaimese troupe. The former delighted the spectators by their evolutions, their dancing being something decidedly novel to the Runcorn public. They consisted of s—Captaia Billing. ton, Messrs Young, Hope, Featherston. Grosvenor, Fieee, 6 Patin, Wilkinson, Haynes, Parrett, Taylor, HamiPoo, Barden, Woolrich, Ashley, Barlow, and Lydiart. Dressed in brilliant cestusnes,they steeled tee procession followed by the members of the Bugle Band 2nd (E.G.) V.B. Cheshire Regiment to whose lively strains they kept up an intermittent dancing. Toe lady cyclists were on the whole fairly well represented. Mrs Clarke. Man- chester, took the part of a foreign fruit girl with con- siderable effect and looked very well in her drapery of grapes and other fruit, Derothy Herrisoa wheeled a dainty little hike trimmed with pretty flowers, and Miss Harriet Hunt's cycle al so looked neat trimmed with flowers and Chinese lanterns. Miss A Peacock had gone Ito considerable trouble in decorating tier minim, with ivy and very nice it looked with the few flowers which relieved the dull green. The lodien c nef "Sitting Bull " was gorgeously arrayed in hie Indian robes and the machine was also attired with trophies of the chase. Mr J Strew, tie chief, c settialy deserves soave- thine more than a passing word of praise for his good taste. Toe royal fusiher was splendidly represeated in the person of Alexander Steele who looked in his martial dress very much a hero. roe most grotesque novelty of the show was the character of the sheemaker portrayed with e ludicroes fidelity by M. S. Hayes, Manchester. He carried two huge boots andlhis machine held a splen did little assortment of the implements of his trales Mr. W Hayes wee again to the front with his cat and dog show which were trundled on two old fashioned machines and were artistically ensconced in a be.' of roses and other fl owers. The menagerie was a distinc- tive feature of the show. Toe colonies were well represented by Mr. F Hindley ;Ireland), .H Nield (West Canadian) Joseph Green (luatralie). J Taylor (Ffindoo), W H Harrison (neero), and Themie Crawford (John B ill). Toe St. Johns Ambulance were well represented. tee officials present heing Meesrs W Bank's (treasure,), and W Gregory (secretary). Tile tableau represented the setting of frae.ured limos, and was as instrue,tive as it was entertaining Tee 2ad V.C.8.R., Ceding Corps ware in charge of Corporal ' Green, and consisted of IF sr' 4 T Mitchiesen, P Miles. 11 Jones, W H Ferrington, B Wrignt, J Ingleefield, J Guy, J Jones, and S Line The Runcorn le Airs Sec- Von of Blew Hungry Band, nrstaniz ad by Mr W El Holt, ineluded Messrs J Davies, J Bzev, J Lunt, yv Lean. W Howard. J Sefton, S Worrell, G parr, T Fseteher, E Honley, J Mooree. A Hughes, J P ckstock, R Et alliiridge, El Wilkinson W llyfield, T S .eele, J Buckley, T Brown, M Hodson. 'This combination was one of the moat grottrque sue laughable features of the parade and , created a vast amount of merriment at every stage of the route. The fire brigade as usual were well to the front having a manual engine and a re. I cart in charge ,of Superintendent Wright and Deputy Superintendent Cousins. One of the most extraordinary features of the show was the group of Allittlo9o dancers. These gen- tlemen are well-known prize winners for their turn out and represented is tribe of Burmese Their costumes were entirely composed of wheaten straw, and t ley had the appearance of small conical hay stacks rather than human beinps. Ties • c Alt anions were frightfully realistic, and doubtless their mimicking was all but perfect. Their names were Messrs J Evans (in command). T Ansel?, W Chesworth, W Bewker, E Bamford. Griffitns, T Marshall, S Harris, W Harri- son, J Elliott, S Pnillips, and T Pace. The ambulance in charge of Mr George Widths, conveyed a khaki clad hero (Mr D Tomlinson). who was attended by Mary Riding, Emma Savage, Ethel Antrobus, and Elea Dunbebin. There were also a number of Board scnoel children dressed in khaki under Mr Brown and very soldierlike they leoked. There were a number of tradesmen's cx- hibite but there was neither the number nor quality of last year. The followine hende headed the different sections :--The Fredshaen Volunteer Band, the Weston Point Salt Works Band, St E ward'a Brass Band and the Preston Brook Band. It should be mentiened that the Morris dancers performed at Mr s Taylot's and Mr R a Postnetee. 'rhe tour of the town finished at Wilsou'e Hotel the parade being its many respects highly suc- cessful.</p>
        <p>CONCERT AND DISTRIBUTION OF PRIZES. In the evening a concert was held in the Puuuc Hall, at which the prizes were dist Tibuted. Thera was a good attendance and a capital programme was Fsub- mitted. Mr R El Posnett presided, and he was upported by Mess's F J Nu:nal. AR NJradan, S Waltio. J. M. Frith, M J Sweetman, and others. D aring an interval in the programme The Chairman said he had had the honour of presiding over the Cottage Hospital Committee this year, and he wanted to say how deeply they appreciated—and he might say the town appreciated—the noble way in which tie Cycling (lob had helped forward this hospital project. (Ap- plause.) He did not believe they would have had a ' hospital anywhere within sight at all if it had not been for the energy and push of the Cyclme Club. and when the hospital was built, as he hoped it would be shorly, It would b-s a noble monument to a noble cause,worked out by noble men. (Applause.) The efforts of the club had been backed up by donations from gentle- men anxious to help forward the movement, and when in the near future the scheme reached ira consumma- tion it would be of inestimable benefit to the town, and particularly to the working classes. The Chairman then read over the list of subscriptions (already mentioned above) which appeared in the official programme of the parade. Since that list was got oat Mr J R Frith had intimased to him that he might put down the Runcorn Gas Company forl£so towards the Hospital Fund. Mr Frith also said that when the question of maintenance came forward he thought he would be able to get his board of directors to contribute LIO per annum towards the maintenance expenses. (Arplause,l They must understand that when they had got the hospital they could not keep it for nothing unless they got someone to endow it. The Manchester Ship Canal Company had promised £.50 for some years towards the cost of maintenance. They might say now that in round figures they had about £1,300 promised and in the bank, due in a large measure to the efforts of the Cycling Club: (Applause.) The real reason for the delay in carrying forward the scheme to completion was that they had b ten unable as yet to get anyone to pre- sent them with a site. If any present had an acre, or failing that half-in-acre of land to give away they would be glad if they would communicate with the com- mittee and hand over the deeds as soon as possible. (Applause.) They would like to be in possession of a site before they made any urgent appeal for funds. First of all they thought they would like to have a por- tion of the hi ath, but they were told that it could not be built upon, as it belonged to the inhabitants of Runcorn for all time. It was considered that the hospi- tal should be placed as high as possible so that patients should have the benefit of pure fresh air. lie (the chairman) was very hopeful that the scheme would be carried through. Mr James Wilding,whose kindness and generosity ware unfailing in any movement for the good of Runcorn, had taken infinite pains in the t reparation of plans and in going to other places to inspect Cottage 1 Hospitals and find out what would he the best for Run- corn. They awed him a debt of 'gratitude. The plans he had prepared showed an estimated cost of £1,200, so that they had abort £lOO in hand to do the furnishinp. If they could get land for, say, £4OO, £5OO, or £6OO, they Would do well. He believed they would be able to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>get it for a reasonfble price and thus carry the scheme forward to completion. (Applause.) Mrs F J Norman thea distributei the prizes as per list given below, and On the motion of Mr Sweetman, seconded by Mr Walton, a hearty vote of thankt was accorded to her</p>
        <p>_ for her presence ad support. The Ctiairinan announced that the cup on the table had been presented by Mr F J Norman. Mr S Taylor. and another poor man—(laughter)—lor competition, at a bend contest to be held in a few week6' time. Applaus</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>PRIZE LIST.</head>
        <p>PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>Ladies' ne)test costume.-1. Mice Harriet Hunt (gipsy), 10. Suffolk Street ;2, Miss Peacock, 34, Trentham Street (olden style). Beet decorated maehing. —l. Miss Dorothy Harrison. L. and N.-W. Hotel. (llwer girl) ;2. Sirs Clarke, 1, Beason Street, Bradford, Manchester (foreign lrult girl). Gent's beat dressed machine and rider.-1, Alexander Steele. Runcorn Cycling Club (Gordon Highlander; 2, W Ray. Runcorn Cycling Club (summer). Most original costume.-1. James Shaw, 13, Bertha Street, Openshaw, Manchester (Sitting Bull). Most comical costume.-1, 8 Hayes, 15, Duke-street, Miles Platting (cobbler). Special prize for best muster military cyclists (value 10s.) —2n.l VBCR Cycling Corps. Horse in best condition without h.rness —l, Runcorn Urban District COU'leit . Messrs H Timmins and Sons. Horse and turnout.-1, Runcorn Co-Jpsrative Scree y ; 2, T Lea.</p>
        <p>Pony. without W Graham. Pony turnout.-1. H H Pomitt. Consolation prizts were awarded to B Monks and G Whitby. Boat horse with gems.-1. Henry Spencer, 33,Shtw-street. Programme sellera' prizas.-1, Lecoy ; 2, Cox. Beat collection in publl3-house.-1, Lloyd. Devonshire Hotel. This gentleman handed back ths prize (51.) to the Hospital fund. A special min' of a silver cup, value 258., was awarded to the Morris dtricsre, while the troupe of Anainese received a ctse of ctrvers. The following are particulars of collections with poles : Messrs A Povey (Runc trn Harriers), £l. 13s Zd ; Leyland el. OS 2d ; Hadfield. 17s 41; Hamilton, 4s SA ; Kilgariff, 33 9d ; Hulse, 2s 7d ; Wainwrigit, 2s 20. -</p>
        <p>The actual amounts collontel on Sttar4a7 are as follows .--oolleotod with box ,s aril poles in the street £36 2i 41, takings at the public hall door £7 124 Bi, to whin there If further artrm ,C 4 to be added hringing the amooot to £9 5491 ; the big lo f draw, £5 173 81 ; Total, £59 18; 51. There are other items to corns in which win be announced in due course.</p>
