      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CoRaESPONDENT.I</p>
        <p>Toe School Board election is now the sole topic of conversation among the ratepayers. For the next four weeks or so the man in the street will be sur- feited with alternate doses of Board and Voluntary systems. Generally speaking, the education ques- tivn en,gages the attention of the average working man once in every three years' and then it is viewed through the filmy spectacles of X s d. As a rule,the elector at this period has a most uncomfortable time of it. He is persistently bothered by a small army of canvassers,and even the sacred precincts of his house are not free from their intrusion. They buzz about like flies an a sunshiny day, worrying one and sometimes stinging The elector should notlhowever. be alarmed. They onli, wish to al- Vance his views on education and really mean well Still, canvassing is by no means an unmixed bless- ing, and it is a great pity that it cannot be swept away once and for all.</p>
        <p>A word to the wise is sufficient: The election is one of the most monastatous that has ever taken place in the history of the town. ',ls essential that the very best men representing the very beet system shall fill the chairs of our local authority. It s almoit a matter of indifference what kind of a man you send to represent the interests of the con r,ituency in Parliament, for, as a rule, he can do littlaor nothing towards guiding the local destinies of the town. His work lies chiefly in dealing with Imperial affairs and acting his little part in the great principles involved. In a local election where local intere-ts are at stake this is entirely different. It is the local authority who determines your rates, who give you a park, who erect your baths, who switch on the current of electricity, instead of gas, who lsok after the health of the town, improve your streets, form your libraries, and control the education of your children. And the latter consideration is by no means the most trifling one. The importance then of selecting suitable men t 3 represent the interests of the elaoLorate cart by no means be over-ratei. Thera is no doubt but both systems of education will be placed in their most bewitching guise but it is for us to mark learn and inwardly digest our duty to cut away the sophistries ;which so often mar party controversy and view the broad principles at stake dispassionately. Then we shall come to a just decision and doubtless send the best men woo represent the best principles to the top of the poll.</p>
        <p>On Monday the mee!:ing in favour of the Pro- gressives was a moss enthusiastic one. The Chairman.Mr FJ Norman,wound up a most spirited speech with the query " Why are we so behind. educationally other countries." Toe Rev J Hirst Flo Howell answered the question in :a lon4 biz- tory of the education problem. He laid the onus entirely on the shoulders of the clerical party and charged them with being directly responsible for En2land's backwardness. It will b 3 for the volun- taryists to answer this serious charge whicl if they cannot explain it away will seriously menace their chances of success on the polling day.</p>
        <p>In reference to the expenses of the Runcorn vol- untary schools we refer our-readers to some in- teresting figures pabli-hei elsewhere unde- the title of" Te.l hilance sheets of the Runcorn Volun- tary Schools."</p>
        <p>The Runcorn cycle parade is now a thing of the past for at least another twelve months, One cannot help being struck at the lak of enthu,dasna among local wheelers. In support of such a worthy object as the founding of a 03ttage hospital it would have been surmised that every 'cyclist would have been only too eager to turn out on parade day It was, however, appaient, as one younz lady whispered to another as the procession moved along that it was a cycle-parade with the cyclists left out The few present were, however, capitally attired and to a certain extent made up for the defl3iency, Next year, time and circumstances permitting it is to be hoped that all local cyclist :will gladden the heart of the secretary, Mr Booth, by putting their shoulders to the wheel to help the good cause forward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>In the County Championship on Saturday Ches- hire created a great surprise by making a draw. On paper Lancashire appeared co have an easy thing on, but as an actual fact they were very lucky to make a draw. As Runcorn supplied two thirds of the Cheshire team, much of the credit is due to the Greens who if I may venture to make a Prediction will not be far from carrying off the Northern Leave Championship when the seasma , has closed.</p>
        <p>A Cheshire enthu4ast in the crowd caused some amusement by repeatedly calling out " Play up, Ches-aar," and then alleginz loudly that the game was "Runcorn versus England, Ireland. Scotland and Wales:' In this he was not far out, for whilst there were ten local Runcorn men in the Cheshire team, the Lancashire team included four Welsh- men, one Irishman, and at least oae Scot. Those who have a peneant for home industries will ad- mire the Cheshire team, particularly when they know that Fish, wh) scored the two tries for ban- caihire, is a Cdeshire born youth, hailing from Los took Graham</p>
        <p>--- Houghton was the hero of the match. He added to his already great reputation by playing one of his best games an I some of his work was cheered with universal enthusiasm. " Old Ebor " says, " Houghton was again an animated lump of solid rock, against which tie Lancashire backs dashed themselves in vain."</p>
        <p>Unfortunately Richardson somewhat severely injured his shoulder, but he pluckily played up to the end of the game, and suffering as be was he scored a brilliant try. It at all possible he will turn m i out to-morrow against Huddersfield. Faulkner s also. expected to make a reappearance, and he will receive a hearty welcome from one and all. He i counts his admirers by the thousands, lot there s no more clever and unassuming player than Jack.</p>
        <p>--- A rather strange thing has occurred. Moran has been lost since the middle of last week. He has taken his departure from the town and whither no man knoweth. Perhaps he is having a brief holiday on the quiet. Unless he has turned up since these lines were penned the working clause will effectu- ally stop his playing on Saturday,</p>
        <p>TO CURE A COLD IN ON S DAY. Take Laxative Brom° Quinine Tablets. All Druggists refund the money if it fails to cure. Is. lid. The genuine is stamped L.B.Q.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>NOTES ON UAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON UAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>With the choice of two matches before me last Saturday I finally decided that the one at Broughton was the handiest to get to and would probably provide as good a game as the other. To Broughton, therefore, went, and saw the despised Cestrians, who were last season beaten by 30 points to nil, play a better game than the chosen of Lancashire. The result of the match was a draw of 8 points each, but really the visiting side should have won for they had mud' the better of the play.</p>
        <p>Most of the blame for the poor show made by Lanca- shire is to be laid on the forwards, though the backs were a long way below par. But the scrimmagers were the laziest lot I ever saw. They lacked nothing in weight or ability ; all they wanted was a spice of [the determination of their foes. In- stead they allowed themselves to be pushed off the ball and generally they gave a display which was a disgrace to aily County team. Possibly they thought ' Cheshire rather small fry, though it is more likely that I most of them would have preferred doing duty for their own clubs rather than assist the County. There was a lamentable lack of esprit de corps and it was only luck and John Fish combined that prevented the County Palatine getting the severe drubbing it de- served.</p>
        <p>There was a crowd of at least ten thousand and they did their best by encouraging shouts to instil some life into the Lancashire men. 'Play up Lancashire" in a remonstrative tone was the cry, varied occasionally with "Play up you lazy—," the blank here being conspicuously well filled at Broughton. It was no use however Lancashire either couldn't or wouldn't, and their ardent supporters had to be content with half the spoils and only a small percentage of the honours.</p>
        <p>It was a big climb down, not one in a thousand en- tertained the possibility of Cheshire being anything but soundly whacked, and the first ten minutes went far to confirm this impression. The Lancashire men handled the ball with some smartness when they got hold of it and Fish receiving from Valentine at centre, outpaced the opposition and grounded near the posts, W. James placing a goal. This seemed a foretaste of what was to follow but it was not. The Cheshire men rallied and forcing Lancashire back with desperate valour soon got on level terms, Butterworth scoring a try and Farmer landing a goal. Even then the Palatinates did not appear to take warning and before the interval the Cestrians got ahead, Richardson scoring an unim- proved try in very clever fashion.</p>
        <p>Lancashire bestirred themselves to some purpose on resumption, but found a resolute defence opposed to them. Fish tried to get through "on his own " but failed, and S. Williams on the opposite side missed several openings in the most unaccountable fashion. Time fled and there was some Lnxiety in Lancashire breasts when Fish took a pass from Valentine at the quarter flag and made for the line. Speed was the main factor, and Fish proved that he had a sufficiency of this commodity by crossing the line a few yards ahead of everybody else. The cheering which greeted the Warringtonian was tremendous, and W. James got an extra shout for the fine attempt at goal he made.</p>
        <p>With the teams on even terms again the excitement increased. Cheshire might still have won but for an al- teration in tactics. They brought an extra man out of the s2rummage and went in for defensive play and spoiling tactics. Punting was resorted to, and none can play this game more effectively than the Cheshire men, but it does not bring points. Lancashire were hampered thereby and the style of play deteriorated, so that no one was sorry when the final came with the score 8 points all. Had Houghton and his men stuck to their original game I firmly believe they would have won if ' only by the advantage they possessed forward, but they appeared to think discretion the better part of ' valour when Lancashire got on even terms in the second half. Anyhow they went very near accom- plishing a great performance and credit is due to them for obtaining what was certainly a moral victory.</p>
        <p>Every man on the Cheshire side played his best, the i pick of the bunch being Houghton and Jolley. The ' ex-international played a full-back game of remarkable . excellence even for him, while Jolley worked as only be knows how. The team was well-balanced, and if the backs as a whole were less brilliant than the Lancas- trians there was a much better understanding among them. Forward the visitors were head and shoulders above the home side, and the eight Cheshire put in the field were the quickest and keenest lot I have seen any- , where. They carried the scrums and descended like an avalanche on the Lancashire backs when there was any following up to be done. Dan Smith has not had such a gruelling for some time I'll warrant. He was a long way below the standard set up by Houghton and quite disappointed his admirers. 'Wilson and Valentine were little better at centre, both playing selfishly and fumbling passes. Fish was the best back on the Lanca- shire side undoubtedly and Lancashire would have been in sorry plight without him. The halves did excellent individual work at times but they did not combine in the most desirable manner. That there will be changes in the Lancashire team for the next County match is a foregone conclusion.</p>
        <p>Warringtonians were delighted that Fish should score both tries for Lancashire, and Runcornians felt as much satisfaction when the 8 points on the other side all fell to the prowess of their representatives.</p>
        <p>Warrington at Flunslet found their masters, and were defeated by 7 points to nil. A score which really does not indicate the bulk of the play which the winners had. They simply overwhelmed Warrington in the scrummages, and even with Fish on it is very doubfful if the result would have been different. Good judges who were present tell me that they never saw the War- rington forwards so badly beaten both by weight and cleverness. The backs in consequence were practically helpless and had to defend continually. No fault could be found with them as the fact that the line was only crossed once amply proves. Hallam played superbly, while Dickenson seemed as good on the wing as he was in centre. The halves worked like Trojans, Allen win- ning high praise for -his dogged perseverance. On the home side the two Goldthorpe's were the prime movers in every attack. Walter scored all the points while Albert gave the visitors a good few wrinkles in tact,cs, as Hallam will be able to testify.</p>
        <p>This defeat and the causes of it emphasise once more the lack of weight in the Warrington forwards: They I are a good lot when they meet an eight some what shnilar,but against the Yorkshire scrimmagers t'Aey seem ! h elpless. On wet days in particular this will lose the. club many a point. More weight is absolutely neces- sary and it is no good waiting till the club gets low down in the table before the fault is remedied. Let us have some evidence of the committee's activity now and not at the end of the season. 'Tis a shame that, the abilities of the backs should be wasted owing to weakness forward.</p>
        <p>To-day's match at Wilderspool with Brighouse Ran- gers should provide Warrington with an easy victory, for the Rangers are at the .bottoin of the League and have had 115 points scored against them already.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team will be as follows :—Hallam, full-back ; Fish, Isherwood, Dickenson, and Harris, three-quarters; Allen and Hockenhall, half-backs ; Morrison, Edmondson, Fell, Cunningham, Eden, F. Swift, J. Swift, and Jolley, forwards.</p>
        <p>---- The League table up to date is as follows For Against PNV L D ... 0 T PTS ... G T PTO Broughton Rangers 7 7 0 0 ...13 20-86 ... 4 4-2) _l4 Oldham Hull linnslet Runcorn 1,00 8 4 4 0 ...12 9-61 ... 5 11-43 ... 8 *Warrington Batley *.SvOnton 5 3 2 0 ... 5 7--31 ... 5 3-19 ... 4 *Salford Bradford Halifax 6 1 4 1 ... 5 7-31 —.ll 11-55 ... 3 Ructirrafiel.i 191125mise Rangers *ealford. Swinton, and Warrington have each had two points deducted for breach of the rules.</p>
        <p>Footballers are in great request in Runcorn as tenants , of public houses. Ex-footballers and present-day players of the Runcorn Club occupy half-a-dozen licensed houses,and the number was increased the other day when the magistrates granted Wilkinson, three- quarter for Runcorn and Cheshire County, authority to sell at the Village Inn, Halton.</p>
        <p>In the course of a week or two E T Jones, the win I threequarter' who played for Warrington last season but one, will be back in the town. He is in better health than he was when last over here, and should make a valuable reserve man. Jones played some good games, notably against Hull, when L Smith was his centre. By the way it is said that Smith has gone to the front, which, most people will agree, is the best place for him.</p>
        <p>I see Salford are playing a wing three-quarter weigh- ng 15 stone in the ir match with Batley at New Barns on Saturday. The new corner is named John Hayton and he hails from Cumberland where six-footers are not so uncommon as they are here. He ought to make some impression on the Cup-holder's defence if he gets near enough to the line.</p>
