      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>AN IMPUDENT BICYCLE. Their—" LIE AFTER LIE. At the Warrington Police Court on Tuesday, before Ir Birchall (presiding) and °trier magistrates, a young man named WJLiain Basford, of Me,sington-road, Birkenhead, was charged with stealing a bicycle value £5, the property of Charles Jones, bicycle dealer, Liver- pool—Superintendent Monaghan stated that on Satur- day afternoon prisoner went into the shop of the pro- secutor at Liverpool and hired a bicycle for an hour. giving the name of J Davies, Walton- lane, Liverpool. He did not return the machine and nothise happened until Monday when prisoner went round to several bicycle dealers and offer- ed the machine for eel Amongst others he went G• the Shop of Mr Ashton,Caurch-atreet, Warrington and asked £.3 for the bioycle, stating that it was his own. He also said he WAR a clerk at the White Lead Works, Sankey Bridges and lived in Piumpton-atreet. Sling Fuvichus Mr Ashton questiosed him, and prisoner said he would take 283. for the machine. Mr Ashton declined to buy it, and a constable, who had received some inf Irma- tion came on the scene and questfo ied the prisoner. The matter was reported to him (the Superintendent) and he went out dind saw the prisoner standing at Mr Ashton' shop-door. He invited him to come into the Police Station and the man did so. He (the Superintendent) qu,stionert him for an hour and the man told him lie after lie. He said he had lived in Plurnptol-3treet a week, that he had served at the front for seven months with the Grenadier Guards, and had had toe bicycle thirteen months. He (Superintendent Monaghan) telentor ned to Sankey Bridges and found prisoner had ott tined fool and lodg- ings for nothing in Plumptor-itreet from the previous day. It appeared that Lb-. bicycle was a light coloured one, and on Monday morning prisener got some enamel and blacked it over.—Prisoner now pleaded gullty and was sentenced to a month's bard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>MINOR OASES. Warrington —l3—c;:o—ugh Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES. Warrington —l3—c;:o—ugh Court.</p>
        <p>MoNDAT.—Before Mr. Monks (in the chsl.), 614e•man Heston, Councillor Smethurst, and Messrs Birchtll,</p>
        <p>_ Wright, and Thornton. FIGHTLYG —For committing a breach of 07. e peace by !Ming at Atherton's Quay on the 19th October Benjamin Fisher, 33. Hesketh-street, and Frederick Banner, 125. Plumpton-street, were bound over to keep the peace in the sum of £5 and ordered to pay the cots. GANIBLING.—WiIItam Richa-dson,32, Hardock-street. and William Bradshaw. 6, Chapel.street. were charged with Play- ing at banker on Sunday, the 13th Inst. -P C, Turaer proved tte easel and defendants were fined 5s and casts each. DISORDERLY BOYS.-I,trne4 Bradshaw, 329, Winwick-road ; Henry Bretherton. 347, Winwiok-road : and Joseph Cald- well, 152. Winwick-road. were charged with being disorderly in VVinwick-road on the 2)th Inst.—P C, Lewis prave 1 the case, and said defendants were shouting and jostling foot- passeng, rs about.—The defendants were ordered to pay the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MONDlT.—Before Mr W Timmins (in the Chair) and Mr 11 H Posnett.</p>
        <p>TEMPoRARY AUTIZORITY7-I-T-h-O-rnas Wilkinson applied for temporary authority to sell a'. a beerhouse, " The Village Inn." Halton.—Granted. John Herhert Andrews was granted temporary authority to sell at" The Bird in Hand," Halton-road. 6. A DIRTY AND FiI,TRY Hot/SR."— 'slaty Clarke, Bridge water-street. was summoned at the instance of the N.S.P.C.C. for neglecting. her six children on the 17th October and divem other dates daring the last six months, in such a manner as to cause them unnecessary suffering.— Mr Knowles. solicitor. appeared for the N.S P.C.C. and said the woman Was a dirty, lazy trollop who neglected her children and.kept the home in a disgraceful condition —John Moulton. echo d mister. at be Victoria-road Board School said he had known the woman fora couple of years. One of her children still went to school and one went until recently. He had had to send them home frequently on account of their dirty habits and the vermin upon them. He had visited the home on several occasions, Once he was in fear and trembling est he had contracted some disease owing to the stench there was in the !loose. Refuse was lying on the floor.— Thomas Bowyer, school attendance officer, said he had visited the house and he corroborated the previous witness's staternent.—Sergeant Grerory deposed vieting the house in company with Inspector Hackett on the 17th inst. Down- stairs in one room were three girls undressed. He had visited them in Canal-street and Surrey-street and the house was in a disgraceful condition. In the whole of his experi- ence he had never seen a wor.e case of dirt. In the room where the children slept they had only some straw,* biageand /I sheet to cover them. The husband was a respectable man and earned at least El a week.—lnsptor Hackett 7,7.5.P.C.C., said he had visited the house twenty time. during the present year. He had put the case some time ago in the hands of Miss Hawkinson anc" Mrs Wood, ° wife of Canon Wood. He had also got the district nurse to attend the house, but they had found it was impos- sible to get prisoner to reform Upstairs the children had to dive into straw when they wanted to sleep He had visited the house at all honrs and Invariably found the woman sitting by the fire with two or three naked children round her. There was an older girl of 17 who had been greatly neglected. He had off -red to take her to hie own home. She mine for a short time and then went home. She was absolutely ignorant and ate her meals like a cat. The defen- dant did not drink and the filthy state of the house was due entirely to negligence. The children reeked with vermin, —sergeant Brwster corroborated and said that the Inspector had not sufficiently painted the ease. He visited the house and the stench was so terrible that he was really frightened,—William Clarke, husband of the deiendant, said there was not a word of truth in any of the previous witnesaes° statements. He did not teach the children to use bad langeage —The Chairman said that the case was a very bad one. The chrdren had been dressed respectebly that morning for a purpose. A most serious offence had been committed but they would take a lenient view and bind the defendant over for 6 month. in sureties of £s.—The Inspector and the police would be requested to make period_ lee visits. POAcTIING AT &amp;Tv/o/c—Charles Stubbs and Herbert Parkinson were summoned for poaching.—Stubbs did not appear,—Mr Lake appeared to prosecute.—James Storey, gamekeeper to Mr Henry Greenall Paid that on the lffila inst he saw some traps and wires on the Sutton estate. He watched them for some time and then sent his assistant to vratch.—Prederick Bathes, assistant gamekeeper. said that he watched by the trap and wires. After waiting for some hours he saw Parkinson and Stubbs and heard them talking about rabbits. One of them nickel mop a rabbit. Witness was biding in a cover and as mom as he showed himself they ran away. One of them lost a cap and the other he gave chase to. He caught up to him and he struck witness In the face. He let him go and followed him down to the Weaver Canal. Stubbs then pulled out a revolver and said he woul.l blow his brains out.—Herbert Patrick Nicholas, secretary to the Runcorn Hone Co • said that Parkinson was on the torviog path and salt that he had lost his cap and that he and `z tobbs had some rabbits to divide.—Parkinson</p>
        <p>-was fined 20s and costs or 14 day, ALLEGED ASSAULC.—sIa,v Griffiths Weston Point WWI summoned by Annie Done for having assaulted her on the 14th inst.—Complainant .tated that on the date in question the deenclant struck her a blow on the arm with a tin can after calling her several foul names.—James Shaw cornbor- -ated.—Defendant swore that she never struck complainant. —Ann, Pickering eoroborated.—Bound over for six months -with 40s and the costs 115. 6d. or six months. OWCFNE LANGUAGE—George Worrall was fined 101 and costs or 14 days tor using obscene language in Wellington- -street.—Defendant elected to go to gaol.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD ELECTION. ---- Speech by the Rev J Hirst, Hollowell.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD ELECTION. ---- Speech by the Rev J Hirst, Hollowell.</p>
        <p>On Monda7 evening the progressive candidates opened their campaign by holding a public meeting in Bruns- wick Sehoo.roone. There was a crowded audience and the meeting was most enthusiastic. Mr F J N orman ()monied the chair. aud he was supported by the Rev W A E,,re, Rev H Morrison, Messrs R H Posnett, W Handley, G Bellamy, J M Frith, A R Norman, J W Pritchard, Wilson, and J Frith. The Chairman said that those who had read the Duke of Devonshire's speech at Liverpool on Saturday would have noticed that the Government was waiting for a breezo to set them going. He knew of nothing more calculated to raise the wind in the locality than a School Board election. (Applause.) If what his Lerdehip said was correct every member should be shaken for resign- ing the interests of the penplejin the question of educa- tion. Deanne with the difficulties that oeset the Govern- ment he said that there was a large party strongly in favour of education alone. He (the speake s) would promise not only to raise the wind in Runcorn but would promise him five candidates as well whose only watchword would be education and education alone. (Bear, hear.) 119 would deal with tbe question on broad lines as he would have other opportunities of addressing them. If they wanted to lay the foundation of a superstrucruee which would never be converted they should get five educational candidates in charge of 'he Runcorn School Bard. (Applause.) They were far b'- hind the other European nations in their educstional system They went forward into the new century with the foreigner who had the light elastic step of the mentally equipped, while the Britieber was going forward with the dull heavy tread of the half educated. In the Army ' or Navy this would be regarded as an acute crises. There wits not a more serious cu'look and it had to be faced. They had to carry the burden of the greatest- Emoire the world had ever seen. A single mistake might wreck that Empire and shatter their commercial supremacy. Where was the disturbing element in con- nection with educational matters ? It was not money, indifference tO human nature, indifference toward edu- cation or lock of isoserest. He knew what the cause was end If Mr eloilewell were not there he would give it. He propounded the question " How do you account for the fact that the Britisher is in the forefront in con- nection vita everything else and so woeful y behind in educational matters. That was the question and he asked him te answer it. (Applause.) The Rev. J. Hirst Hollowell, who was received with loud applaese, said he would do his beat to answer the question. With the exception of religious equality, education was perhans Cue only thing they were back- ward in. Perhaps teat slightly accounted for their backwardness in education. They were behind Switzerland, France, Germany, arid Denmark. and they had the word of an oracle like Sir John G lest that the peasant of Elel and was not so well educat od as the peasantry of butter-flaking Denmark. He felt ashamed when told that the England of Shakespeare and Milton were behind Denmark. (App'ausee.) A good deal of the technical instruction of the country was not availed of because the elementary preparation of many of their seholare had been so detective. The composition of the letters people wrote suffilierty proved this. County Council after County Council had confe,sed it. They were not behind in cricket and football and they should not be in education. E location was net buil line, a bridge a breakwater or a triumph of force. It was the gigantic task of mental development. They required finance and lone' power lithe community were to be educated equal to Germany and the United S Abs. They bao had a divided nation and the education was back- ward because of ecclesiastical ascendency. That was the reason of their failure. The national object of edeca- tion was to mike boys and girls jot) geed craftsmen and good craftswomen. The eccleeiaetic'e policy was to I make sectarians only Anglicans, Iternanists, Baotiete, I Methodists &amp;e. He would de his best in the Sunday ISchool and Ctourch to do the sauce but no one had any right to use the educational appliances for that end. They ought to have had a national system long before Blucher turned up at Waterloo. In 1837 there was passed through the House of Commons a bill authorising the establishment at local schools controlled and supported by the ratepayers When it reached the gilded chamber they airphyxigted it. Toe Archbishop of Canterbury asked their lordehios to nctice that the bill reduced the authority of the clergy of the Church of England in national education and lowered the control of the perieh priest and handed their functions more and more to the people. He asked them to throw out the bill which was accordingly done. In 1838 Joseph Lancaster stepned into tee breach yet. the c ericals always say that Nonconformists have done nothing fur education, which wee absolutely false. He established "The British and Foreign School Society." He erected classes throughout the country. They were religious and fairly efficient. What fellowed ? The people who had thrown out the bill of 1807 took alarm as there was no mention of the ecclesiastical question. in 1811 the "Church ot E igland Society " was Sounded ra,t merely to complete but to smash and destroy the system of Joseph Lancaster. They had not touched the problem previously. Iu 1639 it was felt that there was need for a better training of teachers for they were not being prepared as they hould be and could nat give efficient training to the &amp;oceans.. The prime minister,Lerd Meltyourne,proposed in the House of CIMGI9O9 to establish a training college tor teachers on an unsectarian basis. Taie wan denounced by the same eccnesiaetics and the Bill was forced to be withdrawn by them. Ie 1861 Mr Lingham as Sir John Gorst new, was the president of the Council of E lucation. Before the celebrated education Act of Me Forster thousands of children were refused admis- sion to any school because their parents were Noncon- formists, and were aenied the elements of education unless they would attend the Parish Church and Seeday School. Mr Lingham asked that the children be ad- mitted to church school whether they were Noncon- formists er not and the reply was an absolute negative. They would not give the Conscience Clause until Mr Gladstone forced them to take it. They then got the dual Act of 1870 which gave them a dual system and under which there was a Board for every district where a school existed. If one part of the system—was right, the other—the Voluntary—was wrong. Yet side by side weee two antag on'etic systems recognised by the state. In 1891 the question was dealt with again by Parliament the result le sin e that where heavy fees were charged they were made lighter and where they were already light, absolutely abolished. Up to 1897 every grant that was issued by Parliament out of the national exellever was paid to Cll soiree's irrespective of creed whether B card or Vol intary. Through the same pervertine infl lance the differen"ial Cr ant was given to the sectarian scnool alone. £700,000 was passed to them and not one penny to ithe Board. They had put a premium on nefficiency. The Progressive party Was the 'erne on party to better education. There were other Progressives wile progressed to the rear. Uader the higher elementary Sehool Board minute of last year they hal given £100,003 to voluntary scheele and refused rants to Blares suffering from financial and geographi- °al aiffisulties. There had been issued lately a minute which required every schel sr to es- turned out of the higher elementary school at his 15 de roar. Not many were turned out hoc %use they only had 7,000 scholars in all the B iard and D 'nomination el sehoets, over fineeen 3. are of age. Yet Massacheaseste had 45,000 echolars in its public schoels over fifteen Y ears of age. Then came the Cockerton iedg,ment and the infamous mand ste issued by Sir Jetin Gorst. The mandate of tee 3l July said that subject may be taught to any age be private authorities earrying on every school but without Parliamentary nrente, and that no grants were to be made to Sehool Boarde for scholars over 14 years of age. A bill had now passed which gave an educational authority in existence over 30 years hub the hands of the County Council. If they returned a progressive majority they would render:a service not onlyjlocalle bu also to a great national cri'es' The education epesti in was worthy of discussion. Tney wanted a public system with no ecclesiastical demonstration in it. Their creed they would leave oetside as they entered the school door. They would fight for that until the death but not with weapons stolen from the ratepayers' pocket. They should see that toe School Bard was managed by people who believed in it not by those who would diminish its usefulness. Every denomination was repre- sented on the Beard. The Government would condone any illegality in the interests of the Sectarian. T :err- fore they should not return a sectarian majority who wool I try to destroy the education of their children. Semedenominatienal fmanagere actually piled up the fees of the Beard scholars in order to fill their own schools. They would be told that Board education was an ex- nensive thing. The population of Cheshire was about 800.001 The average spent in drink was £3 per annum or over £2,000,000 a year. The School Board cost the Country but £36,000. He advised those who criee " Down with the rates" to reply tt Down with them." The expenditure of the Runcorn Valuable, Schoole amounted t £5,000 of this amount but £1.50 was vol- untarily subscribed the rest coming from Imperial source. For the £l5O they not their day saboels in repair, saved the sehonl Beard rate. For every is. they Paid they got 2s. 61. back. He asked them to support Mr. Norman and his colleagues and return a progressive majority, (Applause.) A vote of thanks was Passed to tie lecturer on the ImtMoaition of Mr, R. H. Poe nett and the Rev, W. A. Eyre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
