      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>Lancashire v. Cheshire. ---- The Champions Surprised.</p>
        <p>The Northern Union county championship season was inaugurated at Maache6ter on Saturday afternoon, when Lsocashire bad to defend their title to the pre- miership against a surprisingly vigorous attack from Ceeshire. The match took place on the Broughten Ringers ground, and was attended by fully 10,000 pereone. The L tncastrians were expected to win the contest easily, but so far from this being the caee they had to be content with a drawn game, and were rather fortunate on the run'yof the play to do even that. There was only one change in the selected teams. Daviee,the Swinton half-back, was unable to play for Lancashire. but a capable substitute was forthcoming in Lawton,of Oldham, who last season captained the winners of the Northern Union county championship. Teams:— Cheshire—Sam Houghton (Runcorn), back ; Hodeeon (Stockport), Butterworth and Warder (Runcorn), and Robinson (Stockport), three-quarter backs ; Jol ey and Richardson (Runcorn), half becks ; Walker, Liehtfoot, Farmer, Langley, and Weole (Runcorn), and Oram, Trotter, and Simister (Stockport),forwarde. Lancaehire.—Smith (Salford), back; Fish (Warring- ton), Bob Valentine (Swinton), Wilson (Brouehton Rangers), and Weliams (Oldham), three-qearter backs ; Willie James (Brouehton Rangers) ann Lawton (Old- ham), half backs ; Fereussoo (o'dharn), Tunney (Sal- ford). Treweek s (Barrow), J Wilkinson (Oldham), Rul- dick (Broughton Rangers) Briers (St Helens), Preston (Swinton), and R -Melts (Leigh). f wwards. Referee, ste T H Mirehall (Bradford). Touch jadeee, Messrs J H Houghton (Lancashire) and J Love (Cheshire). There was no wind or bad weather to infl terms the telsy, and as the playlne area of Wheater'e Field is fairly level, no advantage was attendant upon the winning of the rose. Houghtnn and Briers were the rival captains, and the toss being won by the Cheshire veteran, Ruddiek kicked off for Lancashire prompt to time. A good return was made by Hou.gh"on to Wil- son, and Cheshire having won the best of the early kick- ing exchanger, scrummegine foil mei in the Lancashire q 'meets. The champions had a bad time for a few minutes, but the first real break-away by their backs Produced a brilliant try. Tuoney first gave the ball to Valentine, who passed t .Foshoind the Warrington three- quarter out-oaced the opposition and scored near the posts. for W James to kick a val. Cheshire had hard luck just afterwards in Richardson having to retire with a damaged saoulder, which left them a man short in the scrummages, Sirweter havine to be drafted from the pack to the vacated place in the half back line. Richardson. however, was not long absent. While he was away Cheshire played up well, until James and Lawton passed out to Valentine, who was not brought to grass until the Cheshire q sartPrs were reached. Than the Cheshire forwards, wine were playing with great resolution, rushed up the field agair, and Smister, Robinson, and Butterworth hetween them nearly brought about a downfall of the Lancashire goal. This contingency was only d lard a momenlefor efher a bad fle-kick by Smith, the Lancashire back, Butterworth picked up and ran over, Farmer eq rinsing the score with a fine goal shot from near Leticia This rouged Lancashire, who peoneptly made tracks for the visitors' Real. and a drop kick by Smith made Cheshire teach down. Though a man short in the scrummage the visiting forwards were able to secure possession of the ball more frequently than the home side, and Butter- worth pot them in a good position again, but the Lancashire half hacks raiscd the eieee, and from a well julged kick by Fish. Cheshire had again to save. About this time R chardson was able to resume his place, and the team were level in num- bereeaeain. Fealty heldine by Fish got Lancashire into difficulties, from which they oily extricated themselves by Houghton having a kick chareei. Tunney and Ruddick, by short, sharp bursts, made Cheshire uneasy, land Lancashire had a good chance had their passing been less deliberate, as it was the Cheshire backs had no diffieulty in breaking tip the combined attack After- wards Ferguseon got over from a punt out, but the referee was against him. Then Houghton saved by a splendid mark in front; of his own posts, when failure would probably have proved disastrous. Phalle the punt-and--ush game, Cheshire attacked again, and after Jolley had had a kick charged. Richardson pickd up. tricked the opposition by feigning to pass, and thus had a clear run in, The goal sent by Farmer was a failure. Lancashire had another chance before half-eime. hut could make nothing of it, owing to the superior clever- ness of the Cheshire forwards in gaining possession of the ball, and at the interval the champions we-e 3 points behind, the score being—Cheshire 1 gnat and 2 tries (8 points), Lancashire 1 goal and 1 try (5 points). The Play was restarted by Farmer, and a misunder- standing as to wheteer Jolley had made a mark or not enabled that player to dodge away and carry the game ' to the Lancashire corner flag, the attack culminating in a futile drop at goal and a minor point. Th-n Wilson ' out Lancashire on the aggressive,ani Fish nearly do leed t wough. Had the Warrinetonian cen'inued the effort he might have scored, but he passed instead, and the ball went into an oppon ant's hands. Just afterwards Williams was almost in at the right hand corner, Houghton tackling him in time. Accuracy and sting in the Lancashire attack had been unmistakably wanting so far ; indeed the team all round had played very moth unlike a charnpinn side, and there were loud cries from the crowd to " Play up Lancashire." The Red R3so wearers responded, and after Williams had again been baulked on the right by Boughton, Feb got a chance from Valentine on the left, and equalised the score with a fast sprint. The try was got near touch, and James made a most deserving attempt to land a goal, but the bill swerved and passed outside the mete. Some flekey play next led .to Lancashire having to defend, and the home supporters must have felt uneasy when Cheshire had a penalty fairly close in. To their relief, however, Firme,'s atternot to take the lead with the goal shot was a failure. Ten Came an melting long rush of tie Lancashire forwards, the visitors' line having a very narrow escape, for both Lawton and Valentine were nearly over. Ths Williams had a fins chance on the right wing, bet missed Valentine's pass. That Lancashire were not establishing a lead was cer- tainly not due to want of opportunity for well develoned attacks, Smith twice all 'wing himself to be tackle with the ball when he had ample room to get in his kick. Cleshire became aggressive again, and Ric hard- son tried a drop at goal., the shot, however, being much too low. Only four minutes now remained for play. and Cheshire were aetaekine betty. when Jones, encele their chance by ending the ball deal from a punt out. In a twinkling Briers, after Preston had charged a kick dribb'ei to the opno?ites end, and ChPshirs escaped by touching down. Toss was the final incident in a hard- foueht but by no means brilliant match, the result of which was a draw with the score.—La.nc•shire, 1 goal 2 tries (8 points), Cheshire, 1 goal 2 tries (8 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>NORTHERN LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BEATEN AT HIINSLET. Warrington journeyed to Hunsiet on Saturday, and found the Leeds club too strong for them, especially for- ward. In spite of the record-breaking attraction at Beadingley, a crowd numbering quite six thousand spectators was drawn to Parkside to witness the game. The claims of his county prevented Fish accompanying the visitors, who completed their three-quarter line by the inclusion of Ratcliffe, a recruit from the " A " team. Smith, having recovered from the injury he sustained in the openinz snatch against Bradford on September 7th, turned out in the Huosiet third line, to the exclusion rf Horne, and a family bereavement accounted for Lunn's absence. The -flicials and players were as follow : ; Hunslet Wrisht, back ; Hannah, w Goldthorpe, Whiteley, and Smith, three-quarters A E Goldthorpe and Gillings, half banks ; Shooter, Wilson, Glew, Baggott, Wray, Broole, Tanningley, and Ramage, for- wards. Warrington : Hallam back ; Dickenson, Isherwood,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <p>11{E EXAMINER FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 1 1901</p>
        <p>, atcliffe, and Harris three-quarters ; Allen and Hockenhall, half backs ; Fell, M 'niacin, Edmondsoa,F Swift, W Swift, Jolley, Cunningham, and Dakin, for- wards.</p>
        <p>Referee. Mr J Bruckshaw (Stockport). Touch judges, Mr A Brooke (Brighouse), and Mr G Roldsworth (Halifax). Soon atter thestart Hallam made a mistake—his first and only one—in completely missing the ball, and Hunslet swarmed on the line. From this position they tried hard to cross by passiog,but Harris was too atten- tive for Hannah to achieve his object, and a couple of drops by the Hunslet captain went astray. The visitors had a turn at atticking, and twice Hunslet were fortun- ate in averting a score. The first Melt happened when Wright near his own line allowed Harris to charge his kick, but in this and the succ,eding rush over the line Hunslet managed to kick di ad. On the restart Walter Goldthorpe had the hardest luck in not scoring from a drop, but a little later he found consolation in a grand try, in the obtaining of whi :h be cleverly outwitted Harris and Ratcliffe. A E Goldthorpe failed at goal. Wartington pressed towards the interval, but the &amp;- fence was sound, and at half.time Hunslet led by three points to nothing. A minute or F o after the restart W Goldthorpe kicked a tine goal from • mark, a feat which he repeated later on. A Goldthorpe almost scored a try as the result of a rousing run, and altogether the home team made matters very warm for Warringten, who were, however, unfortunate in losing the services of F Swif,: before the close. The last incilent of the after- noon'siplay was a charged kick of Smith's, and if Rat- cliffe had been able to kep his feet he should have scored easily. The result of a fast and interesting game was a clever win for Hunslet by two goals and one try (7 points), to Warrington nil.</p>
        <p>HAYDOCK V HARWEIC</p>
        <p>TRANMERE ROVERS V. NEWT3N-LE-WILL3W.3. COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>These rivals for Comtunacion nanoure met on the Rover&amp; ground at Prenron-park, before about 500 spec- tatore. Final, Rovers, 3 ; Newton, nil.</p>
        <p>ORMSKIRK PARK RECS. V. EARLESTOWN ROVERS. (Liverpool acid District Amateur League). At Ormskirk, Eirlestown scored from a foul in the goalmoutb. The flees. forwards were pitying a good game, White having hard lines with a long shot. Elalf time—Earlestown, 1 ; Ormekirk Park Recs, nil ; and this NVAS the position at the final.</p>
        <p>Haydoek met the leaden; of the Lancashire League, who have not bad a goat scored against them so tar this season. on the Wagon ground, Haydock. Final, Dar- wen. 2 ; Haydock, 1.</p>
        <p>Lancashire League. EARLESTOWN TROUNCE PRESCOT. 'Earlestown were again operating on the Mesnes on saturday, their oppo,ents b log one of the new candi- dates for Lancashire League honours, viz Dreamt. They are Ell rivals of Ealestown, some rare tussles having taken place between the teams under the auspices of the Lancashire Alliance. After the welcome victory over Stalybridge Hovers a fortnight *go, the match was looked forward to by the local supporters with a certain amount of confidence. A new outside left should have turned out for Earlestown but as he failed t 3 put in an appearance, Hayes was again deputed to fill the vacant position. Shortly after the advertised time the teams lined up as follows :—Earlestown won the toss. Fletcher set the rphere in motion on behalf or the visitors, the first move of importance coming from the home right, Ellis eventually running the ball over the line. The I visitors then practically monopolised the play and their efforts were at length rewarded. Ailcock got clear away with the ball and when close in he put a splendid Watt which passed into the net whilst Chew was on the ground, he having slipped in a pool of water. Earles- town livened up after this reverse, and before the interval Haws en made tae scores level again, a pretty centre from Bartley leaving the centre forward with the desired opening. The interval arrived soon after with the seorostanding at one goal each. After the resump- tion there was only one team in it, Preacot acting on the defensive the whole of the forty-five minute.. Goals I were scored by Pennington, [pub, Heyes and Ellis for the hornesters who eventually ran out easy winners by five goals to one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>BROUGHTON R&amp;NGERS V. HUDDERSFIELD.</head>
        <p>BROUGHTON R&amp;NGERS V. HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>This match was played at Fartown, Huddersfield, be- fore a large number of spectators. Huddersfield were short of liswhirst, but the Rangers had to rearrange their back division on account of W James and Wilson having been chosen to play for L mcashire. Hudders- field kicked off, and a fine round of passing between their three-quarters forced the Rangers back to their line. Barrett, jlarry,sad Hogg quickly transferred play to the other end, where ;Howson missed a certain try through failing to gather the ball. Shortly afterwards, however, he again got a pass from Hogg, and scored an unimproved try for the Ranger!. Hogg also scored, and at half time the Rangers led by six points to nothing. On resuming, the Huddersfield forwards rushed close to I the Rangers' line, where Harry stopped their progress with a fine save. Ths Rangers could not force their opponents back, and Deere scored an unimproved try for Huddersfield. Then the Rangers complett lv over- played Huddersfield, and bed matters almost their own way for f the remainderf of the game. !lampoon and S James scored tries, both of which Barrett converted. Final score :—Broughton Rangers, two goals and four tries (sixteen points) ; Huddersfield, one try (three points).</p>
        <p>SALFORD V. HULL.</p>
        <p>About 8.000 spectators assembled on the Hull ground to see the first match between these two clubs. Hull were strongly represented, but Salford had zo find a substitute for Dan Smith, their full back, who was playing or Lancashire against Cheshire. Lomas picked up the ball and dropped a goal for Salford. Then fol- lowed a vigorous attack by Hull. From a fine pasqing run by the Hull backs Hambrecht crossed the Salford line and scored a try, whilst the same player scored again shortly afterwards from a pass by Driscoll. Bme scored • try for Salford, and at half time Hull led by sir points to five. It was in the second half that Hull showed far better form than did Salford. Their backs combined splendidly, and they made many passing runs almost the whole length of the field, but their efforts to score for some time were fruaGrated hp the strong tackling of Lomas, Williams, and Vans., Eventually a try by Townsend wts quickly followed by one from Parkinson, tieith,r of which was improved. Jacques also kicked a penalty goaLland the game ended :—Hull. one goal and flu,' tries (fourteen points) ; Salford one goal and one try (five points).</p>
        <p>. . BRADFORD V. BRIGR)IISF.</p>
        <p>At Bradford, b.fore 5,000 Rpectatlre R.sult :—Brad- ford, four goals and four tries (twenty plies) ; Brig- house, one goal and two tries Jeight points),</p>
        <p>BATLEY V. HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>rhe opening portion of trill ggme at Batley wag very stubbornly contested, there being little to choose in the respective merits of the sides. At the interval the scores were nven—fie points each. krain in the lash por- tion of the game the day was exceedingly keen. Try all they knew, neither side could score, and the result conseqaently was a draw of five points each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Lmort V. Swis.-T3s.</head>
        <p>Lmort V. Swis.-T3s.</p>
        <p>At Leigh. before 5.000 sita.ec-ata-t-o;:.- To the first five minutes Messer kicked past the Leigh backs, and in the exciting race which ensued Hampson reached the b sll first, and, picking up smartly, ran in a lizrand try, EID goal resulting. Eventually Swinton were pmalised, aul Dunbavin kicked an caw goal. Play etas even until is before the interval. when Swinton, after fine pass- ing, looked certain to score, but Messer was hod up on ' the line an Le,gh ...Jeered. At half-time Swinton led by three points to two. Some remarkable football was wit- nessed in the second half. Early on Dunbavin, afrer running the length of the field and evadirag all the Swinton team, scored a sensational try, no goal result- ing. Then Swinton werA dangerous f a time, but were eventually beaten off. lievel minutes from the close a bad foul by a Leigh forward ended in the referee ordering play to the Leigh quarter, and after grand peening Lewis scored an unimproved try. L.igh looked nert4in to lose, but three minutes from the finish Dun- blvifkicked a fine goal from Iva,. halt way, and Lligh won by two goals and a try (seven points) o two tries (six points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>SUIIMARY OF RESULTS. G. T. P. (. T. P. Wizan • Rochdale Hornets 3 521 Altrincham Lancaster Morecambe 3 747 Radcliffe . n o o Widnes 2 4 16 Birkenhead LEAGUE TABLE TO DATE. For. Agst. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P. Widnes .............. 8... 8.. 0... 0...15 21-112... 6 3 21...16 Wigan Birkenhead 7... 5... 1... 1...13 15— 71... 3 1— 9... 9 Barrow _l3 21_ 89 7 5_ 21_ 8 Hell K. Rovers • • 41... 3... 0... 1... 3 5 21... 2 1— 7... 7 Mlllom 6 3.. 2... 1... 8 11— 43... 8 10— 49... 7 St Helens 7. 3 .4. 0... 8 4 23... 7 8— 38 .. 6 TAncastor Rochdale inlets 7... 2... 5... 0... 8 7 37... 5 13— 49... 4 Morecambe 'Radcliffe... *Altrincham *Radcliffe, Altrincliam and Stockport have each had two points deducted for breach of rules.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A " y. FLETCHER RUSSELL'S. TRU; march at Wilderspool on Saturday aroused con- ideratl oterest,and there was a capital attendance of spectators. From the cooamencement the "A " team wer„iscsys the smarter combination, and in the first few minutes L,W10,18 plac.d a capital pe-ialty goal. The Engineers retallated,and good forward work resulted in the pavilion goal being .hotly assailed. Passing by the home backs cauord Fletcher's to defend, and Townson taking a pas; from Bite scored in cl;ver fashion. Later on Townson scored a second try, whilst Hpierofr regis- tered a penalty goal for the visitors. I\Tar the interval s paping movement resulted in Bramhall scoring,and at half time Warring-on lei by eleven points to two. Dur- ing the second half play was of a vigorous nature, and it, was not until near the close that Bran:lt:tali registered a try,iwhich was not imnroved noon. Final : War- rington " A," 3 points; Fletcher Russell's, two points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>LANCAeHIRE JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>LANCAeHIRE JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RIINC FIO.RNETS V. WIDNES WRITE STAR.</p>
        <p>Playedon the Incell-lane enclosure P—ri Saturday afternoon. The White Stir commenced operations aril on a return being made Woods got possession and scored a splendid try. No goal resulted. From the kick-Nit the Hornets got •well into the visitors' 25, and after some grand passmg J Green scored at the corner, the goat-kick again failing. Farmer next dropped a pplendid goal from half-way, and at halftime no score stood Hornets, one goal two tries (eight points) ; White Star, nil. 01 reiurning the Hornets backs agata got going, the bill being passed from man to man with lightning rapidity. Following upon a forward runs Thornton scored try number three for the Horne?. No goal re- sulted. The homo team had the best of matters from now up to the finirh. Joyce added a fourth try, which was augmented by Wood, and the White Star had to retire defeated 0 the tune of 16 points to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>SANKEY GREEN A.F.C., v. MILL-LANE ATHLETIC.</p>
        <p>This match was played on the Mill-lane ground last Saturd ty, In the first half Sankey led by two goals to me but at full time the game en led in a draw of five goals each. SANKEY ALBIONS V. EBASMIC A.F.C. This match was pl.yed at Liverpool-roid in beautiful weather and resulted in an easy win for the Albions by 7 goals to 2. P Johnson (4), W Onions (2), W Walton (1). scored goals for the winners.</p>
        <p>, RUNCORN MARINERS V. HALTON-ROAD WESLEYAN 9. These local teams net on the ground of the latter in fair weather. The visitors ran ouc easy winners by five goals to nil. RUNCORN PARISH RECREATION V. RUNCORN ATHLETIC. Played on the Parish ground on Saturday. After a ow-aided game the Parish ran out easy winners by nine goals to one. The scorers were Meadowczoft, Milward, Sayle, Robinson, and T Hawthorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WARRINGTON V. STALYBRIDGE ROVERS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. STALYBRIDGE ROVERS.</p>
        <p>Played at Scaly bridge on Saturdey,betore wily a sparse attendance. B)th sides were strongly represented. The visitors started uphill. After some opening exchanges !Umber centred finely to Brook., who scored. From a splendid centre by Holland, Bamber had an open goal, and missed the bill. Copk, the one-armed custodian, cleared. With a splendid opportunity for scoring Brooks lost his head, and almost fell over the ball. Lynes, the Warrington centre, was going splendidly when Lawson upset him. A free kick just outside the penalty line folio we I, but nothing came of it. Holland sent a beauty across the extreme right. Warrington attacked in vein Score at half-time—Stalvbridge, one goal ; Warrington, nil. In the second half the Rovers had the hest of matters, and scoring three goals, finally won by four goals to nil.</p>
