      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>CLAIM AGANST A NEWTON FIRM</head>
        <p>CLAIM AGANST A NEWTON FIRM</p>
        <p>County Court A ction at Warrington.</p>
        <p>At a special sitting of the Warrington C ounty Court on Monday, before his Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, KC, an action was heard in which George Hibbert, engineer. 57, Parkfield-road, Pendleton, claimed the sum of £6 5s for work done from Messrs Gillespie arse Mason, paper manufacturer, Newton.—Mr Shawcross, barrister, of Manchester, appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr Rigby Swift. barrister, of Liverpool, instructed by Messrs Robert Davies and Company, of Warrington, represented the defendant comyany. Mr. Shawcrosa exrdained th4t the claim was made up of the two items--£3 103 61 for work done in respect of making cLhulations and drawing plans for certain shafting and pulleys, and £2 15s for expenses incurred IM two journeys to Bradford for the purpose of inspecting shafting on behalf of the defendant company. Plaintiff gave evidence bearing out his solicitor's state mentand said he had had over 40 years experience in the kind work mentioned. In cross-examination by Mr. Swift he admitted that the company's engineer could have done the work quite as well as he. Be received commission from the firms who obtained the orders, amounting to £l2 its 31, but taking this into account he did not charge defendants as much as be otherwise would have done. Fcr the defence Mr. Swift said She arrangement come</p>
        <p>to between the parts with reference to the plans&amp;c., was that Mr. Hibnert should reciveefor his services the commission to which he was entitled and which be re- ceived. Plaintiff told Mr. Barton that he would do him afavour by allowing him to do the work, as be would get the trade commisaion.</p>
        <p>Richard Barton, the managing director of the Com- pany, said plaintiff told bim that he would charge nothing for thn work as he would get the trade com- mission. Witness denied ordering plaintiff to go to Bra I ford.</p>
        <p>This evidence was corroborated by James Broady,the foreman.</p>
        <p>His Honour said that from the evidence placed before he wts g at iafi9cl that m,laintiff was eltitled to any him remuneration, he, gave jidgrn:!at for defendants with costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>BONNIE SCOMAND.</head>
        <p>BONNIE SCOMAND.</p>
        <p>" Stands Scotland where it did ?" inquired Macbeth. Yes, Scotland stands where it did ; and is ncw,as it has aver been, the great country of a great people, small in nothing but area. Scotland is at once the land of romance.and of hard,mattei-of-fact.up-to-data industry. And hat of Scotl Ind's eons? They have distinguished themselves in every walk of life the world over. We who say this are English from csre to cuticle, so cannot be accused of prejudice. But Scotsmen owe none of their success to immunity from ills which beset other people. And so it happens that Mr. William Logan. who is em- pl -led on a farm at Snavrton, Batantrae, Ayrshire—the countv which gave Robert Burns to the world—said on Sep. 5,1901—" I enjoyed good heal ..h until a year last June when I began to be trout id with a feeling of heavi- ness at the pit of the stomach after eating even the light- est meal. In course of time this became almost unbearable. Pain settled in my right shoulder ; my eyesight became blurred, and I had many fits of vomit rig. A fluttering sensation at my heart incressad my tears, and I became weak. sickly, and low-spirited. At length I was com- pelled to gee a doctor, then a second, and afterwards a third. They treated me much alike—gave me pills and other medicine, and forbade me a whole list of things— potatoes, meat, cheese, tea, Sz.:, but my daily torture continued."</p>
        <p>_ . Mr Logan was suffering from a severe attack of indi- gestion, and grew steadily worse. Ile contiouce : "My niatits were wakeful and reitless, and many an evening- when I came home from work I went to bed literally empty. Another time I got up and went to work on an empty stomach, afraid t eat my breakfast, so that at last I broke down and had to give up work for a fortnight. Oae day I read in the" Dandbe Weekly News " of a cure brought about by Seigel' a Syrup. The symptoms described were erectly mine, and then and there I bought a bottle and began to take it. I was disappointed ; but as there was shme improvement I continued to take it until I had used four or five betties, by which time I was com- pletely cured. Before I bad finished the secend bottle I knew that I had found the right medicine. I felt better aid brighter in every respect ; could eat, and food again agreed with MI!.. My sleep was restored, and it seemed as if new life had come to me."</p>
        <p>Tnere is no gainsaying evidence such as this. Mr. Logan is now as well as ever he was, and gratefully acknowledges the cause of his restored health. Scots- men are too intelligent, too persevering a race to rest content with enfeebled vitality. Tney discover what it is that blocks their way. and employ the best means to remove it, ev3n as Mr. Logan did. In our Carnegie Competition we have awaried One Guinea to the following :—J. E. Watkins, 72, Talbot Road, Southsea, Hants; A. Mayers, Teemors, Beiturbet, County Fermanagh ; Miss A. Felkes, 20, New S:reet, Gloucester; Ethel M. Watts,2s, Aldbridge Street, Surrey Square, London. S.E . • E Lucas, Green Street Green, near Orpington, Kent. Further awards will be announced in the newspapers from week to week.</p>
        <p>Accident to Mrs. G. S. Reaney. Mrs G. S. Heaney, formerly of Warrington, was recently knocked down by a cyclist in the city of London, and for some weeks has been confined to her room. It will be remembered that the late Rev G S Reaney was at one time the minister of Wycliffe Church, Warrington, and of Cavendish- treet Chapel, Manchester.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATMlid.—A.dvt.</p>
