        <p>"NONCONFORMITY NOT SCHISM."</p>
        <p>Oa Sunday evening at St Paul's Wesleyan Church Run- corn the Rev Alfred Wells,superintendent minister of the circuit preached a powerful sermon on the above subject There was a crowded congregation. The text was taken from the lit Cur. ail, 12 13. "For as the body is one and bath many members and all the member; of that one body being many are one body so also is Christ. For by one spirit are we all baptized into one body whether we be Jews or Gentiles, whether we be bond or free and have been all made to drink into one Spirit. For the:body is not one member but many." The rev I gentleman said he had to speak that night on " Non- conformity not Schism." It was very difficult for Nonconformists to enter into the feelings with which Anglicans regarded what they call ei dissent i.e., Nonconformity. It was a hard matter for one person to pat himseif in the place of his opponents. so as to do full justice to their principles and reasons. Still they were ferried by imperative necessity to iterate or re-iterate the principles upon which they maintained their Nonconformity. As Nonconformists they repre- sented a multiplicity of distinct religious organizations which were outside the pale of the Church ol England as by law established. Eapecially for their isolation from that Church they were the subject of bitter con- troversy and cruel asperitiea. They were charged with being subvertera of the faith of the Aposee Paul and were denounced as heretics and sehismatics and were continually reminded of their position by that dreadful demon religisus unequality, and they were perpetually being called upon and put on their defence. He was prepared to give chapter and verse for every word be would utter that night. He held in hie hand two num- bers of the Runcorn Church Parish Magszine. They contained two articles u ritten under the name of a Canon of the Church of England,the Rev Canon Harnmor. a man whose bigotry and whose fierceness to Noncon- formity could only be accounted for by his association in days long oast with Wesley&amp; n Methodism. They were not his (the speaker's) words, they were puhlished under sanction, for the maeazine to.o upon the cover the names of the clergy of tree Pariih of Runcorn, an i he held that no clergyman would for one moment pub- lish anything of which he did not approve—that in heir town the Anglican system identili rd itself with the views which appeared in that magazine. He had not time to read all the articles, but he would give them a sample :—" We dare not separato from the Church because it is God's Church." The Canon dealt with that and then says " It is still God's Courch !not- withstanding its present diaunions and corruptions. whatever they may be, even if they are as bad as Dis- senters believe thorn to be, still it is the Church of God and England. Just as the carnal community at Corinth was the Church of God." He again says : " We dare not Lie Dissenters because we dare not pre- tend to know bettor than God or dare to be holier and purer than He. We dare not separate from the Church because our Lord Jesus Christ did not separate from the Jewish Church." In the following month he says "We I dare not be D:ssenters because we dare not disobey I Christ because Christ told us that the Church's officers were to be obeyed in all things lawful, however corrupt, them men may be." " They dare not be Dissenters be- cause it is God's will that there should be one body and no more. As there is one I Lerd, one fold, one spirit, and one God, the Father of all, so there is one body of Christians." Nonconformity was much older than the established Anglican Church in the just sense of the word. Through their fidelity to their principles they were recognized now as a Christian community but this had only been won by paying the price of a bitter persecution. They claimed toleration as their due hut the question which invited attention was whether they could throw back the ugly taunt that the Anglican Church threw at them by calling them schismatics. Now what was the Church of God ? Many teachers told them schism was a grievous sin. But they procneded to teach that separation from the Church of Eigland was Schism and there- fore that separation from the Church of England wafi a sin. In that point they spoke untruly. Archbishop Sinclair said he longed with an earnestness that nothing could veil for the day when Nonconformists would come back to the main body of Christianity in that land. What was the main body of Christianity in this or any other land F They could not accept the Anglican Church as being the answer to that question. To that Church independent Nonconformity—he could not say Wesleyan—owed nothing and could therefore never ratarn. Only the night previous it had been dis tinctly stated that John Wesley died a Churchman</p>
        <p>and a cocoa that is unqucstionably SUPERIOR TO ALL FOREMN COCOAS</p>
