        <p>STALL: Presidents. Miss McMullan and Miss M 4-4 41, Wants. Misses A Curran, II Byrne and B Byrne ,aURSDAY'S OPENING CEREMONY. ,Illk„!le was an encouraging attendance at the open- 'tit,7 the bazaar on Tnursday afternoon. Mr F Betberton, of Rainhill, performed the q t;'ag ceremony' and he was accompanied by the tt,o%/It'B Stapleton Bratherton, Mr _Edmund Breth- and Misses Ethel and Monica Bretherton. ki yor of Ma f Warrington (Alderman Roberts) pre- over the proceedings, and there were also ,(lio:/at on the platform the Vicar General of the ta./7e.„8 of Shewsbury, (Canon Marsden), and the rather Mark and Andrew (Sutton). Ryan, prie2a, Whittle, Stanley. Dermal!), and McGrath 1111 charge of the Mission). Thero FRIDAYS PROCEEDINGS. lg of thyZana again a large gathering at the re-open e ftted th—n,,',B2ftar on Friday. Dr Fox presided, and the never helped a bazaar unless he saw 4Vr,17 adequate reason for it. In this case a Was an absolute necessity. The present ilplag at Latchford was a school chapel and the a g-rbutaent had compelled them to use it entirely ore `l3olPurposes. The congregation was there- turned into the street. In the second tlethe Corporation had paved the street in front qtlai navg.church and had *wen them three years f)rB,,a whicla to have the church open for public ,kigir,'ll). That term expired in April next. Then qtiliZ the land was purchased 25 years ago and Iver.sd been called upon to pay the ground rent IsBiaCe,so that it had been practically eating its e4oo(*r• The promised donations amounted to 11,4 Which was very gratifying but that sum was thR Way oft .E 6,000. He trusted that as a result kkat bazaar they would raise a considerable it (Rear, hear.) p ilsit MONKS stated that when he received the 4,tion to open the bazaar he was somewhat aished because there was no Fea.et as to his</p>
