      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Handsome Donation to the Liverpool</head>
        <p>Handsome Donation to the Liverpool</p>
        <p>Cathedral Fund.</p>
        <p>The commencement of the harvest thanksgiving services at St Paul's Church last Thursday evening, was marked by the first visit to the parish of the Bishop of the Diocese (Right Rev Dr Chavasse). The service was rendered of special interest, as in order to give ex- pression to the cordiality of their welcome the congregation had subscribed the handsome sum of nearly 1,200 shillings for the Liverpool Cathedral fund, and at the service on Thursday evening, the Bishop received and feelingly acknow- ledged the donation. The church had been very prettily decorated with flowers, fruit, corn, tC:c , There was a very large congregation. The service was conducted by the Vicar (Rev ER Tarbuck ) Special harvest hymns were sung and the choir rendered the anthem 1' Praise the Lord." The Bishop•preached an earnestdiecourse from the words I will be as the dew unto Israel," Hosea xiv.,s, At the conclusion of his sermon his Hosea, called attention to the fact that the offertory was on behalf of the endowment fund and emphasized the fact that the church was but slenderly en- dowed. Apart from fees,pew rents,and Raster offer, hogs the endowment was less than ..£lOO per year The offertory amounted to .£l3.—Mter the collec- tion had been made the Vicar preiented a purse containing .C 57 10s in gold,together with the list of over 600 subscribers.—The Bishop said he thanked them all heartily in the name of God for the noble offering they had made out of their poverty to build Him a house worthy of the city and Diocese of Liverpool. It was the gr eat desire of his heart the the Cathedral should be regarded not as the Cathe- dral church of the city of Liverpool but as the Cathedral church of the whole diocese of Liverpool. The purse itself was specially made as the gift of Mr J B. Smethurst, the people's warden. It is of leather, stamped in letters of gold with the words " Presented to the Lord Bishop of the Diocese with the offering to the Cathedral Fund of 1,175 shillings by the people of St Pauls' Warrington, October 24th 1901,"</p>
        <p>Home and Foreign Missions.—Sale of Work</p>
        <p>at Warrington. A sale of work on behalf of Florae and Foreign Missions was held in Fairfield Schol, Manchester- road, on Friday and Saturday. The lady members of the congregation attending the Parish Church had been busy for some time past in getting ready the different articles—useful and ornamental—and the rapid manner in which these were disposed of must have been a source of gratification to them. There were several attractionslwhich included con- certs and Maypole dancts, given by the junior girls attending the Higher Grace School, and also an exhibition of South African curiosities lent by Cap- tain Appleton. The following presided at the stalls :—Parochial Stall : Mrs Willis, Mrs Peter Rylands, Miss Pierpoint, Mrs Craik, Mrs Dewhurst, 1 Mrs Holbrook, Mrs Bottomley, Miss Brown, Miss Shaw, Mrs W.P Rylands, Mrs William Fairclougb, Miss Fairclough, and Mrs Collin. St. George's Mission Stall : Miss Hetherington, Mrs Barrett, and teachers. St. Clement's stall: Mrs Charles worth, Mrs Cameron,Miss Wright, Miss Le wis,Miss Gomel and Miss Taylor. Flower Stall : Mrs J E Davies and daughters. Refreshments : The Misses White, Mrs 1 Stevenson, Miss Willis, Misses Litton, Miss Brooke, I Miss Dewhurst, Mies Brown, Miss Gray, and Miss Willcocks. Children's Toy Stall : Misses Dorothy and Vera Collin, Miss Farrell, and the Maters Craik.—On Friday thelsale:was opened by Mrs Lyon and'on Saturday it was re-opened by Mrs J C Parr, in the absence, through illness, of Mrs Stanton 1 of Thelwall.—The Rev Canon I% rrzis having ex- plained the objects of the bazaar impressed upon the visitors the necessity of supporting the foreign missions. Two-thirds of the psople of this world were practically heathens,and knew nothing what- I ever about God. He realised that to a great extent when be was in India, where so many of his Majesty's subjects lived.—Mrs Parr said she was ex- ceedingly sorry Mrs Stanton could not be present. and she was sure they would all regret the cause of her absence, She could only hope that the bazaar I would be a great success. (Applause.)—Mr Craik bad great pleasure in proposing a vote of thanks to their future Mayoress, Mrs Parr, for her kindness in coming to open the bazaar. (Applause ) Her presence showed the deep interest taken by her in all things which were for the public good. (dear, hear, and applause.)—The Rev A F White seconded the motion, and said that Mrs ,Parr was about to undertake a very responsible duty in the town. but he was sure she would fulfil that duty very credit- ably. (Applause.)—The motion was heartily agreed to.</p>
        <p>SWAN FOUNTAIN PENS.—INCLiny of our readers will be interested to learn that Messrs M able, Todd and Bard of "Swan" pen fame have if sued in a bandy compact form, an attractive booklet entitled " ' Swan ' Pen Christmas shopping list of gifts," included in which naturally appear their own inimitable specialities. The booklet is exc ptionally useful for Christmas shopping, as it embraces a list of choice and varied articles which are alphabetically arranged ; while several pages are set apart for entering the names of those for whom the gifts are intended, together with the name of each gift and its cost, so that the book, if carefully kept,becomes a record of expenditure in gilts, and also serves to avoid repetition of the article to the same person. This booklet is sent Gratis and Post Free on application to : —Mabie, Todd and Bird, 93, Cheapside, E.C., 955, Regent-street, W., London, and 3, Exchange-street, Manchester. CLARKE'S WORLD-FAMED BLOOD MIXTURE " is a guaran- teed cure for all Blood and Skin Diseases. It is the most searching blood cleanser ever discovered, and it will free the system from all impurities from whatever cause arising. For Scrofula. Scurvy, Eczema, Bad Legs, Pimples and Sores of 1111 kinds, its eff-nts are marvellous. Thousands of Testi- monials. Bold everywhere, at 2s. 91. per bottle. Beware of worthless imitations and substltutes.—Advt. I.</p>
