      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <p>PLEASANT PARA.GRA_PIR WE are never made so ridiculous by the qualitie, lure have as by those we affect to have. "ARE you certain that you love me ? " "I am.' "But are you sure that you are certain?" CONGENIALITY is what we discover in people win keep still and let us talk about ourselves. THERE is no new needs watching so much as tin (one who is all the time watching someone else. IT isn't working for a living that hurts 6( average man, but the kind of living that he get for his work. Too many people we know are singing, "Scatter sunshine," and waiting for somebody else to do it. DIFFERENT WAYS.—" They sat and held hanil all the. evening." "How sill, I" "Oh, I don't know. You have to in whist.' SOME people are so slow that when they are standing about wondering what will happen, they :are one or two events behind. "You married me for my money." 'What of it? You married me for my beauty." " Well, both securities have depreciated." He: "She-asked me what colour hair I liked hest." She: "That's like Maud; she's always so anxious to please that she would not mind dy(e)ing with that object." Ax fixankr.iNo Vturim.--Curions Old Lady: "How did you come to this, poor man?" Con- vict: "I was drove to it, lady." Curious Old Lady: "Were you, really?" Convict: "Yes, they brung me in the van, as usual." RER REASON.—" Why does Miss Johnson always wear black?" "She is mourning for her husband." "But she nver had one." "It's an account of the one she didn't have that she mourning." Womo REsrwr THEM —Poeteßs : "The poem 33 sent you, Mr. Editor, contains the deepest secrets of my soul." Editor: "I know it, raachtm ; and no one shall ever find them out through me." 'I EXPECT I have taken my last bath at home for a year or so." "Oh, I see. (;oing abroad— eh ?" "No, I mean to stay at home, but I have</p>
        <p>given my wiie a camera for her birthday present." " Really, I don't see the point." "She'll use the bath-room for a laboratory, that's all." Smrrit "It seems to me that Brown in an aw- fully pompous man—that is, he is so stiff in iii . . treatment of strangers," Jones : "Why, don't you know the reason is due to his business? He Is a, -starelunaker." Ax EXTRAORDINARY WomaN.—The Friend : " Have you seen your husband's mother yet ?" The Bride: ." I have, and she is the most extraordinary woman I ever hoard of." The Friend: "How is that ?" The Bride: "Why, she thinks me good eaiough for her son." FRESHLY rising to go; " I'm sorry to,break up Tour hand at whist, Miss Rosalie, but really tean't stay any later." Miss Rosalie: " Oh, never mind, .11r. Freshly; I'm sure we shall get on with a slummy just as well." ARTIST'S FRIEND, pointing to sketch: "I say, Harry, where did you get that ?" Harry: "Why, I got that out of my head." Friend : ' Well, a lucky thing for your head that you got it out." A MAN with a donkey for sale hearing that a friend wanted to buy one, sent him the following written on a post-card: "Dear Jack, if you are hooking for a really good donkey, don't forget me."</p>
        <p>Mamma : Playing Indians is so rough. Why are you crying.? Have they been scalping you again? Spotted Panther (alias Willie): No, mamma ; we have been smoking the pipe of peace. SIGNS OF COMINW TROUBLE.—Johnny: "I tell you, Rapes going to catch it after the company's gone." Tommy: "How do you know?" Johnny: "He's told ma once or twice she was mistaken about something, and she's said: • Why, darling ? ' " Wait A RELIEF.—Doctor (shaking his head): "Well, my dear sir, I can do nothing more for you " Patient: " W-h-a-t ? Good gracious, doctor!" Doctor: "No, really, my friend, you are in perfect health." Tim BOER'S RELIGION.—Boer child: "Father, if I were carrying the Bible in one hand and a gun in the other and an enemy approached, which should drop first?" Boer father "The emeny, my son!" PHYSICIAN.—" If you wrote this prescription you had better give on medicine and go into some other business. Dr. Siptiller, just got his L.R.C.P. : ." Why, what's wrong with it?" Experienced physician: "What's wrong! Why the thing is positively legible." Tax Lady of the House: "Why don't you go to work? bon't you know a rolling stone gathers no moss 7" Browning, the Tramp: "Madam., not to evade your question at all, but merely to obtain information, may I ask of what practical utility moss is to a man in my condition ?' A GREAT REGOMMENDATION.—Miss Dukane: "I want to have some photographs taken. Where would you' go?" Miss Humly : "I'd go to Mr. Bnapshot if I were you. He made some perfectly lovely pictures of me." Miss Lhikane : Did he really? Well, if he is such a clever artist as that I'll go there to." , THE MAJOR. "On the field of battle a brave soldier will always be found where the bullets •aro thickest. You understand? Private Flannigan, where would you be found then on the battle- field ?'' Private Flannigan: "In the ammunition - waggon, sor." A SUPERSTMOUS CREATURE. —He "You acknowledge that yon—cr—like me, yet refuse?" Nile (belle of the season) : "Yes, I must. Thirteen is an unlucky number." "Ah ?" "You are the thirteenth man who has proposed to me, and if we should become engaged I'm afraid something dreadful would happen." "You are foolishly superstitions. What could happen "I might marry you." HE -KNEW A GREAT Dear..—" Now, George Gazzam," said the teacher, "you will please, tell the cla.ss what you know of the social customs of the ancient Greeks and Romans." ." The ancient Greeks and Romans," replied young Gazzarn, in a loud and. metallic tone of voice, "were allowed to marry only one wife each, and this was called • monotony." A Bowrox man was dreaming the other night of tintold wealth that was being beatowed upon him. When he was awoke from his dream, instead of having a fortune, ha found he was losing a part of one. There was an open window in his chamber and a burglar was searching his pockets. POSER--An amusing story is told by a Sootch ontemporary cf the little daughter of a game- . keeper who lives in one of the lodges at Keith Hall. Her father was telling her mother about the ar- rangements for ths Duke of York's visit, and how the Duke would drive round by his lodge. Next morning the little girl asked her mother with an awe-stricken face, "I say, mammy, wull ye ha'e to open the gate for the Duke., or wull he thee oivre't ?" THRICE DEAD. —After the battle of Custozza, ii soldier supposed to have been killed was entered on the books of his company, "Died oil the 24th of June," dm A few days afterwards it turned out • that he was still alive, and the honest quarter- master made the following entry: "Died by mis- take." At length there came a letter from the Ministry of War announcing the death of the man at the hospital, when the sergeant recorded the fact as follows: "Re-died by order of the Ministry." A Soaomos axis iirrsarrtsm.—A story is told of a judge who lately had the hypnotic plea raiseu before him by a burglar. The prisoner claimed that he did not know that he was " burgling " that he did it automatically and unconsciously. tinder the direction of a hypnotist. The judge said .he would give him the full benefit of the law, and also of his hypnotic misfortune. He thereupon sentenced the man to five years' penal servitude, but told him he could, if he chose, send for the hypnotist, and have himself made UneOnSeiolle for the entire term of his imprisonment. • THE FREIVCII3IAN AND THE ENGLISH LANGUAGE. —A Frenchman who was on a visit to this country was one day leaving a large Loodon railway station on a journey. The station was under repairs, and some scaffolding had been erected for the purpose. ..As the train was leaving the station it was seen to pass very near one of the scaffolds, and the porter shouted out as a warning, "Look out ! The Frenchman at once popped out his head, which came in violent contact with the structure. When lie had come to his senses, he said, "Oh, these Englismen are great fools ; they say 'Look out' when they mean Look in.' I never liked their language. Oh, my poor head ! " - - - -</p>
        <p>OLD FALSE TEETH BOUGHT.-31any people have old or disused false teeth. Send your teeth to RD and J B Fraser. Ltd., Princes-street. Ipswich (estop. 1833), and they Wilt remit the utmost value tettan or make you beet offer. Largest and oldest buyer old false teeth In the world. ANTIQUE Wrought IRON and COPPER WARE. for wedding presents, tte.—GABLAYD. Bridge St,—Advt, LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS AID SNOB UNDHR THE CLOCK. BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. TABLE. FLOOR, and HANGING LAMPS of every lescription.—J S 11ABLAJAD,IronmoBger, Bridge St. Advt.</p>
        <p>Ettttro to tb bitor.</p>
        <p>The Corporation Controversy.</p>
        <p>Sir,—! am greatly surprised that Mr. E L Fleming, in a speech at the meeting last week in the Cc-operative Hall should have intruded my name. Be did so on the ground of some reference made by me to him in a priv- ate, friendly I. ter, as fAr back as -1898 about an election of Guardians. If this is deemed by bun a proper use of correspondence between friends, which most people consider strictly private, I must be allowed to differ from him. It seems to me to be a most insufferable liberty, to say the least of it. I fihould fancy it will tend to diminish the amount of his correspondence. and to lirn;t the area of his friendships. I am the more smy at the incident because nithing is farther from my desire than to have even my name mixed up with a controversy a hich appears to emanate from, and be guided by, such very mixed and unfortunate motives. Ido not remember ever having experienced anything similar before, and desire thus publicly to disapprove and repudiate it,—Your faithfu ly, JOHN YONG&amp;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>19, Wil•on Patten-qtreet, October 30:b, 1901.</head>
        <p>19, Wil•on Patten-qtreet, October 30:b, 1901.</p>
        <p>Runcorn School Board Elect:on</p>
        <p>Clergy and Education.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Runcorn is in the threes of a School Board elec- tion. Three years ago the Church or Sectarian party were well organised arid held meetings in various quarters of the town ; the Progressives or unsectarian party made only an eleventh hour demonstration and were in a state of general unpreparedness. The natural result followed ,and for three years the managers of the denominational schools in the town have also controlled the School Board and the Board Schools. That is a brief, plain itatement of fact. The question now to be decided is has this been beneficial to the educational in- terests of the town ; and is it for the welfare of the na- tion that those who are opposed to School Boards and alr their works, should have the naramount voice in ad- ministering the Eibration Acts ? It is time the question of education war taken outside the arena of church and chapel and raised far above the clamour and rancour of party politics. What has the mettal and intellectual equipmett of our sons and daughters to do with the dogma of this church or that chapel ? Why should not Tory and Liberal unite in placing education on a broad, sound and commonsenae basis,and provide good buildings,thnroughly competent teachers, and a system of instruction that will enable the child who is endowed with brains, be his parents never so poor, to rise step by step to the hifsh- est educational eminence and the nation benefit by his attainments ? These are two propositions which would have been easily solved ere this, were it not for the baneful influence of clergy and priesthood in wh3t should be a pun ly secular affair of the people. It has been well said that were it not for the parsons there would be no religious difficulty in education ; and it is a matter of history that but for the Interference and opposition of the ecclesiastical element in the if 11599 Of Lords, England would have had a national system of education almost a century ago. Therefore, before yielding themselves to the specious arguments of the clergy, the people would do well to ponder what has been the attitude of thc ecclesiastic towards the educa- tion of the common people since the written history of this country began. Did they not in early days ever preach the doctrine that education was only for l'oe ruling classes• That to eutivate the minds and interns gences of the masses of the people would make them become a menace and a danger to the State. Was not ever the burden of their proclamations to th 3 people contentment with the lowly lot in which a wise Provi- dence had placed them, and the wickedness and folly of holding opinions diverse to theirs (the clergy) and the squires ? Drawers of water and hewers of wood— beasts of burden, serfs to the rich and powerful—this was all that poor people were fit for in the eyes of the great bulk of the prelates and the parsons. flistorYr any history you like bears out this view, and the very av exceptions are the bearers of illortrious names. Never is there an instance where the parsons as a claes have started a mcvament for the educational advancement of the masses of the people. Did they found the schools and eolleges with which our country is too sparsely dotted ? No, this work was left for lay- men. Laymen have founded and endowed schools and then the clergy have stepped in and diverted the endow- ments to their own pleasure and profit. This is no fancy picture ! . . . . _</p>
        <p>Mr;John Taylor who has made a study of the question says " Pious founders have left free schools fr the. children of the poor of all denominations and the bishops and clergy of the Church of England have captured these schools and used them for denominational and sectarian purposes." Instances by the score could be quoted. Just one concrete example. About 200 years ago William Hulme, a Lancashire merchant, bequeathed certain lands in Lancashire in trust to maintain as ex- hibitioners at: Brszenose College, Oxford, four of the poorest sort of bachelors of arts. The endowment originally brought in £64 a year. The land has become parts of towns, and cities and is now yie'ding about £34,000 a year. Is it being used to maintain poor students at Brszenose college, or for educational purposes at all ? The parsons have captured the endowment and instead of using it for educational purposes they are paying £lO,OOO a year to church parsons, and are building par- sonages and buying advowsons and benefices for their poor relations. They have spent £5,000 in building a house at Oxford and they are paying £l5O a year to a lecturer on divinity. This is the way the clerics des with endowments for the education of the poor. Was it the clerics who established Sunday Schoo's. No it was a layman and the parson did not take kindly to them at the start. Was it the clerics who com- menced elementary day schools for the poor? N 3 again laymen began the work. In each instance the clergy looked on with indifference. which gave place to alarm as success appeared, and then joined in to gain control not to make success more rapid but to act as a break en the wheel of progress. As each successive step has been taken to make the bounds of educa- tion wider, the clergy have ever been more concerned about their own selfish irtnests than the granting of educational advantages to the poor, and politicians of both parties have ever pandertd to their importunities. The result is seen in the educatior al chaos which prevails—a medly which is inimical to the interests of the teacher, the scholar and the country. It is a disgrace to a nation which claims to be in the fore front of nations,tha t the schools should be the battle ground for sectarian jealousy and unholy strife to the great detriment of the education of the children.</p>
        <p>With the record they have the clerics are not safe guides for the electors on the education question. They are the same yesterday, to-lay and to-morrow. They would sacrifice upon the altar of their sectarian cupidity the intellectual and mental and material well being of the children, who in a few years, will have to fight for the industrial and commercial supremacy of the country and upon whcm will devolve the maintenance of a vast empire won by a great expenditure of blood and treasure It is an encouraging sign that many chu,chmen are L bursting the trammels with which their clerey have enshrouded them on this education question and are openly advocating a national system of education in which the welfare of the child shall outweigh every other consideration. A clergyman writing in " The Commonwealth," a periodical edited by Dr Canon Slott flolland,on the sunject of education,says:—"The present system must inevitably perish, and that shortly. Few will regret its departurelt is hampering the whole dove% opments of a saner, liberal, more efficient education. It is fostering incredible bitterness, friction, and needless grievances in a thousand parishes in England. The issues are inextricably interwoven, concealed with false cries, the good fighting the good with the weapons of evil, masquerading as the good." " Cupidity," he continues, " demanding reduction of the rates—miry jealously guarding the educational preserve againit the unhal- lowed footsteps of the poor—the spirit of mastery cling- ing to paver and domination, these, whether within or without the churches, cannot be of Christ. Yet, if all these were to-morrow removed how much would be left I ot the imposing party that respects cur sacred watch- word and arrays itself to battle in the cause of religion. True and eloquent words these for churchmen to ponder , And how applicable to the situation ia Runcorn. Read this too from the recent Church Congress at Brighton. The speaker Mr B'acker, president of the N.U.T. and headmaster of a Church fli,gher Grade School and a loyal churchman. He said "The Church of Eng- land, cared little for primary education, at any rate it cared little for the teachers. It did not pay them as the School Boards did and it employed an army ot un- qualified men, men whose only qualification might be that they were vaccinated that they were ever 18 and that they were approved by H.M. inspectors, The intolerable strain fell most largely on the teachers. The children attending voluntary schools had not equality of opportunity in comparison with the children in Board Schools, The whole system was inferior in the volun- tary schoo's. The Church did not trust its teachers. The system of diocesan inspection simply degraded the best and highest and holiest of the teachers work to the level of the ordinary subjects. What they wanted was a national system, supported by National funds." Mr Blacker evidently objects to the child be.. leg used as a pawn in the game of theological , chess ; and he speaks truths with no uncertain sound. Will Runcorn churchmen learn of such men as here qnoted and in epire of priestly intolerance and bigotry dare to strike a blow for educational freedom in the coming election. I apologise for the length of this letter, but will probably return to the subject again. In conclusion I would state that in religion no sect claims me for its own and in politic I am, yours truly.</p>
        <p>Runcorn, October 29th 1901.</p>
