      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>The Cabinet and the War.</p>
        <p>The order to despatch fresh cavalry (Dragoon Gaards and Hussars) to the front came as a great surprise at Aldershot, and is said to have been the result of the meeting of the Cabinet on Monday. Heavy infantry drafts are also going out, including every available effective soldier in the Army Corps. The troops will go before Christmas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>NEWTON VOLUNTEERS. Fortieth Annual Prize Distribution.</head>
        <p>NEWTON VOLUNTEERS. Fortieth Annual Prize Distribution.</p>
        <p>Last Friday evening the fortieth annual prize meeting in connection with the F Company, 6th V.B.K.L R. was held at the Orderly Hoorn, Newton- le-Willows. Captain JIC D Parker presided, and was supported by Lieu'eoant Crean, of D Comp any of the same Battalion, Colour-Sergeant Appleton, Sergeant J ilimmor (hon. secretary), Messrs P Percival (a member of the Company forty years ago), J McDougal, G McCormick, etc. There was a large attendance of members.—Captain Parker, who had a cordial reception, at the outset distri- buted the prizes as follows : Colonel Sir CI Id Hamilton's cup, Lance-Sergeant J Handicap Prizes :—Private Hargreaves, score 58, prize 21 iDs; Sergeant F Appleton, 50, Et 55 ; Lance-Sergeant W Kent, 50; £1; Private G. McCormick, 50, 17s 6d ; Private H Travers, 50, 129 6d; Lance-Sergeant J Pullan, 48. 105 ; Private W Jones, 47, 9s • Bugler B Jones. 46, 8s; Private J Jackson, 45, 7a • PrivAt'e T Boardman, 43, 6s ; Private T Tapp, 42. 5a : L'anee-Corporal J &amp;tucks. 42. 5s ; Serzeant R Wright. 40, 5s ; Lance-Corporal W Devereux.4o, 55 ; Private H Lanwarne, 40 5s • Private Fltz4ersld, 39, 55; Lance- sergeant H Hobbs. 39. 5. ; Lance-corporal ii H Jones, 37, 51; Lance-Corporal W Pendlebury, 37, ss; three 37'5 counted out. Marksman's Cup (presented by Captain N Mo'iorquodale) : —l. Private T Boardman, cup and £1 : 2. Lance-Sergeant J Pullan, 59. 106 ; Sergeant F Appleton. 56, ss; Sergeant 'dimmer. 51. 2s 6d. Recruits' Prizes.—Private 11 Wright, 21, 12s 6d ; Private F T Kenny, 19, lOs ; Private 3 R Davies. 19, 6s; Private T Nash, 18, 65 ; Private It Litler. 13, 5s ; Private r Porter, 10, 55 ; Private A Guilford.lo, 5s : Private 0 Bennett. 8. 4s. Drill Prizes.—Cadet Hancock and Lance-Sergeant H Hobbs 35 attendances, 11, 3d each ; Bugler B Jones, 33, 75 6.1 ; Lance-Sergeant J Pullin. 31, 55 : Sergeant J Rimmer, 28,55; Lance-Corporal J Anderson, Bugler G Baker, and Private W H Bnrgess, 24, 34 each. Tradesmen's Prizes for highest score at 2CO yards Bugler B Jones, Private 3 Davidaon, Sergeant F Apnieton, Lance-Sergeant J Pullan, Pri,rate T Yapp, Private II Travis, Lance-Sergeant H Nobles, Lance-Sergeant R Hill, Lance- Corporal It H Jones. Lance-Corporal W Devereux, Bugler ti Baker, Lance-Sergeant W Kent, Sergeant J Rimmer, Lance- ° u.poral J Smacks, Private G McCormick, Private W Hargreaves. Private H Wright, Lance-Corporal J Anderson, Private H Lanwarne, Private F T Kenny, Private T Board- man. Sergeant 11 Wright, Lance-Corporal W Pendleburv, Private A Hobbs, Private J W Knobbs, Private Fitzgerald. The healths of Captain Parker and Lieutenant Crean were drunk, and on the proposition of Ser- geant J Rimmer, a vote of thanks was accorded to the prize donors. Captain Parker responded on their behalf. Colour-Sergeant Appleton proposed ' the health of Sergeant -Rimmer for the indefatig- aole manner he had got in the prizes. Sergeant , Rimmer suitably re4ponded. On the proposition of Sergeant Kent a similar compliment was paid to Colour-Sergeant Appleton, who occupied the chair after the departure of Captain Parker and Lieuten ant Crean. A number of musical items were given by Sergeants 'Aright, Appleton and Barker, Cor- porals Devereux, Pendlebury, Privates Hughes, Bullock, Messrs G McCormick, and Seddon, and a recitation by Sergeant Kent. Me Seddon accom- panied. The proceedings closed with the toast of 'The King."</p>
      </div>
