      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meeting of the Earlestown and District P.S.A. Society, was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall, Earlestown, on Sunday. Mr Ellis pre- sided, and the principal speaker was Mr G. Burgess, of Salford. Vocal items were given by Miss L Wright, and recitations by Miss J Williams. Miss Williamson presided at the piano. ALLEGED THEFT OF PIGEONS AT BUETONWOOD.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Occasional Court, on Mond4y, before Mr S R Armitage, George Mottram was charged With stealing from the pigeon cote of Her- bert Turner, Lumber-lane, Buttonwood, three pigeons, value £2 on October 25,—The complainant stated that on Friday night at 10.30 be went to the Pigoon cote and found the door off its hinges, and three pigeons missing. Ile gave information to the police. The prisoner lived in Broad-lane, not far from his house.—Police-constable Garry gave evidence to finding the three pigeons at the house of the prisoner, who said he had bought them. Ile afterwards said they had been given to him by a man be did not know.—The Case was adjourned until Newton Petty Sessions, bail being allowed.</p>
        <p>WARGRAVE CHURCH RE:OPENING .ERVICES.</p>
        <p>After being closed tor over three months for re- novation and alteration, this church was re-opened on Sunday. We have already given an idea as to the work which has been carried out, and on Sun- day the result obtained was given unstinted praise by the large congregations which assembled. Be- sides the replastering of the walls, the revanishing of all timbers, and the alteration of the seating, the lights have been improved, incan- descent lamps being provided. The organ has also been cleaned and enlarged by one evra stop, this having been done by Messrs Rushworth's, o, Liverpool. Ordinary services were held morning and evening on Sunday, when homely discourses were preached by the Rector. tl'e Rev J Carson. In the atternoon a musical service was given by the choir, when the following programme was sub- mitted :—Organ so!o, " Fantasia inF'"; anthem, " Hearken unto this"; sono, "Sun of my soul. Miss K Collett; organ solo, "In Paradissuin " solo, "ft is enough," Mr John Molyneux ; organ solo, " Communion in F" ; anthem, " 0 give thanks ; organ solo, " March for a Church Festi- val." The collection in the afternoon was in aid of the organ renovation fund, and the morning and evening for the church funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>FORN-STERS' PRESENTATION AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>FORN-STERS' PRESENTATION AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The annual gathering of the Court Broad Oak, No. 595 of the Ancient Order of Foresters was held at. the Wellington Hot,d, Earlestown, on Saturday evening. The proceedings were initiated by a dinner to which between 40 and 50 members sat down, the meal being heartily enjoyed. The after proceedings were presided over by Bro Egan, the vice-chair being occupied by Mr T Ritchie. The toasts having been proposed and duly honoured, —The Chairman said he had a very pleasing duty to perform, which should have been performed by one of their oldest and respected members, the Trea-urer of the Court (Bro. Ashcroftl, but unfor- tunately illness had prevented him from being present. It therefore fell upon him to act as a sub- stitute, and he had the extreme pleasure of pre- senting a timepiece to their worthy Secretary, Mr. Latham. (Applause.) He well remembered—it looked but a short time since—when their brother whom they were honouring that night first took up the duties in that court room. They were then very poor indeed; in fact, from the condition of finances it was prophesied that the Court could not survive more than three years. Two or three of them took the affairs in hand and declared they would not die without a struggle. They put their shoulders to the wheel, and now, far from being bankrupt they were in a position to advance money at four and a half per cent, to their brethren in order that they might beild their own houses. The happy position in which they now found themselves was mainly brought about by their hard working secretary, Bro Latham. Dr Patterson said he had great pleasure in support- ing the remarks of Mr Egan. Ha bad found Bro., Latham a most painztaking secretary and always straightforward and fair dealing with his brethren. Bro. La„ham, who was warmly received, said that he felt his position so deeply , that be could not express his thanks adequately. It was not exactly an awkward position, but it was a position of • feeling brought about by the kind words uttered regarding him, He thanked them from the bottom of his heart for their kind references and the present of that beau- tiful marble timepiece. He should prize it most highly, not more for its value than for the kind feeling of 80 many generous friends. As he looked round he saw several in the room who were present when he had the honour to be appointed to the secretaryship, including the proposer and seconder, and he felt heartily thankful for their continued help and support. (Applause). Having referred to the financial position of the court and the insur- ance fund, he again thanked them for the present which he :would band down!as an heir-loom and which would stimulate those who received it after- wards to work in the interests of a friendly society. —The company then sang " Auld lang syne " Songs during the evening were given by Bros Powell, F Ezan, and others, and other toasts were proposed and responded to.—Mr Latham's present consisted of a handsome black marble and bronze 'timepiece bearing the inscription :—" Presented to Bro JAhn Latham by the members and visiting members of Court Broad Oak No. 595, A.0.F., in appreciation of his -services as secretary to the court for 21 years. October 26tb, 1901." The time- piece was supplied by Hesrrs Llayd, Payne and Amiel, Thomas-street, Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>MR. C. H. PIGOT'S CONCERT.</head>
        <p>MR. C. H. PIGOT'S CONCERT.</p>
        <p>One of the most successful and uniformly excel lent ballad concerts ever given in Warrington was the one promoted by Mr C LI Pigot and held in Parr Hall on Monday evening We use the word suc- cesful advisedly, but only from the point of the people who were in the fortunate position of being numbered among the audience. To them it was an eminently successful concert, but it is much to be regretted that from litho standpoint of Mr Pigot the description •• successful" must be qualified. For some inexplicable reason the public did not patron- ise the first high-class concert of the season in any- thing like satisfactory numbers, in fact the hail was not more than half full, and if those who stayed away were the only losers it would not matter so much, but the discouragement to Mr Pigot and other concert promoters is serious. " Musie bath charms," we are told, but the obvi sus conclusion is that in Warrington, at any rate, the public take a lot of charming. On Monday evening the concert part was composed of artistes who have won considerable renown inthe musical world. They were Madam Alice Esty (soprano), Miss Marian McKenzie, (con- tralto). Mr !James Leyland (tenor), Mr Watk n Mills (baritone). Miss Janotha (court pianist to the German Emperor),and Miss Nadia Sylva (violinist). The programme was of course, miscsllaneous, and .' included a feature entirely new to Warrington in the form of Miss Liz Leh mann's song cycle " The Daisy Chain," in which all four vocalists partici- pated,either as soloists or as a quartette party. The "Daisy Chain " is really, if we may explain, a stringing together of twelve songs from childhood, the lyrics being by R L Stevenson,L Alma-Tadema, Norman Gale, and others, the whole forming a charming feature in such a ballad concert as that •of Monday evening. Tuneful singing and perfect vocalisation were allied to pretty words and attrac- tive music, with the happy result that " The Daisy Chain" created a most favourable impression in Warringtou. Several of the numbers had to be repeated before the demands of the audience were satisfied. Of the two instrumentalists nothing but words of praise can be said. Miss Janotha is with- out dcubt the most talented lady pianiste now before the public, and her interpre- tations of the classical selections from Liebling Chopin, Leschetitzky, Mendelssohn, &amp;c., won the highest appreciation of the audience. Miss Janotha kindly responded to several vociferous encores. In Miss Nadia Sylva the Warrington concert-goers made the acquaintance of a violinist new to them. I She was very soon established as a warm favourite, for Miss Sylva is a violinist of distinction besides being an admirable and sympathetic executant We are sure we do but express the prevailing opinion when we say that she will be heartily welcomed on her next visit to Warrington. Madame Alice Esty and Miss Marian McKenzie both sang very accept , ably and added to their popularity in this town. A I splendid recent iOn, too, was accorded Mr Watkin Mills, whose rich baritone voice and finished style were displayed to advantage in everything he essayed. Mr Leyland was hardly so successful, but the solos down in his name were rendered with taste and feeling which merited the recall he ob- tained for his last song.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>WARRINGTON LIBERAL CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield at the Annual</p>
        <p>Meeting.</p>
        <p>Speech on Current Politics,</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington Liberal Club turned up in large numbers on Monday evening to welcome their president. Mr A R Ctosfield, the occasion being the annual business meeting of the Club. Councillor finnion presided.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS. _ _.</p>
        <p>The election of officers was expeditiously carried out, and resulted as follows :—President, Mr A H Crosfield ; vice-presidents, Councillors Tinnion, Plumpton, and Pierpoint ; bon treasurer, Mr John Crosfield ; hoe secretary,Mr T Pemberton ;auditors. Messrs John Harri- son and W Ford ; committee, Messrs S J Beamsn,George Beard, William Bibby, John Chappell, John Davies, E Striders, George 13 ,haw, Robert Woods, John Worrall, and E V Walker. There was only one change, that beinl in the representation of the Committee.</p>
        <p>FINANCIAL REPORT^A SATISFACTORY YEAR.</p>
        <p>Mr PEMBERTON read the statement of accounts for the past year, which showed a profit. He stated that the Club was in a smnd floancial condition, and making headway each year. The report was passed on the motion of Mr John Worrall, which drew attention to the scant inter Pat manifested in the Club by the members of the Liberal Association. He thought they ought to put in an appearance oftener, especially as the Association IT- ceived an annual grant from the Club funds. (Hear, hear.) MR. CROSFIELD'S</p>
        <p>_ Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, who was received with cheers sad :—I thank you for re-electing me president of the club. It is most satisfactory to notice the manner in which the club is prospering from a social point of view; in fact we are growing stronger every year. TOO re- marks that have been made to-night about the aaeist- ance that we ought to get from other members of tee Literal Association are, I am sure, very forcible, and I have no doubt they will go home to the right quarter. Our Chairman has alluded to the reciprocal assistance that could be given to Liberalism in Warrington by members of this club, because besides oue aociel plea • sores and functions here the club basal ways had the other side to its raison deeitre. And unquestionably it ought to be the focus and the centre of active Liberal- lam in the town. I think the remarks that have been made hit two ways. When I came here to-night it struck me that you might expect me not to talk to you much about politics but about yacht races, Tammany Hall, American Trusts or something of that description —(laughter)—and if by any chance it would interest you to hear a bit of that sort at one of our smokers, and you could spare me half-an-hour, it would give me great pleasure to spin you a yarn about the other side of the briny. (Applause.) To-night I do not propose to do so. What few remarks I have to make will be about politics ; and, in the first place, I am very glad indeed to hear what my friend Mr. Pemberton has told me to-night—the same story every FiX months—that the club is going stronger and stronger, and that the last election has left us, AO far as the club is concerned, neither smashed, demolished, or pulverized. (Laughter and applause). Just after the election I got a telegram from my friend Mr. Herbert Gladstone, and it ran " No reason for discouramement, you have made an excellent fight in Warrington," or words to that effect. And I think Mr. Gladstone Was perfectly right. We were handicapped at the last election by the division of the leaders of the party at headquarters, because undoubt- edly that division was reflected localls. Here in War- rington, there were one or two very prominent Liberals of great influence in the Own on whom I had counted, and on whom I had some reason to count for their sup- port ; but they were not able to give it me. I don't saylthis by way of complaint at all. I simply mention it be way of explanation, because what these gentle- men said to me when I called upon them in the short time at my disposal before the polling-day was " No, this Government has got us into this mess and it has got to ace Us out of it" ; and if you get such a remark from leading Liberals what can you expect from the bulk of the voters of the constituency ? I am quite certain, to put the matter in a nutshell, that what the average voter said to himself was " 0 well," to quote old Abraham Lincoln's expressive remark " it does not pat' to swop horses while you are crossing a stream." (Applause.) That was the main factor we had to contend against and the war fever did the rest, and between them they made a very clean sweep of Lancashire. I mention this by way of explanation ; and although some of my friends on the Liberal side did not see their way to lend their influence and support to us, I am glad to say that we are every bit as good friends now as before the elec- tion. (applause.) Whatever else we may say, the election in Warrington was certainly fought in very gold part. and has left no nasty feeling or personal rare acme or animesity of any description, and we may take credit for having accepted our defeat with good humour and that we can now come up 'smiling,. (Applause.) The upshot is that they have had their own way, and WA have got a dose of 'Toryism that ought to satisfy the average man for sometime to come. (Lenghter). The Government are in a fair way to spend £200,000,000. and we shall have to pay the bill. I follow the newspapers as closely as most people, and I see the same wretched excuse is trotted nut. Recently I had the advantage of talking on South African affeirs with a couple of people who have taken a leading part out there, in fact two rather distinguished people who went through the siege of al ifeskine, and the only real excuse they have to give for plunging us into all this misery and disaster is the fact that the Boers were getting rather too uppish, and they said it would net do to knuckle under CO them. I venture.] to point oat vary mildly that that was the point. Had it been a question of knuckling under to the Roera it would have been a different matter, but we have had it from Mr. Chamberlain that in the negotiations preceding the strife the B oers bad oonceded to us ninr-teoths of what we were demanding, and it had been asked a good many times whether the other tenth was svelte all this trouble; and to that we have not had the answer. I am not going to traverse the whole ground I gave you several columns in the newspaper to enable you to study the pros and the cons of the matter, and they will satisfy you for the present. There are, how- ever, just two points I would aek you to keep clearly before you. The first is the complaint we have to make as to what took place during the four montbs chat pre- ceded the iwar. Sir E 'ward Grey, who to some ex- tent justifies what has been done, says of Mr Chamber- lain's diplomacy that it was no diplomacy at all, and in the minds of some of us that is an absolute condemna- tion of the whole thing. The diplomacy which is provocative, which is directed towards creating a bitter feeline and fomenting strife, instead of dealing with thee matters in a statesmanlike way, is not merely a mistake but a blunder, which, in politics, amounts to crime of the worst description. (IpPlanSe). Even after the war was begun our second complaint is that not once, but several times there have been mmortun.ties when a settlement might have been made, There was a time wl:en General Buller was face to face with the Boers; and when he proposed to make Some settlement, and you will remember the telegram sent to him by Lord Roberts ; I am sure not on big authority, because I decline to believe that Lord Roberts would make himself responsible for such a policy as that. There cannot be the slightest doubt that the Government were responsible for the telegram he sent in reply to Builer'3 question when he simply told him that " my terms are unconditional surrender." The next opportunity we had for coming to terms was after the capture of Pretoria. We all knot's', for it is an open secret, that there was every opportunty for making a settlemi nt after Pretoria was taken, and even that stern soldier, Lard Kitchener, of whom I speak with the greatest respect, was prevented taking the opportunity which he had in which it seemed very probable, not quite certain, perhaps that he might have ended the whole war at the end of last February. (Applause.) It is noidoubt in your recol- lection that Mr Chamberlain in tees House of Commons jeered at the settlement he proposed to make. I will only say that if some greet power had invaded this country by dint of overwhelming numbeis and enor- mous wealth, and had driven us into the mountains of Derbyshire, Scotland, Wales, and Westmorland, and given us the choice between unconditional surrender not terms„but grovelling at their feet, and seeing all the havoc played with this country, which has been played in South Africa, and the frightful rate of mortality esoine on there. it would have been a very pretty stiff choice. And I believe that Englishwomen would no more have asked Englishmen to come in and knuckle under than the Boers have done in South Africa. (Applause.) With all respect I submit that it is a shameful alternative to give these people. You have read the figures which have been published in the newspapers,tbat shocking increase of mortality in June, July, August and September. The mortality amongst the children reached 43 per cent, and I believe Sep- tember is one of the worst months of the year in South Africa. If anything like that rate of mortality was to be kept up, in three years there would not be one child left alive in South Africa. They say rnee's sympathies are blunted. Let a man see two or three children fading away like flowers before his eyes, women sickening under horrid conditions, and if he were not a brute his chivalry is touched at once ; but when your hear of this thing going on by hundreds and thousands it seems as if people failed to grasp exactly what it meant. To my mind it is a terrible business in every way. It is not only a prolonged agony for South Africa and a terrible thing for England, but it is a most retrograde step and the worst el ow to civilization and the interests of civilization for the world at large there has been for some time. We have been the protectors of the small nationalities, and should not go back to the old barbaroui principle that " might is right." The subterfuge in the press is perfectly nauseoue. Another serious matter is the amount of money the war is cost ing, and when it is all over we shall have to face the matter ; and I may say in that connection that the financial reforms which we of the Liberal patty have</p>
        <p>advocated—and it is largely part of our raison d' O're to defend those reforms—will not be the less pressnag when the contest is over. The taxation of ground rents as one means of raising the increased taxation that wiil be wanted if we have to pay our way, will become a more and more urgent question. Tnere are other reforms which I am not going to talk about to-night. It is no use. The Smth African matter puts everything elan out of court, but when you bear people say that Liberalism is played out and has done its work, remem- ber that the other reforms, the great temperance ques- tion, the education question, and the old age pensions question which has been trifled with, are not mere dry academic things but have a very direct bearing on the welfare of all the population, and particularly the population of a great working eonstituercy like eve have in Warrington. (Applause). The question of economy, an old Liberal doctrine, which has been rather shelved of late, wants a littl attection in the House of Commons. They voted away on Augost 9:h £67,000,000, and there was no discussion at all ; practi- cally none. That points to this—that in prccess of time, the mere logic of events will force you to devolve a good deal of work dose in Westminster and other ac- ' semblies. It is necessary, if the busineFs of the empire is to go on all right that there should be that devolu- tion in future. I don't say how it will come. I don't say Mr. GitstiStlOLlE'S EIOITI9 Rule Bill as it stood, hut dev,alution will come. I don't pretend that the differ- ences of opinion there are in our party created by this war, have been settled. I don't pretend that we have gct the flowing tide absolutely with us, but what is prrfectly plain is that the tide that carried our oppo- nents to eucclss at the last election by a great deal of juggling and hankey-oankey work is ebbing very fast, and it won't be long before it turns. (Applause.) Councillor TINNION said it had bon a great pleasure to him to liters to the Chairmen's remarks. Although he could net beck everything he had said he had hit a very large proportion of the truth in regard t the South African question. Unt I the historian took the question in baud thee would never have the truth, but when it did come those who had advoceted a mere mereifid policy than that which had beers adopted wo aid have the credit of doing their country as greet a ter7iCO 33 those who had gone the other way. (Anolause.) On the motion of Councillor PEACOCK, seconded by Mr. JOHN RIGBY, a vote of thanks wes accorded to the Chairman, and the meeting terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-01_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>FROM DEATH TO LIFE.</head>
        <p>FROM DEATH TO LIFE.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Leech, a S:alybridge man livirv. at 3, Gerrard Street, gives a striking account of his aid ction. It was in March last he was taken very bad. "The first thing I ntsticed.” he sacs, " was a numb feeling coming over me. This gradually got worse, until I lost the use of my feet, and I had to take to my bed. Then I lost the use of my hands, and could not open or close them. I was utterly helpless, I could not sleep ; my brain seomod as if it would burst my head open ; my tongue was affec;ed, and I could not talk pronerlv ; my appetite failed me, and I was gradually wa.ting away!" Such was the agony Mr. Lech endured ; a living death caused by nervous paralysis. Five doctors agreed that he would never get bettor, as poison had entered his blood, causing paralysis of the nerves and muscles. Mr. Lc'enh did get better; he qot the right remedy— Dodo' s Kidney Pills. Dodd's Kidney Pills make the kidneys act, and take out the poisons that infest the body. Th-ye poisons were the cause of Mr. Leech's terrible condition. His kidneys had ceased to act pro. dery. He began to take Dodd's Kidney Pills ; the kidneys were put in order ; the blood was purified, and before long he had a "sensation " As if the blood was going with a rush through my win." Be quickly re- covered the use of arms and legs, and was soon out of bed.without the paralysis or headache,growing stronger</p>
        <p>retnedy was Dxlcl',. Kidney Pills, which changed him from a nPrvous wreck to a healthy man. Dodd's Kidney Pills are 24. 9 I. pr trx of all chemists, or poqt free on receipt of price from The Dodds Medicine Co., 23, Farrinadon Avenue, London, E.C. Remember the name, D c-d-d-'s.</p>
