        <p>MUNI CI PAL ELECTION. 1901,—T0 THE ELECTORS OF LATCHFORD WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN,— I have been requested by a large and Influential body of Warrington Ratepayers to enter the Town Coun cil. As you are no doubt aware, lam a large ratepayer and employer o• labour in the Ward,and many Burgesses of all shades of opinion and thought, have requested me to stand as their representative. The Conservative Corn. mittee of the Ward have also selected me as their candidate, and with the promise of so much support, I have decided to allow myself to be nominated. The stand I have taken in the matter of Corporation contracts is well-known to you. I have spent both a large amount of time and money in trying to get these matters put right, but the law is so weak on the side of the ratepayer that little can be done by anyone out side the Council for want of information which only a Councillor can obtain. Your present representative has not In any way what-:ver raised his voice against the vast expenditure of money or against the enormous amount of improper and, in some cases, illegal trading which is still going on. If elected I will loyally support J.C. Parr, Esq., for the Mayoralty. I will also support any measure for the consolidation of the Borough Debt, thereby reducing the interest paid on loans, and effecting a eonsiderable reduction in the rates. I will not support any compromise between the two political parties. I will move that the Manchester Resolution preventing Councillors trading with the Corporation, whether as private individuals or connected with LIMITED Coca PANIES, be adopted in Warrington. It is a common expression, and I am sorry to say often a truthful one. that Councillors often forget all their promises directly the election is over. It has been conveyed to me that I shall do the same if returned. Those who have said that do not really know me. My persistent action during the past fourteen mouths ought to be a guarantee of my future conduct. If you return mess your representative I am confident that I shall be ab'e, with the promised support of many large aria in- fluential ratepayers in the town, to carry out the reforms wliich the Warrington ratepayers have hitherto so hope lenity longed for. Trusting to receive the honour of your votes on Fri day, November Ist. I remain. Ladies and Gentlemen, Your obedient servant. JOHN PEMBERTON,</p>
