        <p>Public-House Trust in North Lancashire. Viscount Cross presided on Saturday at Preston over an important conference of county and borough justice, chief constables and others who had been called together to discuss the Public- house Trust movement, which aims at promoting temperance by eliminating the element of private profit from the retail sale of intoxicating liquors, and by placing the control of the trade in the bands of limited liability companies to be adminis- tered by them as a trust on behalf of the com- munity. Earl grey explained the object of the trust, and a resolution was passed "that it was de- sirable to form a public-house trust company for Northern Lancashire." Aro COMMERCIAL FAllllRES.—According to "Kemp's Mer- cantile Gaztte," the number of New Bankruptcy Prc- ceedings in England and Weirs gazetted outing the week ending October 261], was 62. The number in the corresponding week of last year was 89. showing a de- crease of 27, being a net decreaso in 1901, to date, of 176.</p>
